ystem lifetime.\n2. The \"flags missing metadata in real time\" feature directly addresses audit readiness - if a regulator arrives, you need to produce current docs immediately.\n3. Linking code changes and dataset updates to documentation creates an auditable trail - this supports traceability under Article 12(2).\n4. \"Prevents the catastrophic risk of being unable to produce up-to-date technical dossiers during a sudden regulatory audit\" - this is exactly the kind of institutional survival risk I care about. A regulator requesting documentation and finding it stale or incomplete could trigger enforcement action, fines (up to €35M or 7% global turnover for prohibited practices, but even for non-compliance with record-keeping requirements under Article 12, penalties can be significant).\n5. Integration with existing version control tools means lower implementation friction - we're not building a parallel system from scratch.\n\nConcerns:\n1. The proposal is somewhat high-level - \"automatically links\" and \"flags missing metadata\" are aspirational descriptions. The actual mapping between code changes and the specific documentation elements required by Recital 71 (algorithms, data, training, testing, validation processes, risk-management system) would be extremely complex.\n2. It doesn't explicitly address Article 12(1)'s requirement for automatic event logging (the log mechanism itself), though it could be seen as complementary infrastructure that makes the logs more useful/traceable.\n3. \"In real time\" is ambitious - depending on the complexity of the documentation dependencies, real-time flagging might not be feasible or might generate too many false positives.\n\nHowever, from a pure compliance/institutional survival standpoint:\n- The risk of failing an audit due to stale documentation is a very real and severe threat. Fines under the AI Act are significant. Reputational damage from being publicly non-compliant with record-keeping would be devastating for