compliance.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Wrapping existing inference APIs is the most natural and low-cost integration point. The lab already has these APIs; this just adds a layer on top.\n- \"Immutable centralized store\" — this could leverage existing data infrastructure (object storage, time-series databases) rather than requiring new hardware.\n- One centralized system means one place to maintain, audit, and query logs, rather than N scattered logging solutions per project.\n\n**Potential concerns from my vantage:**\n- Is it truly \"unified\"? If the lab has diverse model types (NLP, vision, multi-modal), a single wrapper needs to be flexible enough without becoming bloated. But the scope described is reasonable: timestamps, input metadata, system states — these are generic enough.\n- \"Mandatory\" enforcement could create friction if not implemented well, but from a resource standpoint, mandating one solution is better than allowing N ad-hoc solutions that waste engineering time.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly address Article 12(3) requirements (for biometric/identity verification systems under Annex III point 1(a)), which have more specific logging needs (reference databases, input data for matches, natural person identification). However, the wrapper as described captures \"input metadata\" and \"system states,\" which could be extended. This is a minor gap but not a dealbreaker — it's a framework that can be extended.\n- Cost: building one library + maintaining an immutable store is relatively modest compared to the alternative of each team doing its own logging or hiring compliance engineers per project.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a well-scoped, high-leverage infrastructure investment. It directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) with minimal disruption to research workflows. From my resource-allocation perspective, this is one of the most efficient ways to satisfy the regulation: one build, many benef