d researchers\" step is critical but also a bottleneck — it's not really a dev problem though, more of an organizational workflow issue.\n\n**Quality concerns:**\n- LLM-generated documentation can hallucinate or misrepresent what the system actually does. For regulatory compliance documents that must be accurate and traceable, this is a real risk. The human review step mitigates this but adds latency.\n- \"Clear and comprehensive form\" (Recital 71) — LLMs can produce clear prose, but comprehensiveness depends entirely on the input data quality. If training data provenance isn't in the repo, the doc won't cover it.\n- The tool doesn't address Article 12's automatic logging requirements at all — it's purely about documentation synthesis. But that's fine; it addresses one part of the regulatory response.\n\n**From my dev perspective:**\n- Technically viable: Yes, high confidence. The core NLP/code-parsing is well within current capabilities.\n- Prototype speed: Fast. A useful MVP in 2-4 weeks with a small team.\n- Quality ceiling: Medium-high for what it covers (code-level documentation), but won't produce fully compliant technical files without significant additional data sources.\n- The \"reduces administrative burden\" framing is attractive to researchers and would likely get adoption, which matters for sustained use.\n- Risk: If the generated docs are used as THE regulatory document and contain inaccuracies that slip through review, that's a compliance liability. But with mandatory human sign-off, this is manageable.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a technically sound, quickly prototypable tool that addresses a real pain point (documentation burden). It's not a complete solution to Article 12/Recital 71 requirements — it covers the \"technical documentation\" aspect but not the \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" requirement. But as a component of a broader compliance strategy, it's viable and valuable. The main technical risk is input data coverage (not every