ment:**\n\nPositives:\n- Directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1): automatic recording of events over the lifetime of the system\n- Standardization across projects ensures consistent compliance — critical for an R&D institution with multiple AI systems\n- Removes reliance on individual researchers to implement logging correctly (reduces human error and inconsistency)\n- The \"middleware layer\" approach means it can be applied regardless of which ML framework is used — practical for a diverse research environment\n- Decorator pattern in Python is low-friction for adoption\n- Prevents \"tool fragmentation\" which is a real compliance risk — if each project logs differently, audit becomes a nightmare\n\nConcerns:\n- The description mentions \"timestamps, input hashes, and user IDs\" but doesn't explicitly address the full scope of Article 12(2) events (risk identification, post-market monitoring triggers, operational monitoring)\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems, it's unclear whether it captures reference database identifiers or match data specifically\n- \"Input hashes\" rather than full input data could be a compliance gap for biometric systems where the actual input data for matches must be recorded (Art 12(3)(c))\n- The proposal is an internal tool — it needs to be maintainable, versioned, and auditable itself\n- No mention of log retention policies, tamper-proofing, or access controls — these are implicit compliance requirements\n- No mention of how this interacts with the broader technical documentation requirements (Recital 71)\n\nFrom a compliance perspective:\n- This is a HIGH-value proposal. Without automatic logging infrastructure, the institution simply cannot comply with Article 12(1). It's not optional. The SDK approach is the right architectural choice for ensuring consistency across multiple projects.\n- The fact that it's standardized means during regulatory audits (by notified bodies or market surveillance authorities), the institut