ing a \"specialized documentation engine\" might take significant time before producing value. A simpler approach (Jinja2 templates + CI step) could get us 80% of the way in days rather than months.\n- The proposal mentions reducing \"manual administrative burden on scientists\" - this is good but from my vantage point, it's a secondary benefit. The primary benefit for me would be that structured documentation generation enforces consistency in how we track and report experiments, which improves reproducibility.\n- One concern: if the pipeline depends heavily on experiment tracking software being perfectly maintained, any gaps in data entry upstream will produce incomplete compliance documents. This is a dependency risk but manageable.\n- The integration with CI/CD means documentation gets generated on every run - this actually aligns well with prototyping speed because you get documentation \"for free\" as part of your normal workflow rather than as a separate manual step at the end.\n\n**Potential concerns from my vantage point:**\n- \"Specialized documentation engine\" is vague and could balloon in scope. If it becomes a full document management system, that's a project sinkhole.\n- The proposal doesn't mention how it handles the specific Article 12(3) requirements for Annex III systems (biometric verification logs with start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, verifier identification). These are more structured operational logs than training documentation. The proposal seems focused on model development documentation rather than runtime event logging.\n- Actually, re-reading: the proposal focuses on \"training logs, hyperparameters, and dataset manifests\" which is more about the technical documentation (Article 11) side than the automatic event recording (Article 12(1)) side. But Article 12(3) specifically requires runtime logging capabilities for Annex III systems. The proposal doesn't clearly address this gap.\n- Wait - but my job is to assess