 team\" building this is a resource commitment. If the core team is already stretched across multiple projects, adding this could delay other work. However, the ROI (avoiding N redundant implementations) likely justifies it.\n- A \"unified\" library risks becoming a bottleneck or becoming overly complex if it tries to serve all possible high-risk use cases. The Article 12 requirements vary by category (Annex III point 1(a) has specific minimums), so the library needs flexibility without becoming bloated.\n- There's a risk that researchers resist integrating another dependency into their pipelines, creating friction. But this is a management/adoption issue, not a fundamental flaw in the proposal.\n- The proposal says \"lightweight API\" which is good—it signals scope discipline rather than building an enterprise logging platform.\n\n**Assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point of resource-allocation efficiency and funding constraints, this is clearly a high-value proposal. It's the kind of infrastructure investment that pays for itself multiple times over. The alternative—letting each team build bespoke solutions—is exactly the kind of inefficient fragmentation I should prevent. It also aligns with effective use of existing R&D infrastructure since it integrates into current pipelines rather than requiring new ones.\n\nThe main risk is execution: if the core engineering team is already at capacity, this could slip. But the proposal's framing as \"lightweight\" and focused on a \"common interface\" suggests reasonable scope.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score should be high—this directly addresses my core concern about resource allocation efficiency and preventing redundant spending across teams. I'd put it around 82-85. It's not a 95+ because there are execution risks (core team bandwidth, adoption friction, scope creep in the \"unified\" aspect), but from my vantage point it's clearly one of the most efficient use of limited engineering resources available to me.\