 III point 1(a) systems requires specific logging: reference database, input data leading to match, identification of natural persons in verification (Art 14(5)). The proposal mentions input hashes but doesn't explicitly address the reference database or the identification of verifiers.\n- \"Immutable ledger\" — while good for integrity, could raise questions about data minimization and GDPR if personal data is logged (e.g., identifying natural persons). Need to ensure it aligns with data protection requirements.\n- The scope says \"all high-risk model prototypes\" — but Article 12 applies to high-risk AI systems specifically, not all prototypes. This could mean over-engineering or under-scoping depending on interpretation.\n- No mention of retention period management, which is important for compliance (logs must be kept throughout the lifetime per Recital 71).\n- \"Weights updates\" logging — this is relevant to substantial modification tracking but needs careful scope definition.\n\n**From institutional survival / market reputation perspective:**\n- Having a standardized internal framework is a strong signal to regulators and market partners that the institution takes compliance seriously.\n- Reducing reliance on manual processes reduces human error, which is a major source of compliance failures.\n- However, if the framework has gaps (e.g., doesn't capture Art 12(3) specifics for biometric identification systems), it could create a false sense of security — and being caught with an incomplete \"compliance system\" would be worse reputationally than not having one at all.\n- The middleware approach is pragmatic and scalable, which supports institutional survival (not overcommitting resources).\n\n**Decision:** This is fundamentally aligned with what Article 12 requires. It addresses the core technical obligation (automatic logging), standardizes it institution-wide (reducing compliance risk), and integrates into existing infrastructure (practical deployment). The 