        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1399\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This directly targets the single largest recurring drain of researcher-hours under Article 12 compliance: manually assembling lifetime record-keeping reports from event logs is exactly the kind of low-value administrative labour our limited R&D budget cannot afford to keep absorbing, and the pipeline builds on infrastructure we must already maintain (the automatic logging mandated by Art. 12(1)), making it a high-leverage, one-build-many-payoffs investment that protects scientific capacity without demanding new capital expenditure beyond internal tooling. My main reservation is that 'real-time' parsing appears over-scoped relative to what the regulation actually demands for documentation cadence, and the proposal frames the output as log-derived reports whereas Recital 71's technical-documentation scope (algorithms, training/validation provenance, risk-management records) extends well beyond runtime events—so the pipeline must be scoped carefully to avoid either under-delivering on the full Article 12/Recital 71 documentation obligation or over-investing in a real-time engine where periodic batch generation would satisfy audit timelines at a fraction of the engineering cost.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 83\n    }\n  ]\n}",
