 not a new platform. This is efficient use of existing assets.\n\n**Concerns/risks from my vantage point:**\n- \"Real time\" parsing adds engineering complexity. Do we actually need real-time, or would batch processing at reasonable intervals suffice for audit purposes? The regulation says logs must be recorded automatically over the lifetime — it doesn't mandate that documentation reports be generated in real time. This could scope-creep the project and increase implementation cost unnecessarily.\n- \"Human-readable reports\" is a somewhat vague requirement. The regulatory need is for structured technical documentation (Recital 71: general characteristics, capabilities, limitations, algorithms, data, training/testing/validation processes, risk-management system). A pipeline that just converts logs to prose might miss the broader documentation requirements. The proposal focuses narrowly on log-to-report transformation, but Article 12 and Recital 71 require more than just runtime event logging — they require comprehensive technical documentation kept up to date throughout the system lifetime.\n- However, as a starting point and for the specific subset of documentation that IS derived from runtime logs (performance data, risk events, usage periods per Art 12(3)(a)), this is very much in scope and valuable.\n\n**Overall assessment:** This is a well-motivated, resource-efficient proposal that directly addresses a real operational burden. It leverages existing infrastructure (the logging system required by Article 12(1)) to produce outputs the lab will need anyway. The \"real time\" aspect might be over-scoped but doesn't invalidate the core idea. From my vantage point of protecting research resources and ensuring efficient use of funding, this is a clear win. It's not glamorous science, but it frees up researcher-hours that would otherwise be lost to compliance paperwork — hours that are very expensive given our budget constraints.\n\nI'd rate this highly. The core concept is sound and directly serves my domain concerns. Minor scope questions exist (real-time vs batch, full technical documentation vs just log-derived reports) but don't undermine the fundamental value proposition. I'll approve with a high promise score, maybe 82-85, because while it's clearly good, there are implementation scoping questions that could affect cost-efficiency if not nailed down early (the \"real time\" requirement in particular could inflate build costs without proportional regulatory benefit).\n\nLet me settle on 83.\n",
