lding a new dataset of simulated failure modes, training anomaly detection models, pushing \"the knowledge frontier of AI observability\" — this is a substantial R&D project with uncertain outcomes. It's ambitious but expensive and time-consuming. The actual compliance requirement (logging events) can be met with much simpler engineering work. The proposal conflates the regulatory minimum (you must log certain things in a way that enables traceability) with a research program to build AI-based monitoring on top of those logs.\n\nEffective use of existing R&D infrastructure: If we already have logging infrastructure or are building it for compliance, layering an AI detection system on top is additional scope. The proposal doesn't mention leveraging any existing infrastructure — it's proposing new dataset creation, new model training, new research directions. This is a greenfield research project disguised as a compliance response.\n\nThe key issue: Article 12 is about the *capability* to log events in a structured way that enables traceability and monitoring by humans/authorities. It's not mandating automated AI-driven risk detection on top of those logs. The proposal significantly over-scopes what's needed, turning a compliance engineering task into an open-ended research program. From a resource-efficiency standpoint, this is misaligned: you'd be committing scarce implementation resources to a frontier research project when the actual regulatory requirement could be met with more targeted, lower-risk engineering work.\n\nThat said, there IS some value in thinking about how to make logs actionable — but \"training lightweight anomaly detection models\" and \"creating a new dataset of simulated failure modes\" is a multi-year research commitment that goes far beyond what's needed for compliance. The \"directly satisfies Article 12(2)(a)\" claim is somewhat misleading — Article 12(2)(a) is about what the logging capability should enable (identifying risk situations), 