al documentation) but Article 12 is specifically about LOGGING capabilities (automatic recording of events). The proposal conflates documentation generation with event logging. While related, they're different requirements.\n   - The scope seems manageable — it's a parser and template system, not a full platform rebuild.\n   - \"Every model checkpoint\" could generate enormous volumes of documentation if we have many experimental models that never become high-risk systems. There's no filtering mechanism mentioned for only applying this to high-risk AI systems.\n\n4. **Practical concerns from my vantage point**:\n   - The proposal is well-scoped and uses existing tools — good.\n   - It automates a repetitive task, saving ongoing manual effort — good for resource efficiency.\n   - However, it's somewhat narrow: it addresses technical documentation (Recital 71) but not the actual Article 12 logging requirements (automatic recording of events over lifetime). The \"logging\" here is more about generating reports from existing data, not about building event-recording capabilities into the systems themselves.\n   - Still, for a lab that already uses MLflow/W&B, this is a low-cost, high-leverage intervention. The engineering effort to build parsers and templates is modest compared to alternatives.\n   - It doesn't require new infrastructure purchases or major hiring.\n\n5. **Risk of over-committing**: The proposal is bounded — it's a pipeline that generates documents from existing metadata. It doesn't promise to solve all compliance issues. It's a specific, implementable tool. This makes it easier to approve without committing enormous resources.\n\nMy assessment: From the Lab Director's perspective focused on resource efficiency and use of existing infrastructure, this is a solid proposal. It leverages tools we already have, automates a recurring task, doesn't require new major investments, and protects research throughput. The scope is manageable. I would approve it. The 