        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Lab Director concerned with resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal P2902 is about developing an \"Automated AI Forensic Traceability Framework\" — a research project to develop a standardized, high-fidelity logging system that captures internal model states during inference (neuron activations, attention weights) for critical decisions. It frames this as elevating compliance into scientific inquiry into interpretability and failure analysis.\n\nLet me assess from my vantage points:\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency:**\n- This is described as a \"research project\" — not an implementation of what Article 12 actually requires. Article 12(1) says high-risk AI systems shall \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs)\" over their lifetime. The specific requirements in 12(3)(a)-(d) are: start/end times, reference database, input data matched, and identification of verification personnel. These are metadata/event logs, not neuron-level activation traces.\n- Building a \"standardized, high-fidelity logging system that captures internal model states\" including \"neuron activations and attention weights for critical decisions\" is massively over-engineered relative to what the regulation actually demands. This would consume enormous R&D resources (new tracing tools within deep learning libraries) for something far beyond compliance scope.\n- The proposal reframes a compliance obligation as a \"scientific inquiry into model interpretability and failure analysis.\" This is a classic scope creep — using regulatory pressure to justify a research agenda that the lab may or may not want to pursue independently.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- Logging neuron activations for every critical decision would have significant computational overhead. For production systems, this could be prohibitive in terms of storage and processing costs.\n- The \"dynamic tracing tools within our deep learning libraries\" implies modifying core infrastructure — that's a significant engineering commitment with ongoing maintenance burden.\n- The scientific ambition (interpretability research) is legitimate, but using the AI Act compliance mandate as the justification to fund it misallocates resources. If we want to do interpretability research, we should pursue it under its own merit and budget line, not smuggle it into a compliance response.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The proposal calls for NEW tools (\"implement dynamic tracing tools within our deep learning libraries\") rather than leveraging what may already exist in the lab's infrastructure.\n- A \"standardized\" framework implies creating new governance/standards overhead on top of whatever we already have for logging and monitoring.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this proposal conflates a narrow regulatory compliance requirement (event-level logging: timestamps, reference databases, input data, personnel IDs) with a much broader scientific research program (model interpretability via activation tracing). The resource commitment is disproportionate to the actual regulatory need. It risks pulling engineering resources away from implementing what Article 12 actually requires into building an interpretability research platform that may or may not be scientifically mature enough to produce useful results.\n\nHowever, I should note: there IS some overlap. If we're already going to build logging infrastructure for compliance, capturing some additional diagnostic information at the time of critical decisions could be relatively low marginal cost and might have some future scientific value. But the proposal as written is framed as a full \"research project\" with \"dynamic tracing tools within deep learning libraries\" — that's not a piggyback on existing work, that's a new infrastructure build.\n\nMy decision: reject. The resource commitment is disproportionate to the regulatory requirement, it conflates compliance with research agenda, and it calls for new infrastructure rather than leveraging what exists.\n\nPromise score: From my vantage point, maybe 25-30. There's a kernel of useful thinking (capture diagnostic info alongside compliance logs), but as framed — a full research project building tracing tools into libraries for activation-level logging — it's too resource-heavy and misaligned with the actual compliance need. I'll say 28.\n",
