n details rather than fundamental flaws.\n\nI would approve this. It aligns well with my mandate: efficient use of resources, leveraging existing infrastructure, protecting scientific productivity from administrative overhead.\n\nPromise score: I'd give this around 82-85. It's a solid efficiency play that leverages what we already have. Not a 90+ because there are integration complexities and regulatory uncertainty about whether automated documentation will be accepted without human review layers. But it clearly clears the bar for resource-allocation efficiency.\n",
