        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen a single proposal (P1100) from the vantage point of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nLet me analyze P1100: \"Semantic Mapping of Latent States to Logs\"\n\nThe proposal suggests using probing classifiers to translate high-dimensional internal AI states into human-comprehensible event logs for Article 12 compliance. The idea is to train small interpreters on model internals during inference to map neural activation patterns to discrete events required by the regulation.\n\nFrom a Development Engineer perspective focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping:\n\n**Technical Viability Concerns:**\n- Probing classifiers are an active research area but NOT production-ready technology. They're fragile, model-specific, and require careful validation for each new model architecture/version.\n- \"Training small interpreters on model internals during inference\" is a significant engineering undertaking. The interpretability field has not produced robust, general-purpose solutions that can reliably map latent states to discrete semantic events with the reliability needed for regulatory compliance.\n- Regulatory logs need to be reliable, auditable, and consistent. Probing classifiers have known issues: they can be sensitive to distribution shifts, may give spurious correlations rather than true causal relationships, and their accuracy degrades when the base model changes even slightly.\n- This is fundamentally a research project (\"investigates,\" \"advances scientific understanding\") rather than an engineering solution. The framing as \"research\" is telling - it acknowledges uncertainty about whether this will work reliably.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Concerns:**\n- This would be extremely slow to prototype and even slower to validate for production use. You'd need: (1) access to model internals, (2) labeled data mapping internal states to the specific Article 12 events, (3) training probing classifiers, (4) validation that they work reliably across conditions, (5) integration into the logging pipeline.\n- The \"during inference\" aspect adds latency concerns and engineering complexity.\n- For a high-risk AI system subject to EU AI Act compliance, you need deterministic, auditable logging. A probabilistic probe classifier that might miss events or generate false positives is a very risky foundation for regulatory compliance.\n- Simpler approaches (explicit event hooks in the pipeline, structured logging at decision points) would be far more reliable and faster to implement.\n\n**What Article 12 actually requires:**\n- Automatic recording of events (logs) over lifetime\n- Events relevant for: identifying risk situations, post-market monitoring, operation monitoring\n- For Annex III(1)(a): start/end times, reference database, input data matching, identification of natural persons verifying results\n\nThe practical engineering solution is relatively straightforward: instrument the system at known decision points and capture the required metadata. You don't need to decode latent states if you can structure your pipeline to log events at appropriate granularity. The proposal seems to over-engineer a problem that has simpler, more reliable solutions.\n\nHowever, there IS some value in this direction for systems where the \"events\" are genuinely internal (e.g., when does the model detect it's uncertain? When does it activate certain safety mechanisms?). But even then, engineering hooks at known architectural points would be preferable to probing classifiers.\n\nMy assessment: This is a research project with low technical maturity and high uncertainty for producing a reliable, production-grade logging solution in any reasonable timeframe. From my vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping), this doesn't clear the bar. The simpler engineering approaches are far more viable. The proposal itself frames as \"investigates\" and \"advances scientific understanding,\" which signals it's exploratory research rather than a path to a deployable solution.\n\nDecision: Reject.\nPromise score: I'd give this maybe an 18-22. There's genuine intellectual interest in the problem space, and if interpretability matures significantly over 5-10 years, techniques like these could contribute to richer logging. But for the near-to-medium term engineering challenge of \"make Article 12-compliant logs work,\" this is not a viable path. It's slow, uncertain, and fragile. I'll say 18.\n",
