        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P1911 is about an \"Auto-Documentation Pipeline for Technical Dossiers\" that would:\n- Extract hyperparameters, dataset versions, and validation metrics from experiment trackers (MLflow)\n- Generate EU AI Act compliant technical documentation automatically\n- Use a templating engine to produce real-time technical docs\n- Integrate with existing MLflow logs\n\nLet me assess this from my domain of concern: technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\n**Technical viability assessment:**\n\n1. **MLflow integration**: This is well-trodden ground. MLflow already tracks parameters, metrics, artifacts, and model versions. Extracting hyperparameters, dataset versions (via data version tracking), and validation metrics from MLflow is straightforward — it's essentially querying a structured database/API. The technical risk here is very low.\n\n2. **Templating engine**: Generating formatted documents from structured data using templates (Jinja2, similar) is extremely well-understood technology. Low technical risk.\n\n3. **\"Real-time\" documentation that \"stays up to date throughout the system lifetime\"**: This is where I need to think carefully. The EU AI Act requires docs to be kept \"up to date appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system.\" A pipeline that regenerates docs on every experiment run or model deployment is technically feasible — it's a CI/CD-style trigger. However, \"real-time\" might be overpromising; more realistically it would be event-driven regeneration (on new runs, new deployments, etc.). Still, technically viable.\n\n4. **Scope concern**: The proposal focuses on extracting from experiment trackers into \"the required EU AI Act format.\" But Article 12 is specifically about *logging* — automatic recording of events over the system's