rticle 12(1) - \"over the lifetime of the system\" requirement. A dedicated repository with automated lifecycle policies ensures logs are retained for the full lifetime.\n2. Separation of concerns: regulatory archives vs. operational data means the compliance records aren't at risk of being overwritten or lost when compute infrastructure is reconfigured.\n3. Automated lifecycle policies reduce human error in retention management - important because compliance requires consistent, reliable record-keeping.\n4. The \"versioned datasets\" aspect supports Article 12(2) traceability requirements and Recital 71's requirement for documentation on training, testing, validation processes.\n5. Cost-effectiveness is a plus for institutional survival - we don't want compliance to be so expensive it threatens viability.\n\nConcerns/limitations:\n1. The proposal focuses on storage infrastructure but doesn't address the *content* of what must be logged (Article 12(2)(a)-(c), Article 12(3) for biometric systems). It's a necessary but not sufficient element.\n2. \"Low-cost\" and \"cost-effective\" - I need to ensure this doesn't compromise data integrity, access speed for regulatory audits, or security. Regulators will need timely access to logs (Article 86).\n3. The proposal doesn't explicitly mention security/access controls, which is important for maintaining the integrity of compliance records.\n4. \"Automated lifecycle policies\" - good in principle, but I'd want to ensure these policies align with the actual regulatory retention requirements and can be evidenced to auditors.\n\nOverall: This is a solid, practical infrastructure proposal that directly supports compliance obligations under Article 12. It's not glamorous but it's foundational. Without proper long-term storage for logs, the institution literally cannot comply with \"over the lifetime of the system\" requirement. The cost-optimization angle also serves institutional survival by keeping compliance sustainable.\n\nI would approve this. It addresses a core compliance requirement (lifetime record retention) and does so in a practical, cost-aware manner that supports institutional sustainability. From my vantage point, having dedicated infrastructure for regulatory records is not just helpful - it's essentially mandatory for demonstrating ongoing compliance capability to regulators.\n\nPromise score: I'd rate this around 78-82. It's clearly important and directly addresses a regulatory requirement, but it's somewhat incomplete as stated (doesn't address content requirements, security, access for auditors). It's a strong infrastructure foundation piece. I'll go with 80.\n",
