rage resources on systems that actually require them\" is correct as resource management, but the mechanism (quarterly board) could impede research velocity.\n- From a knowledge frontier perspective, this doesn't contribute to advancing AI science. It's a compliance gatekeeping process.\n\nHowever, I should be fair: every R&D institution needs some way to determine which projects fall under regulatory scope. The question is whether THIS specific mechanism (quarterly review board as a gate before resource allocation) serves the research mission well or poorly.\n\nThe key concern from my vantage: This proposal positions the triage as a GATE on resource allocation, which creates an incentive for researchers to frame projects in ways that avoid \"high-risk\" classification, potentially narrowing the scope of what gets explored. It also doesn't account for how rapidly AI research evolves — a project that starts as a low-risk NLP experiment might become a high-risk biometric identification system within two months. A quarterly cadence is misaligned with research velocity.\n\nThat said, having SOME process to determine regulatory status is necessary and reasonable. The proposal isn't unreasonable in principle, but the specific mechanism (quarterly board as pre-allocation gate) has real downsides for research agility.\n\nFrom my vantage point strictly on knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, and broader scientific impact:\n- This doesn't advance any of those directly.\n- It could indirectly harm them by creating bureaucratic friction and incentivizing scope-narrowing.\n- But it also protects resources that could otherwise be better deployed to actual research.\n\nI think this is a borderline case from my perspective. The principle (determine regulatory status) is sound, but the implementation (quarterly gate before resource allocation) raises concerns for research agility. I lean toward rejection because:\n1. It doesn't advance knowledge or scientific impact.\n2. The quar