ntain.\n- It protects against the scenario where a grant or contract requires high-risk AI deployment and you'd need to scramble to add logging retroactively.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The proposal explicitly builds \"around our model serving infrastructure\" — this is a wrap, not a rebuild. That's efficient. It leverages what already exists rather than creating parallel systems.\n- The sidecar pattern means it can be deployed alongside existing services without major architectural changes.\n- However, there's a risk: if the lab has heterogeneous infrastructure (different model serving stacks, different languages/frameworks), the \"standardized API\" might not actually standardize things cleanly on first pass. But that's an implementation detail, not a fundamental flaw in the concept.\n\n**Concerns/limitations:**\n- Article 12(3) for biometric systems (Annex III point 1(a)) requires very specific logging: period of use, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons verifying results. A generic inference-layer wrapper might not capture all of that — particularly the \"reference database against which input data has been checked\" and the identification of verifiers under Article 14(5). These are domain-specific and may require project-level configuration beyond what a generic middleware can handle.\n- The proposal says it intercepts at the \"inference layer\" — but some high-risk AI systems have complex multi-step pipelines where the relevant events aren't just input/output at inference time. There might be gaps.\n- \"Immutable database\" is good for compliance (audit trail) but adds operational overhead and storage costs over time. Need to think about retention policies aligned with system lifetime requirements.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a strong, well-motivated infrastructure investment that directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) efficiently. It's the kind of \"build once, use