c identification system (Annex III point 1(a)) has very specific minimum logging requirements (paragraph 3), while other high-risk systems may have different needs. A fixed percentage doesn't differentiate between these tiers.\n- The proposal is about budget allocation and project management structure rather than the actual technical content of what must be logged. It's an enabler, not a solution in itself. It doesn't specify WHAT will be recorded or HOW.\n- \"Compute resources\" for compliance-related engineering — this is somewhat vague. Article 12's logging requirements are more about software architecture (log generation, storage, retention) than compute per se. Though if the system generates large volumes of logs, compute for processing/storage could matter.\n- The proposal says \"compliance-related engineering\" broadly but Article 12 is specifically about record-keeping/logging. If this budget line also covers other compliance obligations, it might dilute focus on Article 12 specifically.\n\n**From institutional survival perspective:**\n- Having a dedicated budget for compliance reduces the risk of non-compliance due to resource starvation. This is good for survival.\n- However, a \"fixed percentage\" without review mechanisms could become rigid. If regulations change or the lab's portfolio shifts, the allocation may not track actual needs.\n\n**From regulatory compliance perspective:**\n- This proposal supports compliance by ensuring resources exist, but it doesn't itself implement any requirement of Article 12. It's an organizational/financial enabler.\n- The integration of \"regulatory milestones into every project lifecycle\" is good practice and aligns with the spirit of keeping documentation up to date throughout the system's lifetime (Recital 71).\n\n**From market reputation perspective:**\n- Demonstrating that compliance is budgeted for as a first-class concern signals seriousness to regulators, partners, and customers.\n- However, if the fixed percentage is perceived as too low or too high relative to actual need, it could signal either under-investment or inefficiency.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a reasonable organizational enabler that supports compliance with Article 12 but is not itself a compliance solution. It addresses the resourcing gap that often leads to non-compliance. From my vantage point, I'd lean toward approving it as a necessary structural support, though it's somewhat generic and could be more targeted to the specific technical logging requirements of Article 12.\n\nI'll approve it with a moderate-to-good promise score. It's useful infrastructure but not a direct compliance implementation. Maybe around 62-70 range. Let me settle on 65 — it's a solid organizational enabler that supports my function, but it's somewhat generic and doesn't directly address the technical substance of Article 12's logging requirements.\n",
