isk AI models\" - this is a significant engineering investment upfront. The lab needs to fund an engineering team to build and maintain this layer. Is that a better use of scarce resources than deploying those engineers on actual scientific projects?\n2. However, the alternative is worse: every project team building their own ad-hoc logging would be far more expensive in aggregate and harder to audit for compliance. The unified approach actually saves resources long-term.\n3. \"Across all deployment environments\" - this could expand scope creep. But it's also necessary for compliance (the system must log over its lifetime, which implies multiple environments).\n4. The proposal doesn't address Article 12(3) specifics (for Annex III point 1(a) systems like biometrics/ID verification), but that's a narrower subset and could be handled as an extension of the modular API.\n5. \"The engineering team will develop\" - this assumes internal development. Could there be off-the-shelf solutions? But given the specific nature of AI system logging (capturing model state changes, training data provenance, etc.), custom development may indeed be more efficient than adapting generic tools repeatedly.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- A unified layer means scientists can focus on their research without being distracted by compliance plumbing. This protects the \"scientific ambition\" side of the equation.\n- The upfront cost is real but bounded (one team, one build). Without it, the compliance burden is distributed and unbounded (every project reinvents the wheel).\n- From a funding perspective, this is a capital investment with recurring savings - favorable for grant reporting and institutional sustainability.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- A modular API can be layered on top of existing model deployment pipelines without requiring replacement.\n- It standardizes interfaces, which actually makes the lab's infrastructure more coherent over time.\n- Risk: if the API is too rigid, it could constrain how teams deploy their models. But \"modular\" suggests flexibility.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong proposal from my vantage point. The unified approach is precisely what resource-allocation efficiency demands - build once, deploy everywhere. It protects existing infrastructure (additive layer), reduces per-project compliance costs, and lets scientists focus on research. The main risk is upfront engineering cost and potential scope creep in \"all deployment environments,\" but the modular design mitigates that.\n\nI would approve this. Promise score: I'd give it around 82-85. It's not a 90+ because there's some ambiguity about how much existing infrastructure needs to be touched, the \"engineering team\" cost is non-trivial and not itemized, and the scope (\"all high-risk AI models,\" \"all deployment environments\") could balloon without careful project scoping. But it clearly clears my bar as a resource-efficient way to meet a hard regulatory requirement across the lab's portfolio.\n\nLet me settle on 83.\n",
