ective:** Having robust, automated documentation demonstrates maturity and commitment to compliance to partners, customers, and regulators.\n\n**My assessment:** This is a solid proposal that addresses a real regulatory need (Recital 71's technical documentation requirements, which are explicitly referenced in the Article 12 context). It doesn't fully cover Article 12's logging-specific requirements, but it complements them well. As a compliance officer, I would want this as part of my broader compliance architecture. The automation aspect is important for scalability and reducing error. The \"regulator-ready format\" language shows practical thinking about inspection readiness.\n\nHowever, I should note that this proposal seems to be addressing Article 11 (Technical Documentation) more than Article 12 (Record-keeping/Logging) per se. But Recital 71 explicitly frames both as part of the same compliance ecosystem for high-risk AI systems. The screening instruction says to judge on its own merits from my professional vantage point, and I am the Compliance Officer for the institution dealing with these obligations holistically.\n\nI'll approve this - it addresses a genuine regulatory obligation (the up-to-date technical documentation requirement), reduces institutional risk, is practically implementable, and supports compliance verification. The promise score should be high but not 100 because:\n- It's more Article 11 territory than strictly Article 12\n- \"Guarantees\" language oversells what any system can achieve\n- \"Regulator-ready format\" needs to be defined against actual implementing regulations that aren't all in place yet\n- Doesn't address the specific logging functions of Art 12(2)\n\nI'd put this at around 82-85. Strong compliance value, practical, addresses real obligation, but not a complete picture and has some overstatement in its framing.\n",
