        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P1308 proposes developing \"efficient, lossy compression algorithms specifically for high-risk AI event logs\" using \"sampling techniques and semantic compression.\"\n\nLet me assess this critically from a compliance perspective:\n\n**Critical issues:**\n\n1. **Lossy compression of regulatory logs is fundamentally incompatible with the Act's requirements.** Article 12(1) states systems \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs).\" The word \"all\" implies completeness. Recital 71 emphasizes \"comprehensible information on how high-risk AI systems have been developed and how they perform throughout their lifetime is essential to enable traceability.\" Lossy compression inherently means losing information - this directly undermines traceability, which is the stated purpose of Article 12.\n\n2. **Sampling techniques are antithetical to compliance.** The Act requires logging \"over the lifetime of the system\" - not sampled events. For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics), the minimum requirements in Article 12(3) specify recording of specific data points for each use. Sampling would mean some uses, some input data, or some reference database checks are not recorded. This is a direct violation.\n\n3. **\"Semantic compression\" is dangerous and ambiguous.** What does it mean to semantically compress a log? If you're interpreting what happened rather than recording what actually occurred, you've moved from record-keeping to interpretation/summarization. Regulators need the raw event data for post-market monitoring (Art. 72), traceability, and in the event of an incident investigation. A \"compressed\" or \"semantically interpreted\" log cannot substitute for