 control. The automated test suite checking \"mandated fields\" maps directly to the specific logging requirements in Article 12(2) and (3). It's a verifiable, auditable control that demonstrates to regulators (under Articles 42-43 conformity assessment, and post-market surveillance under Article 72) that the institution has implemented appropriate measures.\n\nFrom market reputation standpoint: If a competitor or partner discovers you deployed a high-risk system with broken logging, that's a serious credibility hit in regulated markets. Having this gate demonstrates organizational maturity and commitment to compliance.\n\nPotential concerns:\n- The proposal says it ensures compliance \"without slowing down the primary research and experimentation cycle.\" This is a nice framing but I need to consider: does this actually work? If it's truly automated and fast, yes. But if it becomes a bottleneck, it could create pressure to bypass it (a compliance risk in itself). However, the proposal as stated doesn't include any \"waiver\" mechanism, which is good.\n- The scope seems focused on logging specifically, not the broader technical documentation requirements of Article 11 or Recital 71. But that's fine - this is a targeted control for one specific article.\n- The \"simulates high-risk event triggers\" language suggests testing under realistic conditions, which is good from an auditability standpoint.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong, well-scoped compliance control directly mapped to Article 12. It's a clear yes from my vantage point. It's not overreaching (it doesn't try to solve all of Article 12, just the logging technical verification), it creates an auditable checkpoint, and it protects against the most direct regulatory exposure under this article.\n\nI would approve this with high confidence. The promise score should be high - maybe 85-90. It's a clear win for compliance but I'll note that it addresses only one dimension (logging functionality) of the broader Article 12/Recital 71 requirements, and its effectiveness depends on test coverage being comprehensive enough to actually catch gaps. Also, \"over the lifetime\" in Article 12(1) means logging must work not just at deployment but continuously - this gate only verifies initial functionality. But as a pre-deployment check, it's a necessary (not sufficient) control.\n\nI'll set promise at 87.\n",
