        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2108\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core mechanism—content-addressable versioning via DVC to pin each inference call to a specific reference-DB snapshot—is technically sound, leverages mature off-the-shelf tooling (good for fast prototyping), and maps cleanly onto Article 12(3)(b). However, the proposal over-scopes by tying 'every single log entry' to a data version when the regulation only requires recording which reference DB was active at the moment of a search/verification event, and it implicitly assumes we control the reference-DB lifecycle (in Annex III 1(a) deployments the database is often externally managed), both of which add prototyping friction without adding compliance value.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 72\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2108 from the vantage point of a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal: \"Automated Versioning System for Reference Databases\" - using content-addressable storage with cryptographic hashes via DVC to link log entries to precise data versions, satisfying Article 12(3)(b).\n\nLet me assess this from my professional vantage point (technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping):\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- DVC (Data Version Control) is a well-established tool for versioning large datasets. It uses content-addressable storage with cryptographic hashes, which is technically sound.\n- However, there's a critical nuance here: Article 12(3)(b) requires recording \"the reference database against which input data has been checked by the system.\" This applies specifically to Annex III point 1(a) systems - which are biometric identification/verification systems (facial recognition, etc.) that match against a reference database.\n- The proposal says it links \"every single log entry\" to a \"precise data version.\" But Article 12(3)(b) doesn't require linking EVERY log entry to a data version - it requires recording which reference database was used for each search/inference call. The scope in the proposal is broader than what's required, which adds unnecessary complexity.\n- Actually, re-reading: \"the reference database against which input data has been checked by the system\" - this means for each inference/search event, you need to record which specific version of the reference DB was used. DVC content hashes could work here, but there's a practical concern:\n  - If the reference database is massive (e.g., a national biometric database with millions of entries), hashing the entire thing per inference call would be computationally expensive and add latency to the critical path.\n  - A more pragmatic approach might be to record a database identifier + version tag rather than computing a full cryptographic hash at inference time. But DVC does provide a way to do this efficiently since the hash is computed once when the snapshot is created, not per-query.\n  - Actually, DVC's model works well: you create a commit/snapshot of the DB state, and then reference that commit hash in the log entry. The hash is pre-computed. So at inference time, you just record which snapshot ID was active. This is technically viable and doesn't add much latency.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- DVC is an existing, mature open-source tool. It's not something we need to build from scratch. This reduces prototyping time significantly.\n- However, integrating DVC into the inference pipeline requires:\n  - Ensuring the reference DB is stored in a way compatible with DVC tracking (git + DVC)\n  - Adding metadata recording at the point of inference that captures the active DVC commit hash\n  - Handling edge cases: what if the DB updates mid-inference? What about partial reads?\n- The proposal says \"every single log entry\" which is over-scoped. We only need to link inference/search events (where a reference DB lookup occurs) to the DB version, not every single log event in the system. This scoping issue could slow down prototyping if we try to instrument everything.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n1. Over-scoping: \"every single log entry\" is broader than what 12(3)(b) requires. The regulation says \"the reference database against which input data has been checked by the system\" - this implies per-search/per-inference recording, not every log event. This could lead to unnecessary engineering effort.\n2. Practical integration: DVC assumes a git-based workflow for versioning. In production high-risk systems with large biometric databases, the operational model might differ. The reference DB might be managed by an external authority (e.g., government agency), and we may not control its versioning infrastructure. We'd need to receive