oss-functional engineering team\" — is this sufficient? Compliance requires robustness, not just a thin wrapper. But the proposal says \"modular API,\" which suggests it's scalable.\n- The proposal focuses on efficiency and preventing redundant work, which are operational benefits but also compliance benefits (consistency).\n- Risk: if the unified layer has a bug or gap, ALL projects inherit that failure mode. However, from a compliance standpoint, having one auditable system is actually better than N different ones for regulatory inspection.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention retention periods, log immutability, or access controls — but these are implementation details that would be in the technical specification, not necessarily in the strategic proposal level.\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" (Art 12(1)) is addressed by making it an infrastructure layer rather than a per-project add-on.\n\n**Compliance assessment:**\nThis is strongly aligned with Article 12's requirements. A unified logging infrastructure:\n1. Ensures automatic recording (Art 12(1)) across all systems\n2. Standardizes what events are recorded, ensuring the \"level of traceability... appropriate to the intended purpose\" (Art 12(2))\n3. Makes it easier to ensure Annex III 1(a) minimum fields are captured (Art 12(3))\n4. Supports post-market monitoring (Art 72) and operation monitoring (Art 26(5))\n5. Demonstrates organizational-level compliance commitment to regulators — critical for market reputation\n6. Reduces the risk of non-compliance due to individual project oversight\n\nThis is a high-value proposal from my vantage point. It directly addresses a core mandatory requirement in a scalable, auditable way. The institutional survival angle: if we're caught with inconsistent or missing logs across projects, that's a serious regulatory finding. A unified layer mitigates this systematically.\n\nI would approve this. Promise score: This is very strong from a compliance perspective — it directly addresses Article 12 in the most scalable way possible. I'd rate it around 88-92. The slight deduction is because \"small cross-functional engineering team\" might be under-resourced for the full complexity of Article 12(3)'s specific requirements, and the proposal doesn't explicitly address log retention, integrity, or auditability — but these are implementation details I'd expect to be addressed in the technical design phase. The strategic direction is clearly correct.\n\nI'll go with 90.\n",
