        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2887\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The proposal targets a genuine obligation under Recital 71 ('technical documentation should be kept up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system') and CI/CD integration is a practical, low-friction way to reduce documentation drift—a common finding in regulatory audits. However, its stated scope (extracting hyperparameters and validation results from code) covers only a narrow slice of what Recital 71 enumerates (risk-management documentation, data provenance, training/testing process narratives, capabilities-and-limitations descriptions), and it does not address Article 12's distinct requirement for automatic operational event-logging during system use; calling the manual effort 'eliminated' overstates the residual human-judgment work a compliance file still demands. As a partial but real mitigation of documentation-staleness risk, it warrants resource commitment.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 67\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the perspective of a Compliance Officer whose domain concerns institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P2887 is about an \"Automated Technical Documentation Pipeline\" that:\n- Extracts model hyperparameters and validation results directly from code\n- Maintains a living technical dossier\n- Integrates documentation generators into CI/CD pipeline\n- Updates compliance records automatically upon every merge to main branch\n- Claims to eliminate manual effort for keeping technical documentation up to date as mandated by Recital 71\n\nLet me assess this from my vantage point:\n\n**Compliance perspective:**\n\nRecital 71 states that \"The technical documentation should be kept up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system.\" It requires documentation containing information necessary to assess compliance with relevant requirements and facilitate post-market monitoring. This includes \"general characteristics, capabilities and limitations of the system, algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used as well as documentation on the relevant risk-management system.\"\n\nArticle 12 focuses specifically on logging capabilities - automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system. It's about event logs, not technical documentation per se. The proposal conflates Article 12's logging requirement with Recital 71's broader technical documentation obligation. However, keeping documentation up to date IS a regulatory requirement under Recital 71 and related articles (Article 11 on technical documentation).\n\n**Strengths of the proposal:**\n- Directly addresses the \"kept up to date\" requirement from Recital 71\n- Automation reduces risk of documentation drift, which is a real compliance risk\n- CI/CD integration ensures documentation reflects actual deployed code\n- Reduces human error in manual documentation updates\n- Creates an audit trail (version-controlled docs tied to code commits)\n\n**Weaknesses/concerns from my vantage point:**\n- The proposal focuses on \"model hyperparameters and validation results\" which is a subset of what Recital 71 requires. Recital 71 also demands documentation on risk-management systems, data provenance, training processes, testing processes, system capabilities AND limitations. A pipeline that only extracts hyperparameters from code won't capture the full scope of required documentation (e.g., risk assessments, impact analyses, intended purpose descriptions).\n- \"Eliminates manual effort\" is an overstatement - compliance documentation requires human judgment for interpreting risks, documenting intended purposes, describing limitations in context. You can't auto-generate a risk-management system description from code.\n- The proposal addresses Recital 71 (technical documentation) rather than Article 12's specific logging requirements. These are related but distinct obligations. A compliance officer would note that this doesn't fulfill Article 12's requirement for automatic event recording during operation.\n- However, keeping technical documentation up to date IS a genuine regulatory obligation, and automating parts of it is smart.\n- From a market reputation standpoint, having an automated pipeline demonstrates organizational maturity and commitment to compliance.\n\n**Institutional survival angle:**\n- If documentation becomes stale, that's a direct non-compliance finding. Automation reduces this risk significantly.\n- However, if the pipeline only captures hyperparameters and validation results, we could still be found non-compliant for missing other required elements (risk management docs, data documentation, etc.)\n- The CI/CD integration is practical and implementable.\n\n**My decision:**\nThis proposal addresses a real compliance need (keeping technical documentation current) but its scope is too narrow to fully satisfy the regulatory requirement as stated in Recital 71. It's a good partial solution. From my vantage point, I'd want it approved because:\n1. It directly targets a documented regulatory obligation\n2. It reduces compliance risk by automating what would otherwise be manual and error-prone\n3. The CI/CD integration is practical and within our technical capabilities\n4. Even partial automation is better than none\n\nBut I have reservations about the \"eliminates manual effort\" claim - it doesn't eliminate ALL manual effort, just part of it. And it doesn't address Article 12's specific logging requirements (event logs during operation). It addresses a related but distinct obligation.\n\nI'll approve it because it addresses a genuine compliance gap with a practical solution, even though it's not the complete picture. The promise score should reflect that it's useful but incomplete - maybe around 65-70. It's clearly valuable from a compliance standpoint (addressing the \"up to date\" mandate), but it only covers part of the documentation landscape and doesn't touch Article 12's specific logging obligations.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. My bar is: does this proposal clear my threshold for committing scarce implementation resources? \n\n- It addresses a real obligation: YES\n- It's implementable with existing CI/CD infrastructure: likely YES\n- It reduces compliance risk: YES (reduced documentation drift)\n- Is it sufficient on its own to satisfy the full requirement: NO, but that's not required for approval\n- Does it create any new compliance risks: Not really - automated docs are generally better than stale manual ones\n\nI'll approve. Promise around 68 - it's genuinely useful and addresses a real obligation, but the scope is narrower than the full regulatory picture and doesn't touch Article 12's operational logging requirements specifically.\n",
