al documentation requirements (Article 11, Annex IV) are extensive: they require risk management system documentation, data governance details, accuracy metrics, human oversight measures, etc. A pipeline that just parses MLflow/Git metadata may not capture all required elements (e.g., risk-management system documentation, validation processes in the regulatory sense). The proposal somewhat oversimplifies what \"satisfies the requirement\" means.\n2. However — and this is important from my resource perspective — it doesn't claim to be a complete solution. It's an automation layer that handles the mechanical parts (code provenance, hyperparameters, dataset manifests) which are exactly the things already captured in MLflow/Git. The risk-management documentation would still need human input, but that's a smaller scope than doing everything manually.\n3. \"Triggers upon every major model version change\" — this is reasonable and not over-engineered. It doesn't create a constant logging burden.\n4. The proposal correctly identifies the value proposition: protecting intellectual productivity by minimizing administrative toil. This is exactly my domain concern.\n\n**Resource-allocation assessment:**\n- Build cost: Moderate. Parsing existing MLflow/Git metadata into reports is well-understood engineering. Not a research problem, more of an integration/reporting task. Should be doable with existing engineering resources without pulling researchers away from science.\n- Ongoing cost: Low once built. Automated triggers mean minimal human intervention.\n- Opportunity cost saved: Significant. Researchers no longer spend time manually updating compliance documents for every model iteration.\n- Risk of over-investment: Low. It's a focused tool, not a sprawling platform.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\nThis proposal is well-aligned. It doesn't consume significant new funding (it leverages existing tools), it protects researcher time (the scarcest