ot an open-ended R&D bet.\n- The \"stress test\" framing suggests periodic, discrete events rather than continuous integration into the development lifecycle — which is less efficient from a resource perspective.\n\nI think this is borderline. The goal is right (regulatory compliance on logging), but the implementation approach as described (separate quarterly audits by compliance officers) is not the most resource-efficient way to achieve it. It creates parallel processes rather than integrating into existing workflows. However, some form of periodic verification is necessary and reasonable.\n\nI'll lean toward a moderate promise score — maybe 45-55. The need is real but the implementation as framed is not optimally efficient from my vantage point. It's not terrible (it targets a specific requirement and has bounded cost), but it doesn't maximize use of existing infrastructure or minimize resource drag on R&D output.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. As Lab Director, I care about:\n1. Resource-allocation efficiency — quarterly compliance audits are a recurring cost that competes with research time. But they're bounded and predictable. Not ideal but manageable.\n2. Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints — this doesn't advance science; it's pure overhead. But it prevents costly regulatory failures. It's insurance, not investment in capability.\n3. Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure — the proposal as written creates a NEW process (compliance officers doing stress tests) rather than integrating logging verification into EXISTING deployment pipelines and monitoring systems. This is the weakest point from my perspective.\n\nI'll go with: reject, promise around 42. The rationale being that while the underlying compliance need is valid, the proposal as framed creates a parallel organizational process (dedicated compliance officers, quarterly cadence) rather than integrating logging sufficiency checks into existing engineering and deployment infrastructure, which would be more resource-efficient and less disruptive to R&D throughput.\n\nWait — but I should also consider: maybe in this lab's context, they don't have robust CI/CD or monitoring already, so a separate audit is actually the practical path. But as written, the proposal doesn't demonstrate that it leverages existing infrastructure; it creates new structure. From my vantage point, I'd want to see integration with existing systems before approving dedicated compliance personnel for quarterly reviews.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise 43.\n",
