ersioning captures the lifetime aspect mentioned in Recital 71.\n- \"Minimizes risk of human error\" — compliance is about consistency, and automated logging reduces the chance that a researcher forgets to log something.\n- Protects from regulatory fines — this is directly aligned with institutional survival.\n\nPotential gaps/concerns:\n- Does it address Article 12(3)(b) — \"reference database against which input data has been checked\"? The proposal mentions \"inputs\" but not specifically reference databases for biometric-type systems. However, the framework might be general enough to accommodate this per-project.\n- Does it address Article 12(3)(d) — identification of natural persons involved in verification? This is a human-in-the-loop element that pure software logging might not capture without workflow integration.\n- The proposal says \"across all high-risk AI projects\" which is good for institutional consistency, but the specific requirements under 12(3) apply only to Annex III 1(a) systems. The framework needs to be flexible enough to handle different categories.\n- \"Over the duration of the lifetime of the system\" — does the proposal address data retention and archival? It mentions logging but not how long logs are kept or how they're preserved after a model is decommissioned. Recital 71 emphasizes keeping documentation \"throughout the lifetime.\"\n- The proposal focuses on the technical implementation (middleware, telemetry) but doesn't explicitly mention how it supports post-market monitoring workflows (Article 72 connection in 12(2)(b)) or risk identification (12(2)(a)).\n\nFrom a Compliance Officer's perspective:\nThis is a fundamentally sound and necessary initiative. Article 12(1) is not optional — \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events.\" Without this, the institution cannot comply at all with high-risk AI system requirements. A unified framework across projects is exactly what an institutional compliance function would