        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2118\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core engineering pattern—intercepting model calls via a framework-agnostic middleware layer to auto-capture session metadata and input hashes—is technically proven (proxy/decorator patterns are standard) and directly implements Article 12(1)'s requirement for automatic event recording with minimal per-experiment burden. Embedding it in baseline prototyping templates makes compliance-by-default, which materially improves prototyping speed by removing a manual logging step from every researcher's workflow; the main technical risk is that 'universal' framework-agnosticism across async/sync, distributed, and custom-pipeline scenarios may under-deliver on Day 1, but even covering the top two or three internal frameworks delivers most of the value at low latency cost to the experiment loop.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 84\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to screen proposal P2118 from the vantage point of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution whose domain is \"technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\"\n\nLet me analyze this proposal:\n\n**Proposal P2118: Standardized Telemetry Middleware**\n- A universal logging wrapper integrated into all baseline AI prototyping templates\n- Framework-agnostic API layer that intercepts model calls to record session metadata and input hashes\n- Developers maintain current workflow while system handles background recording\n- Minimizes manual overhead for researchers\n- Ensures technical viability for high-risk deployment\n\nNow let me evaluate this from my professional vantage point (technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping):\n\n**Strengths:**\n1. It directly addresses Article 12(1) — \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs)\" by embedding logging into the prototyping infrastructure itself. This is architecturally sound.\n2. Framework-agnostic API layer is a smart technical choice — it means you don't have to rebuild the wrapper every time the team switches from PyTorch to JAX or uses a new serving framework. This directly supports speed of prototyping because teams won't be blocked by logging implementation.\n3. Intercepting model calls to record session metadata and input hashes is technically feasible with existing patterns (middleware, decorators, proxy layers). This isn't speculative tech — it's well-understood engineering.\n4. By baking it into \"baseline AI prototyping templates,\" you ensure compliance-by-default rather than compliance-by-remembering. This reduces the risk of a developer forgetting to add logging to an experiment that later becomes a high-risk deployment.\n5. It addresses Article 12(3)(a) (period of each use — start/end timestamps are natural session metadata) and partially 12(3)(b) and (c) through input data recording.\n\n**Weaknesses / Concerns:**\n1. \"Universal\" is a strong word. In practice, truly framework-agnostic interception at the model-call level can be fragile. Different frameworks have different call patterns, async/sync considerations, distributed training scenarios, etc. The engineering complexity of making this truly universal could be significant and might slow down prototyping if it introduces latency or failure modes.\n2. It captures \"session metadata and input hashes\" — but Article 12(3) requires more specific things like the reference database against which input data was checked, the actual input data that led to a match, identification of natural persons in verification. Input *hashes* alone may not satisfy the requirement for recording actual input data (though this could be a privacy-conscious design choice).\n3. The proposal is scoped to \"baseline AI prototyping templates\" — what about experiments that deviate from those templates? What about custom pipelines, A/B testing frameworks, or one-off scripts? If it's only in the baseline template, it might miss edge cases.\n4. No mention of log storage architecture, retention policies, or how logs are made available for audit purposes. But as a screening proposal (not a full implementation plan), this level of detail might not be expected.\n\n**From my vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping):**\n- The core technical idea is sound and implementable with current engineering practices. Middleware/proxy pattern for logging is well-established.\n- It directly supports prototyping speed by automating a compliance requirement that would otherwise be a manual burden on every researcher.\n- The \"integrated into baseline templates\" approach means it's a one-time infrastructure investment that pays off across all future experiments.\n- The framework-agnostic claim is the main technical risk, but even a partially agnostic solution (covering 80% of common frameworks) would be highly valuable.\n- The proposal doesn't over-promise — it says \"core event data\" not everything in Article 12(3). It's scoped appropriately for an initial implementation.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a technically viable, well-scoped infrastructure investment that directly serves the dual goals of compliance (Article 12) and prototyping efficiency. The engineering patterns are proven. The main risk is scope creep toward \"universal\" but even a limited version is high-value. I'd approve this with moderate-to-high promise.\n\nI'll rate it around 82-85. It's technically sound, well-aligned with my domain concern, and the implementation path is clear. The \"universal\" framing and the gap between input hashes vs. full data recording keep it from being a perfect score, but those are refinement issues, not viability blockers.\n",
