ere is moderate. Compressed logging and selective sampling are well-understood techniques. The novelty would be in their application to this specific compliance context. There's no indication of deep theoretical contributions, new algorithms with provable properties, or fundamental insights about AI systems. It reads more as an engineering optimization project dressed up in research language. The phrase \"transforms a mandatory compliance burden into a novel scientific contribution\" is doing a lot of rhetorical work — the intellectual merit seems overstated relative to what's actually being proposed.\n\n3. **Broader scientific impact**: Would this contribute meaningfully to the broader scientific community? Compressed logging for AI compliance is quite narrow. The techniques (compression, sampling) are general-purpose and already well-studied. Unless the project produces genuinely new compression algorithms or sampling theory applicable beyond this niche context, its broader impact would be limited. It's solving a practical problem but not advancing science in any broad sense.\n\n4. **The framing concern**: The proposal explicitly frames itself as turning \"a mandatory compliance burden into a novel scientific contribution.\" This is a bit of a red flag from my vantage point — it suggests the primary motivation is resource optimization (\"optimizing our limited infrastructure resources for future growth\") rather than genuine scientific curiosity or intellectual ambition. A research project driven primarily by cost reduction, even if framed in scientific language, doesn't necessarily clear the bar for \"intellectual merit\" and \"broader scientific impact.\"\n\n5. **Regulatory fit**: From a pure regulatory standpoint (Article 12), the law requires \"automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" The proposal mentions \"selective sampling techniques\" — this could actually conflict with the requirement for complete logging. Article 12(1) say