om my Compliance Officer vantage point:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Directly addresses Article 12(1)'s requirement for automatic recording of events over the lifetime\n- The immutable audit trail concept is excellent for compliance - it prevents tampering, which regulators would view favorably\n- Linking Git commits + dataset hashes to model weights creates a verifiable chain of custody - this is exactly what \"traceability\" means in Recital 71\n- Automating this reduces human error in record-keeping (a major compliance risk)\n- Preserving R&D velocity while maintaining compliance is critical for institutional survival\n- The \"immutable\" aspect addresses the requirement that records be kept up to date and reliable\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- It's a partial solution. Article 12(3)'s specific minimum requirements (for biometric systems) aren't addressed here - those are operational logging requirements, not development record-keeping\n- \"Auto-generates compliance reports by scraping metadata\" - this could be insufficient if the metadata isn't comprehensive enough. Regulators want documentation in \"clear and comprehensive form\" per Recital 71\n- The proposal focuses on the DEVELOPMENT phase (Git commits, dataset hashes, model weights) but Article 12 requires logging over the LIFETIME of the system, which includes deployment and operational use\n- \"Shifting burden from scientists to automated infrastructure\" - this is good operationally but there's a risk: if the automation fails or produces incomplete records, who is responsible? Compliance cannot be fully delegated to a tool without human oversight\n- The proposal doesn't mention retention periods, access controls for regulators (Article 14 of the Act gives authorities access), or format requirements\n\n**From my compliance perspective:**\nThis is a strong foundational piece. It addresses the core of Article 12(1) and much of the traceability requirement in Recital 71. The immutable audit trail concept is exactly