        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2569\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal is primarily an administrative documentation protocol (templates, monthly audits) rather than a technical solution to Article 12's core mandate of automatic event recording; from a dev-engineering standpoint it adds process friction to prototyping cycles without specifying the logging architecture, storage infrastructure, or how 'automatic recording' will be engineered into system design. The version-control integration idea has limited merit for code/config artifacts but conflates heterogeneous documentation types (training-data lineage, runtime logs, risk registers) in a way that would slow iteration without delivering the concrete engineering capability we actually need.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 32\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2569 from the vantage point of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is about creating an institutional protocol for a \"living Compliance Dossier\" that houses all technical documentation required by Recital 71, with mandatory templates, monthly internal audits, version control integration, documenting algorithms, training data, and risk-management decisions.\n\nFrom my perspective as a Development Engineer concerned with technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping:\n\n**Technical viability concerns:**\n- \"Living Compliance Dossier\" integrated into version control is technically feasible but raises significant engineering questions. Version control (git, etc.) is designed for code artifacts, not necessarily for living documents that span the entire lifetime of a system including runtime logs, training data lineage, and operational records.\n- \"Monthly internal audits\" — this is an administrative process, not really a technical one from my vantage point, but it does imply some engineering work to produce audit-ready outputs.\n- The proposal conflates several things: documentation (which Recital 71 requires) with the automatic logging capability that Article 12 actually mandates. Article 12(1) specifically says systems \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs).\" This proposal doesn't really address the technical architecture for automatic event recording — it's more about a documentation process.\n- The integration into version control is interesting but potentially problematic: compliance documents, training data references, and risk-management decisions are very different artifact types. Forcing them all into a VCS workflow could slow down prototyping significantly.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping concerns:**\n- \"Mandatory templates\" for every high-risk project would add overhead to the prototyping cycle. As a dev engineer, I care about how quickly we can go from idea → working prototype. Mandatory documentation processes that gate progress would slow this down.\n- \"Monthly internal audits\" create periodic disruption to development cycles. Engineers get pulled away from building to prepare for audits.\n- The proposal is largely a process/administrative protocol rather than a technical solution. It doesn't specify HOW the automatic logging (Art. 12(1)) will be technically implemented, what logging infrastructure we'll build, how logs are stored, retention policies, format standards, etc.\n- However, there IS value in having structured documentation integrated with development workflows. A well-designed compliance dossier that's part of the dev pipeline rather than bolted on could actually work. The version control integration is a reasonable technical choice for code and config artifacts.\n\n**What's missing from a dev engineering perspective:**\n- No mention of how automatic logging (the core requirement of Art 12(1)) will be technically implemented in the system architecture\n- No discussion of log storage infrastructure, retention, scalability\n- No mention of how logging capabilities will be designed into the system vs. bolted on\n- The \"reference database\" and \"input data matching\" requirements (Art 12(3)) for biometric systems aren't addressed technically\n- No technical architecture for event recording\n\n**What's reasonable:**\n- Having a structured documentation process is good practice\n- Version control integration for code/config artifacts makes sense\n- The concept of keeping docs up to date throughout the lifetime aligns with Recital 71\n\nOverall assessment: This proposal is primarily an administrative/process protocol that touches on technical aspects only superficially. From my vantage point, it doesn't address the core technical challenge (implementing automatic event logging in system architecture), and its process-heavy approach (mandatory templates, monthly audits) would add friction to prototyping cycles without delivering concrete engineering solutions. It's not technically unviable, but it's more of a PM/compliance function deliverable than something that would meaningfully advance our technical capability for meeting Article 12.\n\nHowever, I should consider: does having structured documentation templates actually help development? Yes, somewhat — knowing what you need to document upfront can inform design decisions (e.g., if you know you'll need to log \"the reference database against which input data has been checked,\" you'd architect the system to capture that). But this proposal doesn't make that connection explicitly.\n\nI'm leaning toward reject with a moderate-low promise score. The idea isn't bad in principle, but as framed, it's an administrative protocol that would slow prototyping without providing the technical architecture we actually need for automatic logging. It's more of a compliance function deliverable than a development engineering solution. Maybe 35-40 on the promise scale — there's some value in structured documentation integrated with dev workflows, but the proposal as stated doesn't address the core technical challenge and would add process overhead.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise 32. It has some kernel of usefulness (version control integration for docs) but is fundamentally misaligned with what a development engineer needs to see — a technical architecture for automatic event recording, not an administrative audit process.\n",
