        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2584\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal directly operationalises the 'shall technically allow for automatic recording' mandate in Article 12(1) by making logging a non-negotiable, architecture-agnostic platform layer rather than a per-project afterthought, which is precisely how an institution prevents the compliance gaps that trigger enforcement exposure under Articles 99–101. The immutability requirement and middleware-based interception also support the traceability and integrity expectations in Recital 71 and give us a defensible audit trail for regulator inquiries; my residual concern is that the proposal does not yet specify how it will satisfy the granular minimum fields of Article 12(3) (use-period timestamps, reference database identity, matched input data, verifier identification under Art. 14(5)) for Annex III point 1(a) systems, but as a foundational infrastructure commitment this is a design detail to be resolved in implementation rather than a disqualifying gap.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 84\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution subject to the EU AI Act, with focus on institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P2584 describes a \"Unified Event Logging Infrastructure\" — a standardized software library and API that mandates automatic event recording across all high-risk AI developments, with consistent timestamps, state capture, middleware layer intercepting inputs/outputs into an immutable database.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance perspective:\n\n**Strengths from a compliance standpoint:**\n- Article 12(1) explicitly requires \"High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" This proposal directly addresses that requirement with a mandatory, automated approach.\n- The \"immutable database\" aspect supports traceability requirements in Recital 71 and Article 12(2), which demands \"a level of traceability of the functioning of a high-risk AI system that is appropriate to the intended purpose.\"\n- Consistent timestamps address the temporal recording needs, particularly for Annex III point 1(a) systems under Article 12(3)(a) (start date/time and end date/time).\n- Removing manual logging burden reduces human error risk — a significant compliance concern since manual logs are vulnerable to omission, alteration, or inconsistency.\n- \"Regardless of the specific AI architecture\" is important because Article 12 applies to all high-risk AI systems uniformly. A unified approach ensures no project falls through the cracks.\n- The middleware intercepting inputs and outputs aligns with Article 12(3)(b) (reference database) and 12(3)(c) (input data for which search led to a match).\n\n**Concerns/weaknesses:**\n- \"Mandates automatic event recording across all high-risk AI developments\" — this is good in principle, but the proposal doesn't address the specific differentiated requirements of Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification, etc.), which require more granular logging including verification of results by natural persons per Article 14(5).\n- The proposal says it will \"provide consistent timestamps and state capture\" but doesn't explicitly mention recording the period of each use (start/end), reference databases, input data for matches, or identification of natural persons involved in verification. These are specific minimum requirements under Article 12(3) that must be addressed.\n- However, as a foundational infrastructure proposal, it's establishing the base layer upon which project-specific logging can build. The middleware approach is sound.\n- \"Immutable database\" — this is good for compliance (prevents tampering with logs), but from an operational standpoint, we need to ensure data retention policies align with the \"lifetime of the system\" requirement in Article 12(1).\n- The proposal doesn't address logging capabilities specific to post-market monitoring (Article 72) or operation monitoring (Article 26(5)) as required by Article 12(2)(b) and (c), but this could be a design detail.\n\n**From institutional survival perspective:**\n- This is a high-value investment because it creates a single, reusable compliance asset across all projects. If we don't have this, each project team must build its own logging, leading to inconsistency and gaps. A gap in any one system's logging = potential non-compliance = fines (up to 35M EUR or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices, 15M EUR or 3% for other violations).\n- It reduces regulatory risk significantly by ensuring uniform compliance with Article 12 across the portfolio.\n\n**From market reputation perspective:**\n- Demonstrating a robust, institution-wide logging infrastructure is a strong signal to regulators (notifying authorities, market surveillance authorities) and to clients/partners that we take AI Act compliance seriously.\n- It supports our ability to respond to information requests from regulators under Article 79(1) or during post-market monitoring.\n\n**From regulatory compliance perspective:**\n- This is fundamentally aligned with the letter and spirit of Article 12. The \"automatic recording\" requirement in paragraph 1 is directly addressed. The traceability emphasis matches Recital 71. The approach of making it mandatory and standardized reduces the risk that individual projects will under-implement logging capabilities.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a strong, well-aligned proposal. It addresses the core regulatory requirement of Article 12(1) in the most robust way possible — institutionalizing automatic logging as a platform capability rather than leaving it to individual projects. The immutability aspect supports integrity of records. The architecture-agnostic design ensures coverage across all high-risk systems.\n\nThe main gap is that it doesn't explicitly enumerate the specific minimum fields required under Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems, but as an infrastructure proposal, establishing the base layer is the right first step, and those specific fields would be configured per project type on top of this foundation.\n\nI would approve this. It's a foundational compliance investment that directly addresses a \"shall\" requirement in the regulation. The promise score should be high — maybe 82-85. It's not a perfect implementation plan (lacks specificity on Annex III 1(a) fields, retention policies, data access for regulators), but as a directional commitment to build unified logging infrastructure, it's exactly what compliance needs to de-risk the institution.\n\nI'll go with approve and a promise of 84.\n",
