r integration headaches later when regulators or auditors need to review across systems.\n\n**Concerns/Weaknesses:**\n- \"Develop a middleware layer\" is a non-trivial engineering investment. It requires dedicated engineering resources upfront before any project benefits. In a lab where every engineer-hour competes with research output, this has an opportunity cost.\n- The proposal says \"across all high-risk AI prototypes\" — but in an R&D context, prototypes may be short-lived or experimental. Over-investing in infrastructure for prototypes that get abandoned could be wasteful. However, the integration with existing pipelines mitigates this somewhat.\n- No mention of who maintains it, what happens when ML pipeline architectures shift, or how versioning is handled. But these are implementation details, not deal-breakers at screening level.\n- The proposal is appropriately scoped — it doesn't try to solve all of Article 12 (e.g., the specific Annex III requirements in paragraph 3, post-market monitoring systems). It focuses on the foundational logging layer.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a high-leverage infrastructure investment that directly addresses my core concerns: it reduces duplicated effort across projects, integrates with existing infrastructure rather than building parallel systems, frees engineering capacity back to scientific work, and has bounded scope appropriate for an R&D lab's funding constraints. The amortization logic is strong — one build serves many future projects. The risk of over-investment is moderate but manageable given the bounded scope described.\n\nI would approve this. It's exactly the kind of \"build once, use many\" infrastructure investment that makes sense when you're resource-constrained and have multiple high-risk AI systems in development. It protects scientific ambition by removing repetitive compliance burden from individual research teams.\n\nPromise score: This is a strong fit for my vantage point. The amortization acr