ing how model changes alter decision pathways) has real scientific value for understanding model drift, capability emergence, and failure modes. However, the proposal is somewhat vague on the actual scientific questions it would answer. \"Discovering how high-risk decisions are actually reached\" is a massive claim - activation patterns alone don't necessarily reveal causal mechanisms. The connection to the EU AI Act's specific requirements (Article 12) feels somewhat bolted on; the scientific value exists regardless of regulatory context, and the regulatory framing doesn't add much intellectual depth.\n\n**Broader Scientific Impact:** If successful, a standardized cross-model instrumentation framework could be genuinely useful for the interpretability community. A queryable format that's compatible with regulatory requirements but also scientifically rich could serve dual purposes. The potential to create a public benchmark or dataset of decision pathways would have broad impact. However, the practical challenges are enormous - storing and querying activation patterns across frontier models at scale is a serious systems engineering challenge, not just a research one.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Low-overhead\" is doing a lot of work in this description. Capturing attention weights across all layers for large transformer models during inference has non-trivial computational cost.\n- The proposal conflates logging (what the Act requires) with interpretability research (what would actually be scientifically valuable). These are different problems.\n- \"Standardized instrumentation API\" across model versions implies a level of architectural homogeneity that may not exist in practice, especially as architectures evolve.\n- The regulatory framing (\"compatible with the Act's requirements\") is somewhat thin - the Act requires event logging (start/end times, reference databases, input data), not activation patterns. The proposal is really a research project wearing compliance c