        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2491\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core idea of a shared logging library to avoid per-project duplication is directionally sound, but the proposal as scoped commits substantial upfront engineering to a 'centralized, immutable ledger' and universal coupling for every prototype—infrastructure that likely duplicates telemetry/monitoring stacks we already maintain and that imposes rigidity on fast-moving R&D prototypes before we have enough concurrent high-risk systems to amortize the build cost. Pre-configuring Annex III templates (which are narrowly scoped to biometric verification under point 1(a)) as 'universal' misallocates design effort, and making every project a dependent of one middleware layer creates a single-point-of-failure that will slow individual prototyping rather than preserve it.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 46\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Lab Director focused on resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal P2491 is a \"Universal Compliance Logging Framework\" - essentially building a shared middleware library for automated logging across all high-risk AI prototypes.\n\nLet me think about this from my specific domain:\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency:**\n- Building a universal framework upfront requires significant engineering investment BEFORE any individual project needs it. This is a classic \"build the platform first\" approach. The question is: do we have enough concurrent high-risk AI projects to justify the amortization of this build cost? If we only have 1-2 high-risk prototypes at any time, the shared framework might be over-engineering relative to just implementing logging per project.\n- However, if we DO have multiple high-risk AI systems in development simultaneously (which an R&D lab developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act likely does), then a shared library avoids duplicating effort across projects. Each project otherwise would need its own logging implementation, which is repetitive and error-prone.\n- The \"shared dependency for every project\" language is concerning - it implies coupling all projects to one framework, creating a single point of failure and potentially slowing down individual projects if the framework needs updates or has bugs.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- The proposal frames this as \"preserving prototyping speed\" by eliminating manual logging per project. This is reasonable IF we already have multiple concurrent high-risk systems. But building a universal framework is itself a substantial resource commitment that diverts engineers from actual R&D work.\n- The \"centralized, immutable ledger\" adds infrastructure cost (storage, access control, immutability guarantees) on top of the software development cost. This is not trivial for an R&D lab with funding constraints.\n- The pre-configured templates for Annex III requirements are useful but could become rigid as regulations evolve or as we develop novel system types that don't fit neatly into existing categories.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Does the lab already have logging/telemetry infrastructure? Many R&D labs already have monitoring, telemetry, and data capture systems for their experiments. The proposal says \"automated event recording and telemetry capabilities\" - this might overlap with or conflict with existing infrastructure rather than leveraging it.\n- A \"centralized, immutable ledger\" suggests new infrastructure build-out rather than integrating with what exists.\n- The modular middleware approach is sound architecturally, but the \"universal\" scope is where efficiency concerns arise.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core idea - sharing a logging library to avoid per-project duplication - is sound from an efficiency standpoint IF multiple concurrent high-risk systems exist. However:\n1. The \"universal\" and \"shared dependency for every project\" framing suggests premature standardization. In R&D, prototypes evolve rapidly; locking them into a shared framework early can slow iteration.\n2. The centralized immutable ledger is heavy infrastructure that may be disproportionate for the current scale of high-risk systems in development.\n3. It doesn't mention leveraging existing telemetry/monitoring infrastructure - it seems to propose building new parallel infrastructure.\n4. The Annex III point 1(a) specific requirements (paragraph 3 of Article 12) are very specific to biometric identification use cases. Pre-configuring templates for that narrow case and calling them \"universal\" is questionable.\n\nHowever, the basic concept of a shared logging library IS efficient if we have enough concurrent projects. The issue is the scope and ambition of making it truly \"universal\" with centralized immutable storage from day one. A lighter-weight version - a shared library with standard interfaces but local logging first, with optional central aggregation - would be more resource-efficient.\n\nI think this is borderline. The idea has merit but as scoped, it's over-invested relative to what we likely need now. It's the kind of \"platform build\" that consumes 6-12 months of engineering before delivering value to individual projects, and in the meantime those projects either go without proper logging or build their own ad-hoc solutions anyway (which defeats the purpose).\n\nI'll lean toward reject but with a moderate promise score - it's a reasonable long-term direction but as scoped for immediate resource commitment, it's premature and over-engineered. Actually, let me reconsider...\n\nThe proposal says \"This eliminates the need for manual logging implementation in every new project, preserving our prototyping speed.\" This is the key value proposition from an efficiency standpoint. If we're going to have multiple high-risk AI systems (which Article 12 applies to), then yes, building it once and reusing IS more efficient than building per-project.\n\nBut \"universal\" and \"centralized immutable ledger\" are where I lose confidence in resource efficiency. The lab can likely handle logging requirements with a lighter approach initially - maybe just the shared library without the centralized immutable ledger infrastructure - and scale up as needed.\n\nDecision: reject (as scoped, it's too heavy an upfront investment for the current stage; a lighter version would be more appropriate)\nPromise: 45-50 range. The underlying idea is sound but the execution scope is overcommitted relative to our resource constraints and existing infrastructure.\n\nLet me settle on 48. It's a legitimate direction but as written it commits significant resources to building platform infrastructure that may not match current scale, duplicates potential existing telemetry capabilities, and the \"universal\" coupling creates rigidity in an R&D context where prototypes change rapidly.\n",
