r. The \"logging efficiency\" angle is relevant — if logging adds too much overhead, it can slow down prototypes. But the mechanism proposed (monthly human audit) is the wrong tool for this.\n- The proposal creates a bottleneck: everything goes through two people monthly. That doesn't scale well and creates dependency.\n\n**What would actually help from my vantage point:**\n- Logging middleware/sidecar that's built into the system architecture by default\n- CI/CD gates that check for required log fields\n- A reference logging SDK or template that new projects can adopt in their first sprint\n- Automated performance profiling of telemetry overhead as part of test suites\n\nThe proposal is a reasonable organizational idea but from my technical prototyping vantage point, it's:\n1. Too slow (monthly cadence)\n2. Resource-heavy (pulling people off active work)\n3. Not directly enabling faster/better prototyping\n4. A governance mechanism rather than a technical enabler\n\nHowever, I should acknowledge some value: having someone focused on telemetry overhead IS relevant to my domain if logging is slowing down prototypes. But the \"monthly audit\" format is wrong. The \"central point of accountability\" is more of a compliance/org concern than a dev-engineering one.\n\nI'm leaning toward reject or low approve. Let me think about whether this clears my bar...\n\nThe proposal doesn't actually produce any technical artifact that makes prototyping faster or more viable. It's an organizational structure. From my vantage point, I care about: can we build the logging capability efficiently? Can we prototype systems that meet Article 12 without massive overhead? This proposal addresses neither directly. It creates a review layer that adds process overhead (which is ironic given its stated goal of minimizing performance overhead).\n\nI'll reject it. The promise score should reflect that while there's a kernel of relevant thinking (telemetry optimization), the mechanism is wrong for