        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1737\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal embeds a hard, automated compliance gate directly into the engineering workflow, verifying that Article 12(1) and 12(3) logging capabilities are present before any system can leave the development environment. From a regulatory-compliance standpoint, a CI/CD-enforced check is precisely the kind of systemic, evidence-generating control that demonstrates to authorities (and to ourselves) that our quality-management system under Article 17 actually operates — it converts a 'shall' obligation into a technical blocker rather than a post-hoc audit item, materially reducing exposure during prototyping and external testing phases. My only minor reservation is that the proposal anchors its test suite specifically to Article 12(3) minimums (Annex III point 1(a) systems); if we also develop high-risk systems in other Annex III categories or under Annex I, we will need parallel gates mapped to the broader Article 12(2) event-relevance criteria — but that is a scope-expansion question for implementation planning, not a reason to reject the core mechanism.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 84\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the perspective of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain concerns are: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P1737 is about implementing a \"High-Risk Prototype Certification Pipeline\" — a CI/CD gateway that validates logging capabilities before deployment. Let me assess this carefully.\n\n**Key aspects of the proposal:**\n1. It integrates into CI/CD pipeline as a technical gateway\n2. Validates logging capabilities BEFORE any deployment\n3. Implements automated tests verifying the system's ability to generate mandatory logs per Article 12, section 3\n4. Prototypes failing these telemetry tests cannot be pushed to external testing environments\n5. Purpose: prevents non-compliant systems from leaving development phase, reducing legal risk during prototyping\n\n**From a Compliance Officer perspective:**\n\nStrengths:\n- This is directly aligned with Article 12 requirements. The regulation mandates that high-risk AI systems \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs).\" Having an automated gate ensures this requirement is verified before any system moves forward.\n- It specifically references Article 12(3), which lists the minimum logging capabilities for Annex III point 1(a) systems. This shows regulatory specificity.\n- Preventing non-compliant systems from reaching external testing environments reduces the risk of being caught out by regulators or in incidents where logs are expected but absent.\n- CI/CD integration means this is systematic, repeatable, and not dependent on manual checks — which is exactly what a compliance function wants: built-in controls rather than post-hoc audits.\n- \"Institutional survival\" angle: if we ship a high-risk system without proper logging, we're in direct violation of Article 12(1). This could lead to fines (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices, though for general non-compliance it's up to €15M or 3%). Having this gate is a strong evidentiary control showing due diligence.\n- \"Market reputation\" angle: demonstrating that we have systematic compliance checks built into our development pipeline shows regulators and clients that we take compliance seriously.\n\nPotential concerns:\n- The proposal says \"validates logging capabilities before any deployment.\" Article 12(1) says logs must be recorded \"over the lifetime of the system.\" A CI/CD gate verifies the capability exists, but doesn't verify ongoing operation. However, this is a reasonable first-line control — you can't have lifetime logging if the capability isn't built in at development time.\n- The proposal references \"Article 12, section 3\" for the specific telemetry tests. This is correct — Article 12(3) specifies minimum logging for Annex III point 1(a) systems. However, I should note that not all high-risk systems fall under Annex III point 1(a). If we develop other types of high-risk AI systems (Annex I or other Annex III categories), Article 12(2) applies with more general requirements about \"events relevant for\" risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring. The proposal seems narrowly focused on 12(3) minimums. But this is a prototype pipeline — it's a good start and can be expanded.\n- Actually, re-reading: the proposal says \"verify a system's ability to generate the mandatory logs specified in Article 12, section 3.\" This could be interpreted as only testing for 12(3) requirements. For systems that don't fall under Annex III point 1(a), we'd still need 12(2) compliance. But the proposal is about a \"prototype certification pipeline\" — it's specifically targeting high-risk prototypes, and if they're in the biometric/critical infrastructure category (Annex III 1a), this is spot-on. Even for other categories, having logging capability verified is good practice.\n\n- One concern: \"Prototypes failing these specific telemetry tests cannot be pushed to external testing environments.\" This is a strong control. From a compliance perspective, I'm supportive of hard gates. However, I need to consider whether this could create operational friction that leads people to find workarounds (e.g., pushing to non-\"external\" internal test environments where the gate doesn't apply). But as written, it's a reasonable control.\n\n- The \"reducing legal risk during prototyping\" framing is important. During prototyping/development, are we already subject to Article 12? The AI Act applies when systems are placed on the market or put into service. Prototypes in internal development might not be \"on the market.\" However, if they go to external testing environments (which could be considered a form of deployment/testing that regulators could scrutinize), having logging is prudent. Also, as we move from prototype to pre-production, these controls need to be in place already. Building them now reduces transition risk.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong compliance-aligned proposal. It's specific to the regulatory text, implements an automated control (which is what regulators prefer over manual processes), and directly addresses Article 12 requirements. From my vantage point of institutional survival and regulatory compliance, this is exactly the kind of systematic, built-in control that demonstrates our organization has a functioning compliance management system. It's not just \"we'll keep records\" — it's \"we'll technically prevent non-compliant systems from advancing.\"\n\nI'd approve this. The promise score: it's well-targeted, specific to the regulation, and addresses a real compliance gap in the development lifecycle. My only minor reservation is that it focuses on 12(3) minimums specifically, which may not cover all high-risk system categories we develop. But as a pipeline gate for prototypes, this is excellent. I'd give it around 82-85.\n\nLet me settle on my decision: APPROVE, promise score around 84.\n",
