ystem behavior from operational data could be scientifically novel. But the proposal doesn't engage with this depth — it just says \"flags anomalies.\"\n- The idea of monitoring AI systems for behavioral changes using a separate model is related to interpretability, robustness evaluation, and distribution shift detection — all active research areas. But the proposal as stated is thin on what's actually novel or scientifically challenging about it.\n\n**Intellectual Merit:**\n- As stated, this reads more like an engineering product (\"alert compliance officers when thresholds are exceeded\") than a research contribution. The intellectual content is minimal: apply anomaly detection to logs. There's no mention of novel algorithms, theoretical contributions, new evaluation frameworks, or fundamental questions about what constitutes \"modification\" in the behavioral trace of a complex AI system.\n- It could be elevated by asking: What is the information-theoretic limit on detecting modification from partial behavioral observations? How do we distinguish legitimate distribution shift (new users, new data) from actual model modification? These are scientifically rich questions the proposal doesn't touch.\n\n**Broader Scientific Impact:**\n- If done well, methods for detecting AI system drift/modification from logs could be broadly applicable beyond EU compliance — to safety monitoring in autonomous systems, healthcare AI, etc. But as proposed, it's framed purely around compliance and market reputation (\"enhancing our market reputation for safety\"), which signals a product/compliance motivation rather than scientific impact.\n- The proposal doesn't mention publishing findings, contributing to open benchmarks, or generalizing the methodology beyond this specific regulatory context.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is primarily an engineering/application proposal dressed in light research language (\"AI-driven monitoring tool,\" \"secondary diagnostic