s significant technical gaps that would slow down actual prototyping and delivery. I'm leaning toward a moderate promise score. It's not something I'd reject outright because the basic data pipeline concept is sound, but it's not something I'd enthusiastically back either because the \"mapping\" problem is hand-waved and the CI/CD placement is architecturally questionable.\n\nI'll give it around 52-58. Let me settle on 55. It's technically feasible in a narrow sense but the proposal as written would lead to a prototype that doesn't generalize well, creating rework rather than saving time. Actually, let me reconsider - from a pure prototyping speed perspective, could you build something useful quickly? A basic log parser that outputs structured data for Article 12(3) fields (timestamps, database references, input data, person IDs) is quite doable in a week or two. The \"mapping to requirements\" part is where it gets fuzzy. \n\nI'll go with approve at a moderate score - the narrow version of this (auto-generating structured log exports for Article 12(3) fields) is technically viable and would save real engineering time. The broader \"maps to all of Article 12\" framing is overreaching but the core use case exists. Actually, wait - should I approve or reject? The bar is whether it clears my professional threshold. \n\nThe proposal as written has enough technical merit in its narrow interpretation (automating log extraction for record-keeping fields) that it's worth pursuing, even if the full scope described is overambitious. A development engineer would see the basic pipeline concept as sound and the specific Article 12(3) data points as clearly defined. I'll approve with a moderate promise score reflecting the gap between the aspirational description and what's actually buildable quickly.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, 58.\n",
