 it amortizes a one-time cost across many projects and reduces ongoing manual effort.\n\nThe main risk is scope creep or over-engineering for the \"unified\" part — but even so, having a common foundation is far cheaper than N separate implementations. I'd approve this with moderate-to-high promise. It's not 100 because there are implementation risks (the framework might be too rigid for diverse model architectures), and it doesn't explicitly address all the specific Article 12(3) requirements, but as a foundational architecture decision, it's sound and resource-efficient.\n\nI'll give it around 82-85 promise. It's clearly the right architectural direction, highly efficient in resource allocation, and well-aligned with using existing infrastructure. The slight deduction is for the risk that \"unified\" could mean over-engineering or insufficient flexibility for very different model types, and the proposal doesn't explicitly address all minimum logging fields from Article 12(3).\n",
