        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Lab Director concerned with resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nProposal P2063: \"Automated Traceability Middleware Framework\" - a standardized software layer for automatic logging across AI architectures, integrated into CI/CD pipeline, using API hooks and immutable ledgers.\n\nLet me assess this from my specific domain concerns:\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency:**\n- Building a custom middleware framework is a significant engineering investment. The proposal says \"we will build\" which implies developing in-house rather than leveraging existing solutions.\n- However, if we're building multiple high-risk AI systems (which as an R&D lab subject to the EU AI Act we likely are), a shared middleware layer could amortize costs across all projects rather than each team building its own logging infrastructure.\n- The CI/CD integration is smart - it means compliance becomes part of the deployment process automatically rather than a separate manual step. This reduces ongoing per-system overhead.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- Building custom middleware diverts engineering resources away from actual AI research/scientific development. This is a real concern.\n- However, the alternative - each research team manually implementing logging to meet Article 12 - would be even more resource-intensive over time and would pull researchers even further from their core work.\n- The \"without manual developer overhead\" claim is important: it front-loads cost but reduces recurring burden on scientists.\n- The scope seems reasonable - API hooks, immutable ledgers, timestamps, decision paths. This isn't an over-engineered solution; it's a focused compliance layer.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Integration into the existing CI/CD