        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P1201 from my vantage point as Compliance Officer of an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\n\nMy domain of concern: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal is about conducting a systematic risk categorization audit of all current and planned R&D projects against EU AI Act risk levels (Annex III categories), then allocating logging resources strictly based on those classifications.\n\nLet me think through this carefully:\n\n**Strengths from a compliance perspective:**\n1. This is fundamentally a prerequisite to any compliance program. You cannot implement Article 12's logging requirements without first knowing WHICH systems are high-risk. The obligation under Article 12 applies specifically to \"high-risk AI systems\" - so categorization is the gateway determination.\n2. Mapping against Annex III categories is exactly what the regulation requires - you need to determine if your system falls under Annex I (product safety) or Annex III (specific use cases like biometrics, critical infrastructure, employment, etc.)\n3. The cross-functional committee approach is smart because risk categorization isn't purely a legal question - it depends on intended purpose, deployment context, and technical architecture that engineers understand.\n4. \"Allocate logging resources strictly based on those classifications\" - this is good compliance hygiene. It prevents over-engineering (wasting resources) AND under-engineering (missing obligations).\n5. This directly supports institutional survival: misclassification is a major regulatory risk. If you build logging into a non-high-risk system, you waste money but aren't penalized. If you DON'T build logging into a high-risk system, you face enforcement action, fines (up to 7% of global turnover), and market withdrawal orders.\n6. \"Current AND planned\" is important - Article 12 applies from the point of placing on market, so planning ahead for new projects is essential.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n1. The phrase \"only spend expensive engineering hours on systems where the regulation actually applies\" could be read as a cost-cutting justification rather than a compliance necessity. However, in practice, this IS the correct interpretation - Article 12's obligations are specific to high-risk systems. Non-high-risk systems have different (or no) logging requirements under this specific article.\n2. The risk categorization must be done carefully and documented itself, as the classification is part of what authorities will examine. A hasty or incorrect classification could be worse than no classification at all - it creates a false sense of compliance.\n3. The proposal says \"Annex III categories\" specifically but doesn't mention Annex I (AI systems that are safety components of products covered by Union harmonisation legislation). This is a gap, though it might just be shorthand in the proposal text.\n4. \"Strictly based on those classifications\" - there's a nuance here. Even if a system is not currently high-risk, its intended purpose could change (substantial modification), or a regulatory update could reclassify it. The logging infrastructure should perhaps have some flexibility. But for initial resource allocation, this is reasonable.\n5. From a market reputation standpoint, having a documented risk categorization process demonstrates to customers and partners that the institution takes compliance seriously - this is actually a competitive advantage.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis proposal addresses a fundamental prerequisite for any Article 12 compliance program. Without knowing which systems are high-risk, you cannot implement logging capabilities appropriately. The cross-functional approach is appropriate because intended purpose determination requires both legal and technical input. The \"current AND planned\" scope shows forward-looking thinking.\n\nThe one concern is the phrasing around cost optimization (\"only spend expensive engineering hours\"), but the substance of the proposal - that resource allocation should be proportional to regulatory obligation level - is correct and necessary. Over-allocating to non-high-risk systems isn't required, though building in some flexibility for reclassification would be prudent.\n\nFrom my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation), this is essential infrastructure for the compliance program. Without it, we're blind to where our obligations lie. It's not sufficient on its own (you still need to build the actual logging capabilities), but it's a critical first step that de-risks the entire program.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score should be high because without proper categorization, everything else in the compliance program is built on sand. However, I'd note the Annex III-only mention as a gap and the cost-optimization framing as slightly misaligned with a pure compliance mission (though substantively correct).\n\nPromise: I'll give it around 82-85. It's essential but incomplete (Annex I not mentioned explicitly), and the \"strictly\" language could create rigidity issues for systems whose classification might evolve.\n",
