ject, not a greenfield build.\n\n**Concerns / limitations:**\n- \"Templated reporting engine mapped to EU AI Act requirements\" — the mapping itself requires regulatory expertise and will need updating as guidance evolves (implementing regulations, harmonized standards). This is a non-trivial ongoing cost.\n- The scope as described is somewhat broad — syncing model versioning, training data metadata, AND validation results into one system. There's integration complexity here. Multiple toolchains might not play nicely together.\n- \"Replaces manual report writing\" — in practice, it will reduce but not eliminate manual work. Regulatory nuance often requires human judgment. But even 50-70% reduction is substantial.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly address Article 12's logging requirements (automatic recording of events/logs) — it focuses more on the technical documentation aspect from Recital 71. That said, the \"validation results\" sync could partially overlap with logging outputs. It's a reasonable scope for one project; full Article 12 compliance would need additional work on automatic event logging in production systems.\n\n**My assessment:** This is a high-value, bounded-scope integration project that leverages existing infrastructure to reduce ongoing operational costs. From a resource-allocation standpoint, it's attractive because:\n1. It uses tools we already have (experiment tracking)\n2. The output directly serves a regulatory obligation (not optional)\n3. It reduces recurring FTE costs for documentation\n4. It prevents costly audit-preparation rushes\n5. The scope is well-defined enough to be implementable within normal engineering capacity\n\nIt's not a moonshot; it's operational infrastructure that pays back in saved labor hours and reduced compliance risk. For an R&D lab where every engineer-hour on compliance paperwork is an hour not spent on research, this has strong ROI potential.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score should reflect high confidence from my vantage point — it's a clear efficiency win using existing infrastructure with bounded scope. I'd say around 78-82. It's very aligned with my mandate, though there are some integration risks and the regulatory mapping will need ongoing attention that adds modest recurring cost. Not a 90+ because the \"automated\" framing might be slightly optimistic — template engines for regulatory documentation often require significant human curation as regulations evolve, and the multi-source data sync has real engineering friction. But it's clearly worth pursuing.\n\nI'll go with 80.\n",
