nerating documentation (which is more of an Art 11 requirement) and ensuring the logging capability exists. However, bundling both under one framework is reasonable from a compliance operations standpoint.\n2. The proposal doesn't explicitly address Article 12(3) - the specific minimum logging requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification). This is a critical gap. If the lab develops any biometric AI, this middleware would need to capture: period of each use, reference database, input data matching results, and identification of natural persons verifying results. The proposal doesn't mention these specific fields.\n3. \"Intercepts system inputs, outputs\" - this could be problematic for some architectures. Not all models have clean \"input/output\" boundaries (e.g., multi-step reasoning chains, agentic systems). The proposal's generality might oversimplify the technical challenge.\n4. The proposal frames it as a compliance tool but doesn't address who owns maintenance of logs over the system's full lifetime - an R&D institution may not operate in deployment long-term. This is more of an operational concern than pure compliance, but it affects whether logs actually persist as required.\n5. \"Minimizes administrative overhead for researchers\" - from a compliance standpoint, I'd want to ensure that minimizing overhead doesn't mean reducing the depth or completeness of records. There's a tension here.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong, directly relevant proposal for an R&D institution. It addresses the core requirement of Art 12(1) and (2) in a scalable way. The modular approach is appropriate for an institution developing multiple systems. However, it lacks specificity on the Annex III point 1(a) requirements and doesn't explicitly address log retention/durability over system lifetime. Still, as a foundational infrastructure investment, it's highly aligned with compliance needs.\n\nFrom my vantage point (institutional survival, regulato