loss, or reputational damage.\n- The proposal captures \"session metadata and input hashes\" which aligns with Article 12(3) requirements (recording period of use, reference databases, input data).\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- It's a prototyping-stage tool. Article 12 applies to the high-risk AI system itself over its lifetime, not just during prototyping. The proposal says \"baseline AI prototyping templates\" — this is a good starting point but the compliance officer would want assurance that the logging architecture extends through to production deployment of actual high-risk systems.\n- It captures \"input hashes\" rather than full input data. Article 12(3)(c) requires recording \"the input data for which the search has led to a match.\" Hashes alone may not suffice for full traceability — you'd need to be able to reconstruct or reference the actual input. However, for Annex III point 1(a) systems specifically, this might be sufficient as a baseline; for other high-risk categories, more granular logging would be needed.\n- The proposal mentions \"session metadata\" but doesn't explicitly address all of Article 12(3)'s minimum requirements: (a) start/end date and time of each use, (b) reference database against which input data has been checked, (c) input data for match, (d) identification of natural persons involved in verification.\n- \"Lifetime of the system\" is a long-duration requirement — the proposal doesn't address log retention, storage architecture, or integrity over extended periods.\n- From a market reputation standpoint, having a standardized compliance infrastructure is actually a strong signal to regulators and customers.\n\n**Overall assessment from Compliance Officer perspective:**\nThis is a solid foundational step that directly addresses Article 12(1)'s core requirement of automatic event recording. Building it into the prototyping pipeline means the institution develops muscle memory for compliant logging before systems go high-risk. The fr