arly. The proposal claims it catches gaps \"early in the research pipeline,\" but scheduling sprints at the *conclusion* of a development cycle means you're finding out near the end that your logging infrastructure was inadequate. That's not really \"early.\" True early catching would mean integrating compliance checks continuously throughout development (shift-left), not batch-auditing at the end.\n- The proposal says it \"prevents costly last-minute redesigns by catching record-keeping gaps early in the research pipeline\" — but the mechanism described (end-of-cycle sprints) is actually a late-stage check, contradicting the stated rationale. This is an internal inconsistency that suggests the proposal wasn't well thought through.\n- A \"formal internal audit process\" with rotating teams adds bureaucratic overhead to what could be more efficiently handled by embedding logging requirements into development standards from day one (e.g., making Article 12 compliance part of the definition-of-done for each sprint/module rather than a separate gate).\n- The resource cost: dedicated sprints of senior researchers = significant person-hours pulled from research. For an R&D institution operating under funding constraints, this is a meaningful opportunity cost.\n\nHowever, there's a reasonable kernel here: some form of pre-deployment verification that logging meets Article 12 is necessary and sensible. The question is whether the mechanism (dedicated senior researcher sprints at cycle-end) is the most resource-efficient way to do it.\n\nMy assessment: The underlying need (verify logging before deployment) is legitimate and important from a resource-efficiency standpoint — catching issues before deployment saves money. However, the proposed mechanism is problematic:\n1. It's described as \"early\" but scheduled at cycle-end (late).\n2. Using senior researchers for compliance verification sprints is an inefficient allocation of scarce human capital when this could be handled 