        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen a single proposal (P2473) from the vantage point of a Lab Director at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me analyze proposal P2473:\n\n\"Efficient Observability for Large Scale AI Models. This research project investigates methods to log high-dimensional system states without incurring prohibitive computational or storage overhead. It focuses on the technical limit of traceability. The team will experiment with sketching algorithms and compressed sensing to maintain a traceable history of model activations and decisions. We will benchmark these against standard logging. This transforms a regulatory constraint into a scientific contribution regarding AI system transparency.\"\n\nFrom my vantage point as Lab Director focused on resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:\n\n**Rationale assessment:**\n\nPros:\n- This directly addresses Article 12(1): \"High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" For large-scale models, logging every activation is indeed computationally and storage-prohibitive. So this is a genuine engineering problem that the lab will face in compliance.\n- Sketching algorithms and compressed sensing are legitimate, well-studied techniques for data compression/summary. There's existing infrastructure in ML systems (e.g., model interpretability tools, profiling frameworks) that could be leveraged.\n- The framing of \"transforming a regulatory constraint into a scientific contribution\" is appealing — it justifies the work on both compliance and publication fronts. This means the output serves dual purposes: compliance documentation AND publishable research, which improves resource-allocation efficiency per unit of funding.\n- It's scoped: experiment with specific techniques (sketching, compressed sensing), benchmark against standard logging. This is a bounded project, not an open-ended exploration.\n\nCons / Concerns from my vantage point:\n- \"Investigates methods\" and \"focuses on the technical limit of traceability\" — this has a research flavor that could stretch beyond what's needed for compliance. Article 12 doesn't require logging every activation; it requires automatic recording of *events* (logs). The proposal seems to aim at logging full model activations, which may be overkill relative to the regulatory requirement. Events ≠ full state. This could mean spending resources solving a harder problem than required.\n- However, if the lab's high-risk systems are complex (e.g., large foundation models used in Annex III contexts), then \"events\" might indeed be high-dimensional and this research could be necessary. But for simpler high-risk systems, standard logging suffices.\n- The proposal says \"experiment with sketching algorithms and compressed sensing\" — these require specialized ML/systems expertise. Does the lab have this infrastructure? If we need to hire or build new capability, that's a resource cost. But if we already have data compression / signal processing groups, this leverages existing infrastructure well.\n- The scope seems reasonable: it's an experiment + benchmark, not building a production system. That limits the resource commitment.\n\nOverall assessment from my vantage point:\n- This is a bounded research project that directly serves compliance needs (Article 12) while producing publishable output. The dual-use nature improves resource efficiency.\n- The main risk is scope creep toward \"logging full model states\" when the regulation only requires event logging. But even so, understanding the technical limits of traceability at scale is valuable for the lab's longer-term compliance architecture.\n- It leverages existing ML infrastructure (model profiling, data pipelines) and adds a focused research component without requiring entirely new capabilities.\n- The resource commitment seems proportionate: a team experimenting with known techniques and benchmarking them. Not a multi-year open-ended program.\n\nI'd lean toward approving this. It's well-scoped, directly relevant to the regulatory requirement I'm responsible for implementing, and produces dual value (compliance + research). From a resource-allocation perspective, it's efficient: it uses existing infrastructure, has clear deliverables (benchmarks), and addresses a real problem at scale.\n\nMy concern is whether \"logging high-dimensional system states\" overshoots what Article 12 actually requires (events, not full state). But even if it does slightly overshoot, the knowledge gained about compression of observable signals would be directly transferable to compliant event logging at scale. So the research still has clear application value.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's a solid, well-motivated project with dual compliance/research value, but there's a slight mismatch between what it investigates (full state logging) and what the regulation strictly requires (event logging), and it does require specialized expertise that may not be trivially available in existing infrastructure.\n\nLet me settle on 75. I'll approve.\n",
