        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2375\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core pattern—middleware interception feeding an append-only, hash-chained store—is technically proven and directly satisfies Article 12(1)'s automatic-recording mandate; however, 'all high-risk models' glosses over significant heterogeneity in I/O schemas across model families (LLMs vs. CV pipelines vs. tabular classifiers), meaning the wrapper will need per-architecture adapters that reintroduce exactly the per-project engineering effort the proposal claims to eliminate. On prototyping speed: an immutable, cryptographically-hashed store on every inference call adds measurable latency and rigidity during rapid iteration cycles unless a clear dev/test bypass or async-sampling tier is designed in from the start.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 74\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the vantage point of a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2375 is: \"Unified Automated Logging Architecture\" — a middleware layer that intercepts inputs, outputs, and metadata for all high-risk models, storing them in an immutable database with cryptographic hashing.\n\nLet me assess this from my professional vantage point (technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping):\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- A middleware/logging wrapper is a well-understood pattern in software engineering. Intercepts inputs/outputs/metadata → stores in a database. This is technically very feasible.\n- \"Cryptographic hashing\" for immutability — this is standard practice (hash chains, append-only logs). Feasible but adds complexity.\n- The challenge: \"all high-risk models\" implies heterogeneity. Different model architectures (LLMs, computer vision, tabular, time-series) have very different I/O patterns. A single standardized wrapper that works across all of them is ambitious. You'd need adapters for each model type.\n- \"Secure, immutable database\" — this is a non-trivial infrastructure project. Think append-only stores, potentially distributed, with access controls. Not trivial but well-trodden path (event sourcing patterns, audit logs).\n- Timestamping throughout system lifetime — technically straightforward at the software level.\n- The \"without requiring redundant engineering effort from individual researchers\" claim is somewhat idealistic. Researchers will still need to integrate their specific model pipelines into the wrapper, handle edge cases, deal with performance overhead of logging every inference.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- From a prototyping speed perspective, this is a significant upfront infrastructure investment before any research can proceed. Building this middleware layer could take months of engineering time.\n- However, once built, it DOES accelerate prototyping for subsequent projects — no need to re-implement logging each time.\n- Risk: the \"one-size-fits-all\" wrapper may become a bottleneck. If the middleware is slow or inflexible, it slows down every experiment. Researchers iterating quickly on prototypes might find the mandatory logging layer adds latency or friction.\n- The cryptographic hashing and immutability requirements add overhead to every single inference call. For high-throughput prototyping (e.g., running thousands of test cases), this could be a measurable performance hit.\n- \"Secure, immutable database\" — during active prototyping, researchers often need to iterate, delete bad runs, re-run experiments. An immutable store complicates the development workflow unless there's a clear separation between dev/test environments and production logging.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nThis is a solid, well-scoped engineering project that addresses a real regulatory requirement (Article 12(1) — automatic recording of events). The technical approach is sound: middleware interception + immutable storage + hashing. It's the kind of infrastructure investment that makes sense at scale.\n\nConcerns from my vantage point:\n1. \"All high-risk models\" is scope creep in a single proposal. Different model families will need different adapters, and the heterogeneity problem is non-trivial.\n2. The performance overhead during prototyping/experimentation phases could be significant if not carefully architected (async logging, sampling options for dev environments, etc.).\n3. The \"without requiring redundant engineering effort\" claim underestimates the integration work per model type.\n4. However, the core concept is technically viable and the architecture pattern is proven.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a technically feasible infrastructure project that directly addresses Article 12(1) and partially (a)-(c). The main risk is execution complexity across heterogeneous models, but the fundamental approach is sound. For speed/quality of prototyping, it's a net positive once built, though there's an upfront cost and some ongoing friction risk.\n\nI'd say this is a strong proposal — maybe 72-78 range. It's clearly viable, addresses a real need, but has execution risks around heterogeneity and performance that could slow things down if not handled carefully. I'll approve it.\n",
