nfrastructure:**\n- MLflow already exists in the lab — this is a direct repurposing of existing infrastructure for regulatory compliance.\n- The experiment tracker already captures hyperparameters, dataset versions, validation results. The proposal just adds an output layer.\n- No new servers, no new SaaS subscriptions (presumably), no new data pipelines to build from scratch.\n\n**Concerns/risks:**\n- \"Custom template engine\" — who builds this? What's the maintenance cost when regulations update or document format changes?\n- Real-time generation during training adds a small overhead to training loops, but MLflow already does real-time logging so this is marginal.\n- The scope as described covers technical documentation (Recital 71) well, but Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems requires very specific data points (start/end times of use, reference database, input data matches, verification personnel). This proposal focuses on training/documentation side, not the operational logging side. However, that's a different subsystem — this proposal is still valuable for the documentation component.\n- \"In real-time during the training process\" — technically, some validation results aren't available until after training completes. Minor implementation detail but worth noting.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a high-efficiency, low-risk, well-scoped proposal that directly leverages existing infrastructure (MLflow) to solve a compliance problem with minimal new resource commitment. The bounded scope of \"map structured data → template\" makes it predictable in terms of cost and timeline. It frees engineers from manual documentation work, which has ongoing labor cost implications. From my resource-allocation perspective, this is one of the highest-leverage compliance investments available: small input (a template engine + integration), large output (continuous automated documentation for all models).\n\nI'd approve this. The promise score should be high — maybe 82-