rrow in scope. It checks metadata presence but the real challenge of Article 12 compliance is ensuring your logging *architecture* captures all relevant events (the traceability requirement for identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring). A log validator doesn't validate that you're logging the right events - only that whatever logs exist have the right fields.\n- But as a first-pass tool to catch obvious gaps early, it has real value and is technically very feasible.\n\n**From my vantage point specifically:**\n- Technical viability: HIGH. This is a well-understood engineering task. No research risk. Could be built with standard tools (JSON/YAML schema validation, log parsing frameworks).\n- Speed of prototyping: HIGH. Bounded scope, clear requirements from the regulation text itself. Could have a working prototype in days.\n- Quality: The tool would produce useful output - a pass/fail report on whether logs meet minimum Article 12 criteria. Clear, actionable.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- It's somewhat narrow - it validates log *content* but not logging *capability*. A system could have perfect logs that still miss critical events because the logging hooks weren't placed correctly. But as a diagnostic tool to catch gaps early, it still has value.\n- The \"reference database\" check (12(3)(b)) is interesting - how do you validate that the logged reference database is actually the correct one? You can check that a field exists and has a value, but semantic correctness is harder. Still, presence checks are useful.\n- For Annex III 1(a) systems specifically (biometrics), the requirements are more specific and thus more mechanically checkable.\n\n**Decision:** This clears my bar. It's technically straightforward, fast to prototype, has clear bounded scope, and addresses a real gap in the development process. The risk of late-stage redesign from missing log fields is real and this tool catches that early. I'd approve it.\n\nPromise score: From my va