omise is about reputation protection and avoiding penalties — those are important but they're more compliance/legal concerns than R&D efficiency concerns\n- That said, avoiding a major regulatory penalty would free up resources that could be redirected to R&D\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a reasonable governance mechanism. From my resource-allocation perspective:\n- It doesn't require new infrastructure — it uses existing systems and the compliance team\n- The quarterly cadence is manageable\n- However, the \"random\" selection and \"immediate retrieval\" requirement could be disruptive to ongoing R&D work\n- The cost is moderate (a few hours of compliance team time per quarter, plus researcher time for retrieval)\n- The benefit is risk mitigation — avoiding penalties that would consume significant resources\n- It doesn't directly advance scientific ambition but it protects the funding envelope by preventing costly compliance failures\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a low-cost, high-value governance mechanism that protects the lab's resource base. It doesn't require new infrastructure or significant R&D diversion. The quarterly cadence is reasonable. The \"random\" aspect might be slightly disruptive but it's manageable. I'd approve it with moderate promise — it's useful and efficient but it's not a transformative initiative from my vantage point; it's good operational hygiene.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 62-68. It's solid, low-cost governance that protects resources, but from my R&D efficiency perspective it's more of a necessary overhead than something exciting. It doesn't directly advance scientific ambition. But preventing a major penalty is important for resource preservation. I'll go with 65.\n",
