n**: By making compliance a fast, automated gate rather than a slow manual review bottleneck, this actually protects scientific velocity. Researchers can iterate rapidly knowing the compliance check runs in minutes, not weeks. It reduces the friction between doing good science and meeting regulatory requirements.\n5. **One-time build, ongoing benefit**: The CI/CD plugin is built once and benefits every subsequent high-risk system project. High ROI over time.\n\n**Arguments for caution/rejection:**\n1. Scope concern: \"Simulates various usage scenarios\" — how many? For which systems? Article 12(3) specifies minimum fields only for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics). The scope of what needs to be tested could balloon depending on the portfolio of high-risk systems being developed.\n2. Maintenance burden: As the AI Act's implementing regulations and standards evolve, the test suite itself will need updating. This is an ongoing resource commitment, not just a one-time build.\n3. However, these are manageable concerns. The core concept is sound from a resource-efficiency standpoint.\n\n**Overall assessment:** This is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It's exactly the kind of infrastructure investment that makes sense: build it once, use it across all projects, reduce per-project compliance overhead dramatically, prevent expensive late-stage failures, and leverage existing CI/CD infrastructure rather than building parallel systems. The one-time cost of developing the plugin is well-justified by the ongoing savings in engineering hours and risk reduction. It also aligns with keeping R&D velocity high (protecting scientific ambition) while meeting regulatory requirements (managing the funding/compliance tension).\n\nI'd rate this quite highly. Maybe 82-85. It's not a perfect proposal — there are scope questions about which systems it covers, and maintenance will be needed as regulations evolve. But from a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, it's clearl