le 12 mandate is about runtime logging capabilities embedded in the system itself. The proposal doesn't address the core logging requirement.\n- That said, from my vantage point, I should judge this on its own merits: Is it a useful compliance tool? Yes, for the documentation generation/update aspect. Does it meaningfully reduce compliance risk? Partially - it addresses one important dimension (up-to-date technical docs) but not the core logging mandate of Article 12.\n\nI think this is approvable from my perspective because:\n1. It directly supports the \"kept up to date throughout lifetime\" requirement\n2. It reduces human error in documentation\n3. It's a practical, implementable tool\n4. From market reputation, having automated compliance documentation is a differentiator for enterprise clients\n\nBut I have reservations because it's framed around Recital 71 rather than the operative Article 12 logging requirements, and it doesn't address the runtime event logging that Article 12 actually mandates. It's complementary but incomplete relative to what I need as Compliance Officer.\n\nI'll approve it but with a moderate-to-good promise score reflecting that it's useful but addresses only one dimension of my compliance needs. Maybe around 68-75. It's clearly valuable, reduces real risk, and is implementable. But it's not the full picture of what Article 12 requires, and the framing slightly misidentifies which obligation it's addressing. I'll go with 72 - solidly useful, meaningful compliance advance, but not transformative on its own for my full domain of concern.\n",
