pabilities for: (a) identifying risk situations, (b) facilitating post-market monitoring, (c) monitoring operation. The proposal's focus on \"development lifecycle\" and \"training runs\" may not fully cover operational event logging during deployment.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics), Article 12(3) has very specific minimum logging requirements (start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, identification of natural persons in verification). The proposal doesn't specifically address these granular operational logging needs.\n- However, the \"metadata registry that captures the state of the environment and data lineage during every training run\" is a strong foundation, and the system could be extended to cover operational logs.\n\n**Compliance assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this proposal addresses a critical gap: ensuring we can demonstrate compliance with the documentation and record-keeping requirements. The EU AI Act requires that technical documentation be available to notified bodies and market surveillance authorities. Having an automated system rather than manual processes significantly reduces the risk of incomplete or inconsistent records, which could lead to enforcement actions (fines up to 7% of global turnover for non-compliance with certain provisions).\n\nThe proposal doesn't fully cover Article 12(1)'s operational logging requirement (automatic recording of events during system operation), but it addresses the broader Recital 71 documentation requirements well. It's a strong compliance enabler, though not a complete solution for all of Article 12.\n\nFor market reputation: Having robust automated provenance tracking demonstrates to customers and regulators that we take compliance seriously. This is a positive signal.\n\nInstitutional survival: Non-compliance with record-keeping could lead to system withdrawal orders or fines. This proposal mitigates that risk significantly for the documentation aspect.\n\n**Decision