ed by the regulator achieves this.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- Distributed ledgers are notoriously hard to prototype well. Performance tuning, node synchronization, consensus latency - these take months to get right and introduce many failure modes.\n- The proposal is framed as \"research\" which suggests exploration rather than targeted engineering. For a regulatory compliance requirement with a clear scope (Article 12), you want focused engineering, not open-ended research into \"various attack vectors.\"\n- \"Evaluating against various attack vectors\" is vague and open-ended - this could consume enormous resources without converging on a deployable solution.\n- The phrase \"positions the lab as a global leader in the science of trustworthy AI\" suggests ambition beyond what's needed for compliance. This is a marketing/positioning goal, not an engineering one.\n\n**Does it actually address Article 12?**\n- Article 12 asks for: automatic recording of events, traceability appropriate to intended purpose, specific logging capabilities for certain high-risk categories (start/end times, reference databases, input data, identification of natural persons).\n- None of these requirements demand cryptographic immutability or distributed ledgers. Standard structured logging with append-only storage and integrity checks satisfies the regulation.\n- The proposal solves a problem that isn't in the regulation while potentially neglecting the simpler engineering work actually needed (designing the schema for what events to capture, ensuring start/end times are recorded, storing reference database identifiers, etc.)\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is technically over-engineered relative to the regulatory requirement. From my vantage point of technical viability and prototyping speed/quality:\n- The simpler path (structured logging + hash chains or signed logs) achieves compliance with far less risk and faster delivery.\n- Distributed ledgers introduce performance concerns