pabilities, limitations, algorithms, data, training, testing, validation processes). But that's a domain/mapping problem, not a deep technical one.\n- It could be prototyped quickly: connect to existing tracking tool → template the output → generate document. A working prototype in a few weeks is realistic.\n- The \"up-to-date\" aspect means it needs to be event-driven or periodic, but that's standard infrastructure.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- PDF generation is somewhat dated as an approach. Regulators might prefer machine-readable formats (JSON, XML) alongside human-readable ones. But the proposal says \"PDF summaries\" which satisfies the \"clear and comprehensive form\" requirement of Recital 71.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly address Article 12's logging requirements (automatic recording of events over lifetime). It focuses more on the technical documentation side (Recital 71) rather than the runtime event logging. However, it does mention \"experiment logs\" which could be a starting point.\n- \"Guarantees we have an up-to-date audit trail\" — this is a strong claim. In practice, maintaining \"up-to-date\" requires governance processes, not just automation. But as an engineering tool, it can automate the capture part.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a technically sound, well-scoped proposal that addresses a real compliance need (Recital 71's technical documentation requirement). The engineering complexity is moderate to low — it's integration work plus document generation. It leverages existing infrastructure (experiment tracking tools) rather than requiring greenfield development. A prototype could be delivered quickly. The main risk is scope creep if \"compliance reports\" tries to cover all of Recital 71's requirements without clear boundaries, but the core pipeline concept is solid.\n\nFrom a prototyping speed perspective: this is a good candidate because it builds on existing tools and has a clear input→output transformation. The prototype can demonstrate value q