pliance engineering task dressed up in scientific language.\n- The primary driver here seems to be regulatory compliance (Article 12 record-keeping), not a genuine research frontier question. The \"scientific asset\" framing feels somewhat retrofitted. A provenance graph for regulatory compliance is useful but doesn't push the knowledge frontier per se.\n- However, the reproducibility angle IS at the knowledge frontier — we don't fully understand how sensitive model outputs are to specific dataset compositions and hyperparameter choices. A systematic provenance graph could enable meta-analyses across experiments that would be otherwise impossible. This is where it touches genuine research value beyond mere compliance.\n- The scope is broad (\"every model version,\" \"dataset slices\") which could consume significant engineering resources without clear research output. But the proposal says we're building infrastructure, not a specific study — so the question is whether this infrastructure enables frontier research or merely satisfies a checkbox.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this has genuine merit because:\n1. Reproducibility and traceability are legitimate scientific values, not just regulatory ones.\n2. The system, if well-built, would enable new forms of analysis (e.g., \"what hyperparameter ranges led to the most robust models across X dataset slices?\") that push understanding forward.\n3. It elevates documentation from a compliance artifact to a research tool — this is exactly the kind of reframing that increases intellectual merit.\n\nHowever:\n- The novelty is modest; it's an engineering system, not a new scientific method or discovery.\n- The \"broader scientific impact\" claim depends heavily on whether we actually use it for research insights vs. just filing compliance documents.\n- It doesn't push the knowledge frontier directly; it enables reproducibility, which is necessary but not sufficient for frontier advancement.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a reasonable investment that strengthens the scientific infrastructure of the lab. It's not a moonshot or a paradigm shift, but it genuinely enhances the intellectual rigor and reproducibility of our research output. I'd approve with moderate confidence — maybe 62-68 range. It's useful, scientifically motivated (not purely compliance-driven, though that's also a factor), and could enable new analyses. But it's not transformative from a knowledge-frontier standpoint.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise ~65.\n",
