ed:\n  - It only addresses 12(3)(d) (identification of natural persons). The full article requires logging of period of use (a), reference database (b), input data for matches (c), AND the person identification (d). This proposal seems to focus narrowly on (d). But that's fine - it could be one component of a larger logging solution.\n  - \"Immutable log\" is good practice but the technical implementation matters. If it's just a database table, that's not truly immutable in a security sense. You'd want cryptographic hashing chains or similar. But for prototyping purposes, even a basic append-only store demonstrates viability.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- The proposal is technically low-risk and well within the lab's capabilities to prototype quickly.\n- It's essentially a form submission + SSO authentication + logging. Very standard web development.\n- One concern: \"dedicated digital verification portal\" - if this implies building a full new application rather than integrating with existing workflows, it could be more work than stated. But the \"lightweight\" qualifier suggests they're keeping scope tight.\n- The coupling to corporate SSO is reasonable but introduces a dependency on IT infrastructure teams. For prototyping, you might stub this out initially.\n- Is there any technical risk? Minimal. This is not cutting-edge technology.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a technically straightforward, low-risk proposal that maps cleanly to a specific regulatory requirement (12(3)(d)). From a prototyping speed perspective, it's excellent - you can have something working very quickly. The technical viability is high because the underlying technology is mature and well-understood. It's essentially building an audit log UI with SSO auth.\n\nI'd rate this as quite promising from my vantage point. It's not groundbreaking, but it's exactly the kind of bounded, technically feasible deliverable that clears my bar for \"can we build this qu