fficiency: the core infrastructure (MLflow-based auto-documentation) is sound and efficient. The specific Article 12(3) fields for biometric systems would need to be configured into the logging schema, which is a configuration task, not a new system build.\n\n**Overall assessment from my perspective:**\nThis is a well-conceived, resource-efficient proposal. It leverages existing tools (MLflow), repurposes data already captured for scientific purposes, adds minimal new headcount (one compliance lead), and addresses a real regulatory risk with disproportionate penalty exposure relative to implementation cost. The \"living documentation\" concept directly addresses the Recital 71 requirement that documentation be \"kept up to date appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system.\"\n\nThe main resource concern is whether one compliance lead is enough, but for an initial implementation phase, it's proportionate. The proposal doesn't over-engineer; it uses standard MLOps tooling.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar. It's efficient, pragmatic, and aligns with using existing infrastructure well. I'll approve it.\n\nPromise score: From a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is strong. MLflow adoption is low-cost, the automation reduces recurring manual documentation work (which would be expensive at scale), and the one FTE addition is proportionate. The main uncertainty is whether the monthly audit cadence and single lead are sufficient for all our systems over their lifetimes, but as an initial implementation framework, it's sound. I'd give it around 78-82. Let me settle on 80 — strong proposal with minor concerns about scalability of the human audit component.\n",
