ion resources to pursue it, not funding to explore whether an idea might someday work.\" This is key. A research project exploring zero-knowledge proofs and encrypted logging formats is fundamentally exploratory — we don't know if ZKPs will be practical for this use case at scale, performance-wise.\n- Resource allocation: Implementing Article 12 compliance (basic logging) doesn't require ZKPs or encrypted formats. The regulation says to record events, input data, etc. A straightforward logging implementation would satisfy the letter of the law. This proposal adds a research layer that may not be necessary for compliance.\n- However — if our high-risk systems handle sensitive data (biometrics under Annex III point 1(a) is referenced in Article 12(3)), there IS a genuine tension between recording input data and privacy. But is this a research problem or an engineering problem? Existing encryption, access controls, pseudonymization, and tokenization may suffice without novel cryptographic research.\n- The proposal conflates a compliance requirement with a \"market-leading feature\" — that's a stretch from my resource-allocation perspective. We need to comply; we don't need to be innovative in HOW we comply unless the straightforward approach doesn't work.\n- The scope is somewhat vague: \"explore zero-knowledge proofs AND encrypted logging formats\" — this is broad research territory, not a focused engineering solution.\n- From a funding constraints perspective: dedicating researchers to explore novel cryptographic approaches for logging when simpler solutions (encrypted storage, access-controlled databases, pseudonymization) likely exist is inefficient use of resources.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom the resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this proposal has a fundamental problem: it treats what may be an engineering implementation challenge as an open research question. Article 12 requires logging; we can implement that with existing encryption and access-control technologies without pioneering ZKPs for log storage. The \"research\" framing means uncertain timelines and outcomes — exactly the kind of exploration the prompt says I should NOT fund (I commit to pursuing, not exploring).\n\nHowever, there IS a legitimate kernel: if our systems in Annex III point 1(a) (biometric identification, etc.) must record input data matches per Article 12(3)(c), and that data is highly sensitive, then standard logging might conflict with GDPR or IP protection. The tension is real. But the solution space likely includes well-understood techniques (pseudonymization, encryption at rest, access controls) before we need ZKPs.\n\nThe proposal also says \"prototype these systems within our existing high-risk pipelines\" — this is good for infrastructure use, but it assumes the research will produce something prototypeable on a reasonable timeline, which ZKP research may not.\n\nI think this is a borderline case. The problem identified (privacy vs. traceability) is legitimate and relevant to my domain. But the proposed solution path (ZKPs, novel encrypted logging formats) is exploratory research rather than focused implementation, and it likely over-engineers what could be solved with existing cryptographic infrastructure. From a resource-allocation standpoint, I'd want to first determine whether simpler solutions suffice before committing resources to novel cryptography research.\n\nI'll lean toward reject, but with moderate promise score because the underlying problem is real. The proposal would benefit from being scoped down: \"Assess whether standard encryption/pseudonymization satisfies Article 12(3)(c) for sensitive data; if not, evaluate ZKPs as a fallback\" rather than launching a research project on ZKPs and encrypted formats.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal says it's an R&D institution. They DO develop AI systems AND do research. So there's more latitude for research-oriented proposals. But the prompt is clear: \"approving a