ever, there's a risk: if the middleware adds overhead or becomes brittle (breaks when experiment code changes structure), it could actually SLOW prototyping by creating friction points that require workarounds.\n- The \"reusable\" aspect means one-time investment pays off across many prototypes — good for speed at scale.\n- During prototyping, the exact form of inputs and event triggers is still evolving. A rigid middleware might force researchers into a particular structure, potentially constraining creative exploration. But if it's designed as an opt-in decorator (as stated), this risk is mitigated.\n\n**Compliance Relevance:**\n- Article 12(1) requires \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" Capturing timestamps, inputs, and event triggers aligns well with this.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems specifically (biometric identification), the minimum logging requirements are more specific (reference databases, matched input data, verification persons). A generic middleware would NOT cover these specifics — it would need system-specific extensions. But as a baseline for \"all\" prototypes, capturing timestamps/inputs/triggers is a reasonable foundation.\n- This is a prototype-phase tool, not a production logging solution. That's appropriate — you want to bake logging in early so it doesn't become a retrofit nightmare.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this is technically very viable (well-understood patterns, existing infrastructure), directly improves prototyping speed by removing manual logging burden, and addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) for automatic event recording. The main risks are: over-generalization (\"universal\" may be too ambitious for heterogeneous prototypes), potential brittleness during rapid prototype iteration, and the fact that it won't fully satisfy the specific Annex III 1(a) minimums without additional work. But as a baseline foundation that removes t