to-generating the structured sections for hyperparameters, dataset lineage, version histories) while leaving narrative sections to human review would still be a massive efficiency gain.\n- CI/CD hooks during \"every experimental run\" could introduce overhead that slows down research iteration if not carefully designed. But this is an implementation detail, not a fundamental flaw in the proposal.\n\n**From funding constraints perspective:**\n- This is a bounded engineering project with clear ROI (reduced researcher hours spent on compliance documentation). It's not a moonshot requiring sustained multi-year funding.\n- The components it builds on (version control, experiment tracking, CI/CD) are likely already funded infrastructure. We're adding hooks and a generation layer.\n- Compared to hiring dedicated compliance staff or having senior researchers spend 20% of their time on documentation, this is cost-effective.\n\n**From effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- It leverages existing version control, experiment tracking systems, and CI/CD pipelines rather than building parallel systems.\n- It converts existing data (training logs, metadata) into compliance artifacts without requiring new data collection mechanisms beyond the hooks.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a well-scoped, high-value proposal that directly addresses a core operational pain point for an R&D lab subject to the EU AI Act. The resource efficiency argument is strong: it converts researcher time from compliance overhead back to scientific work. The infrastructure leverage is good—building on existing systems rather than creating new ones. The main risk is overpromising \"automatic\" when full autonomy may not be achievable, but even 70-80% automation would be transformative.\n\nThis clears my bar comfortably. I'd approve it with a high promise score because:\n1. Bounded scope (not open-ended)\n2. High leverage on existing infrastructure\n3. Directly addresses the lab's most expensive compliance cost (researcher time)\n4. Clear ROI and measurable outcomes\n5. Well-understood engineering patterns applied to a specific problem\n\nI'd give it around 82-85. It's not a 90+ because the \"automatic\" claim needs tempering and there's real engineering risk in making generated documentation genuinely compliant (not just present but *comprehensible* per Recital 71). But from a resource-allocation standpoint, this is exactly the kind of high-leverage, bounded investment I want to greenlight.\n",
