uilding a siloed solution has quality/architecture concerns if we don't have a broader logging framework.\n\nThe proposal is technically sound and would be quick to prototype, but it's extremely narrow in scope (one Annex III category) and risks being a one-off module that doesn't generalize. From my perspective as someone focused on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality, I'd say it's viable and fast, but the limited reusability makes me question whether committing scarce implementation resources to this specific silo is the best use — though I shouldn't compare against other proposals.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. My job is to judge each proposal on its own merits. On its own:\n- It IS technically viable (clear spec, standard tech)\n- It WOULD prototype quickly (small data model, clear integration points)\n- The quality risk is low (well-understood problem)\n- But the scope is so narrow that it feels like a small task rather than a \"proposal\" worth committing scarce resources to\n\nI think I'd lean toward approve with a moderate promise score. It's technically viable, quick to prototype, but limited in impact and reusability. It's not a high-risk bet, but it's also not something I'd be excited to champion. Maybe 55-60 range — clearly doable, reasonably fast, but narrow.\n\nWait, let me reconsider the \"reject\" case. Would I reject this? The main reason to reject from my vantage point would be if it weren't technically viable (it is) or if prototyping would be slow/low-quality (it wouldn't be). The narrowness isn't really a technical viability issue — it's a scope/prioritization issue, and the instructions say not to compare proposals. On its own merits, it's a well-defined, quick-to-build, technically sound module.\n\nI'll approve with a moderate score around 58. It's clearly buildable and fast, but from my engineering perspective it's a small, narrow task that doesn't stretch technical capability or create reusable infrastructure.\n",
