c strategic planning advice. Every lab should obviously know what their obligations are. Is this really a novel, actionable proposal or just \"do the obvious compliance triage\"?\n- A \"cross-functional committee\" can be bureaucratic overhead in an R&D environment. Labs tend to move fast; standing up committees can slow things down.\n- However, I think the core value is real: without this mapping, you risk either under-investing (non-compliance) or over-investing (wasting scarce resources on logging infrastructure for non-high-risk systems).\n\nOn balance: From a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is one of the most directly relevant proposals. It's a prerequisite step before any actual implementation work. Without knowing which projects are in scope, you can't allocate resources efficiently. The \"prevent wasteful over-engineering\" angle is exactly what a Lab Director cares about when funding is constrained. The cost of doing this audit (a committee reviewing project descriptions against Annex III) is small relative to the cost of misallocating engineering and infrastructure resources.\n\nHowever, I should note: the proposal mentions a \"cross-functional committee\" which could be seen as adding bureaucratic overhead. But for a one-time or periodic strategic review, that's reasonable and necessary — you need legal/compliance input alongside engineering understanding of what each system actually does.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It directly addresses resource-allocation efficiency by creating the decision framework that prevents misallocation. It's a low-cost, high-leverage activity. I'll approve it with a reasonably high promise score because it's foundational to efficient resourcing, though it's not a novel technical innovation — it's good governance/strategy work.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 78-82. It's very relevant and important, but it's also somewhat \"obvious\" in the sense that any compliance program needs this step. It's not groundbr