 to be extensible enough to capture these domain-specific fields.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- PRO: Reduces per-project overhead. Engineers don't have to reinvent logging for each prototype. This directly speeds up prototyping.\n- PRO: Technical uniformity means less debugging, easier onboarding, consistent behavior.\n- CON: Building this middleware layer itself is a significant engineering investment upfront. It's a platform/infrastructure project that takes time before it delivers value.\n- CON: Risk of over-engineering - if the framework is too rigid or tries to be too universal, it could slow down prototyping rather than speed it up. Engineers might spend time fighting the abstraction layer.\n- CON: The \"without requiring manual coding\" promise may not hold for novel/research prototypes where the pipeline structure changes frequently. In an R&D lab, you're often experimenting with new architectures - a fixed middleware might not fit.\n- However, the proposal positions this as serving \"high-risk AI prototypes\" specifically, which suggests it's targeted at systems that will actually need compliance, not every experimental prototype.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is technically viable and aligns well with my domain concerns. A shared logging library/middleware is a standard software engineering pattern (SDK, instrumentation layer). The main risk is the upfront cost and potential rigidity in an R&D context where prototypes evolve rapidly. But for systems that are heading toward high-risk deployment, this kind of infrastructure investment pays off quickly.\n\nThe proposal is well-scoped: it's not trying to solve all of Article 12 (just the logging/automatic recording part), it targets the right integration point (ML pipelines), and it addresses a real pain point (repetitive compliance coding across projects).\n\nI think this clears my bar for approval. It's technically sound, directly improves prototyping speed for compliant systems, and the engineering inv