g Git infrastructure\" — experiment logs, version control metadata, dataset versions, hyperparameters are already being captured (or easily capturable) in our existing workflows.\n- The pipeline doesn't require new hardware, new cloud contracts, or major platform changes. It's an integration layer over what we already have.\n- This is a textbook example of squeezing more regulatory compliance value out of existing infrastructure rather than buying new systems.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Auto-generates the required technical documentation\" — there's a risk that automated generation produces documents that are technically complete but lack the nuanced understanding a human scientist would add. The regulation requires documentation to be \"clear and comprehensive.\" A machine-generated document might miss context about why certain design choices were made.\n- However, the proposal says it extracts from experiment logs and version control — these contain objective data (hyperparameters, dataset versions, validation results). The qualitative narrative parts might still need human input, but the bulk of the factual documentation could be automated.\n- Implementation risk: getting this right for all the different system types in the lab takes time. But since it's building on existing infrastructure, the learning curve is lower than starting from scratch.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It directly addresses resource efficiency (freeing scientist time), leverages existing infrastructure (Git, experiment logs) rather than requiring new capital investment, and maintains scientific ambition by reducing administrative overhead. The fact that it builds on what we already have makes the incremental cost low relative to the benefit across all EU AI Act-regulated projects.\n\nThe main caveat is that \"auto-generates\" might be slightly overpromising for the qualitative/analytical parts of technical documentation, but the core value prop