tation generators, possibly a dashboard or archive. This is a moderately complex engineering project but nothing that requires novel R&D.\n\n**Speed of Prototyping Assessment:**\n- A prototype could be built relatively quickly if the underlying ML pipeline already emits structured metadata. The main work would be: (1) defining the schema for what gets recorded, (2) wiring it into existing CI/CD steps, (3) generating documentation artifacts in a format suitable for regulatory review.\n- This is an incremental enhancement to existing infrastructure rather than a ground-up build. That's good for prototyping speed.\n- However, \"integrated toolset\" could scope-creep quickly if the team tries to solve every aspect of Recital 71 at once (algorithms documentation, risk-management documentation, etc.). The proposal as stated focuses on training data records and validation metrics, which is a well-bounded subset.\n\n**Quality Considerations:**\n- Linking docs to CI/CD ensures documentation stays in sync with code — this directly addresses Recital 71's requirement that \"technical documentation should be kept up to date.\"\n- Version control provides traceability (who changed what, when) which supports audit requirements.\n- The approach is aligned with the regulation's intent: automatic recording, up-to-date documentation, clear and comprehensive form.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal mentions \"market reputation\" and \"regulatory audits\" as motivations — these are business/legal framings rather than technical ones. But that doesn't make the technical solution wrong.\n- It says \"integrated toolset\" which is somewhat vague in scope. From a prototyping perspective, I'd want to see this broken into phases. But the core mechanism (CI/CD → auto-update docs) is clear and buildable.\n- One risk: if our current ML training pipelines don't emit structured metadata about data provenance or validation results, there's prerequisite work needed before the documentation pipeline can 