a points for Annex III point 1(a) systems). The term \"efficiency\" could be misleading and might lead the committee to optimize for speed rather than completeness.\n- The proposal focuses on \"final validation phase\" — but Article 12 says logs must cover \"the lifetime of the system,\" not just at deployment. A gate only at final validation doesn't ensure ongoing logging capability is maintained throughout the system's operational life. Post-market monitoring (Article 72) and operation monitoring (Article 26(5)) require continuous logging, not a one-time sign-off.\n- It's somewhat generic: \"audits system logs against Article 12 requirements\" — but doesn't specify how it handles the specific sub-requirements of Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (start/end times, reference database, input data matches, identification of verifying persons).\n- The proposal is a process/protocol, which is relatively low-cost to implement compared to actual engineering work on logging capabilities. It's a governance layer on top of technical implementation.\n- From a market reputation standpoint, having a documented internal compliance gate is a positive signal to regulators and business partners.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a sensible, proportionate governance measure that directly supports Article 12 compliance. It's not a substitute for the actual engineering work (building logging capabilities into systems), but it's a necessary verification step. The main weakness is that \"final validation phase\" timing risks missing the \"lifetime\" requirement, and \"efficiency\" is the wrong metric. But as a process addition, it's low-cost, high-value from a compliance perspective, and reduces institutional risk significantly.\n\nI would approve this. It's a clear compliance control that fills a real gap in our governance structure. The promise score: it's solid but not transformative — it's a necessary governance layer but doesn't by itself ensure compliance (the technical imp