ther model doesn't satisfy the requirement for traceable event recording — it's a derived artifact, not a record of what actually happened.\n   - The proposal says \"instead of raw system logs.\" This is problematic. Article 12(3) explicitly requires specific structured data fields (timestamps, database references, input data, person IDs). You can't replace those with semantic summaries.\n\n4. **What the right engineering approach looks like:**\n   - Structured event logging with deterministic schemas\n   - Append-only audit trails\n   - Integration with existing observability infrastructure\n   - Automated capture of model inference events (inputs, outputs, confidence scores, model version)\n   - For Annex III point 1(a) systems: specific fields for biometric matching scenarios\n   - This is all prototypable in a matter of weeks using existing tools and patterns.\n\n**Decision:** Reject. From my engineering vantage point focused on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality, this proposal solves the wrong problem with the wrong tool at the wrong timescale. It's a research bet where an engineering solution is needed. The regulatory requirement calls for reliable, structured, automatic event recording — not generative neural explanations.\n\n**Promise score:** I'd give this maybe 8-12. There might be some marginal value in having *supplementary* human-readable summaries alongside proper logs (for operator UX), but the core proposal as stated — replacing raw logs with neural-generated semantic events as a research project — is fundamentally misaligned with what's technically needed and what can be prototyped quickly. The \"frontier contribution\" framing tells me this is an academic aspiration dressed up as compliance work. I'll say 10.\n",
