ilities. Does the suite cover ALL categories of high-risk systems or just the biometric ones? The text says \"all mandatory fields\" which could be interpreted broadly, but the example given is specific to point 1(a).\n- However, even if it's initially scoped to one category, the architecture (CI/CD plugin that scans log outputs) is extensible. And as a compliance gate, even partial coverage is valuable.\n- The proposal says \"a new prototype's logging output\" - this suggests it works during development/integration testing, which is good for catching issues early.\n\n**From institutional survival perspective:**\n- This directly prevents the institution from deploying non-compliant systems. A single Article 12 violation could lead to fines (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover under Article 99) and reputational damage.\n- Having an automated gate means we can demonstrate proactive compliance posture to regulators, which is favorable in any enforcement interaction.\n\n**From regulatory compliance perspective:**\n- This operationalizes the \"shall\" requirement in Article 12(1). The \"technically allow for automatic recording\" needs to be verified - this suite does exactly that verification.\n- It ensures traceability requirements are met before deployment, supporting Articles 72 (post-market monitoring) and 79 (risk management).\n\n**From market reputation perspective:**\n- Demonstrating rigorous internal compliance processes enhances the institution's credibility with enterprise clients who will be subject to their own AI Act obligations as deployers.\n- Reduces risk of publicized non-compliance incidents.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong, well-scoped proposal that directly addresses a specific regulatory obligation (Article 12) through an operational mechanism (CI/CD gate). It's not exploratory - it commits resources to building something concrete that has clear compliance value. The early-catch approach minimizes cost of non-compliance. As a Compliance Officer, this is exactly the kind of infrastructure I'd want in place before any high-risk system goes live.\n\nMy decision: approve. This directly serves my mandate of ensuring regulatory compliance and protecting institutional survival.\n\nPromise score: This is highly relevant to my function. It's concrete, actionable, addresses a specific legal obligation, and has clear risk-reduction value. I'd rate this around 82-85. It's not a 90+ because the description focuses somewhat narrowly on one category (Annex III 1(a)) fields as examples, and I'd want to see that the architecture covers all high-risk categories. But the core mechanism is sound and directly serves compliance. Let me settle on 83.\n",
