ldn't need to build logging from scratch each time. This is a force multiplier for our R&D velocity.\n- Pro: Standardization means compliance becomes a \"checklist item\" rather than a per-project engineering challenge, freeing researchers to focus on the actual model work.\n- Con: Building this framework itself takes significant upfront investment before any project benefits from it. There's a risk of over-engineering — trying to be too universal leads to a bloated API that's hard to use, or under-specification where projects still need custom code.\n- Con: \"Universal\" across diverse environments is a classic trap in internal platform teams. The more heterogeneity you try to absorb, the more complex and slow-to-evolve the framework becomes, potentially becoming a bottleneck for prototyping speed rather than an enabler.\n- Con: There's a risk this becomes a large project that takes months before delivering value, during which new high-risk AI projects still need to figure out logging on their own or wait.\n\n**Risk Assessment from my vantage:**\n- The core idea is sound and technically achievable. A logging abstraction layer with configurable schema for Article 12 fields is very doable.\n- The risk is in the \"universal\" and \"across diverse project environments\" scope. If this becomes a big-bang platform project, it will slow prototyping rather than speed it up.\n- A more pragmatic approach would be: start with a well-defined SDK for our primary serving stack (say 80% of use cases), make the schema configurable for Article 12 fields, and let edge cases be handled with custom adapters. This is still technically viable but much faster to deliver.\n- The proposal as stated doesn't address the performance overhead of logging in real-time inference pipelines (latency-sensitive systems), serialization of potentially large input data, or storage/retention architecture — but these are implementation details that a competent engineering team would work through.\n\n**Dec