entification), and other high-risk categories may have different logging needs. A \"modular middleware\" might handle some but not all cases - but the proposal does say \"modular.\"\n- The proposal mentions \"reference database IDs\" which maps to 12(3)(b), but doesn't explicitly mention capturing \"input data for which the search has led to a match\" (12(3)(c)). However, it says \"data queries\" are intercepted, so this might be covered.\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" - need to ensure the framework accounts for long-term retention and accessibility of logs, not just capture. The proposal doesn't explicitly address storage/retention architecture.\n- Recital 71 also emphasizes technical documentation being kept up to date. This is more about logging than documentation, but a compliance officer would want to confirm that this logging feeds into the broader documentation requirements.\n- The proposal is an internal tool - it's not a product feature per se but an infrastructure investment. From a compliance standpoint, this is exactly what you want: a systemic control embedded in the development pipeline.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is strongly aligned with my mandate. Article 12(1) is an absolute obligation (\"shall technically allow\"). Having a standardized framework ensures that no project can accidentally ship without logging capability. For institutional survival, this is critical - non-compliance with mandatory logging requirements could result in enforcement action, market access restrictions, and reputational damage. The \"compliance by default\" approach is exactly what a compliance officer wants to see rather than relying on per-project checklists.\n\nThe proposal is well-scoped and directly addresses the regulatory text. It's not overreaching - it focuses specifically on the logging requirements of Article 12. It's an internal infrastructure investment that scales with the institution.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score should be high be