        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2103\",\n      \"rationale\": \"A single modular logging library with mandatory APIs is the most resource-efficient path to Article 12 compliance: it amortises a one-time engineering investment across every current and future high-risk pipeline, eliminates the duplication that would arise if each project team independently built its own traceability layer, and keeps researchers unburdened by compliance plumbing. The main risk is scope creep in the 'universal' qualifier—domain-specific fields under Article 12(3) (e.g., biometric reference-database logs) will require extensions—but a well-scoped modular core with plug-in interfaces still delivers far better cost-per-system than fragmented per-project efforts, and it slots into existing pipelines rather than demanding new infrastructure.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 82\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to evaluate proposal P2103 from the vantage point of a Lab Director at an R&D institution focused on resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal:\n\n\"Universal High-Risk Logging Framework. A modular software library integrated into all AI pipelines to automate event recording and timestamping. It will provide a standardized way to capture system inputs and outputs. The engineering team will develop a set of mandatory APIs that every high-risk model must call to log its operational state. This ensures consistency across diverse research projects. This prevents fragmented compliance efforts and ensures no high-risk system is deployed without baseline traceability.\"\n\nFrom my vantage point (resource-allocation efficiency, scientific ambition vs. funding constraints, effective use of existing R&D infrastructure):\n\n**Pros:**\n- A modular, reusable framework is efficient — building once and deploying across projects avoids redundant per-project compliance work. This is a resource-efficient approach compared to each team independently solving the logging problem.\n- Standardization reduces cognitive overhead for individual research teams; they don't need to figure out compliance from scratch.\n- \"Mandatory APIs\" creates a clear, auditable boundary — you either call them or you're non-compliant. This is operationally clean.\n- Prevents fragmented efforts, which in an R&D lab with limited engineering staff is a significant efficiency gain. Fragmentation would mean multiple teams building slightly different logging solutions.\n- \"Baseline traceability\" before deployment means you catch compliance issues early rather than retrofitting later (which is more expensive).\n\n**Cons / Concerns:**\n- Building a universal framework up-front requires a significant upfront engineering investment. The lab needs to allocate dedicated engineering time before any specific project benefits. In a resource-constrained R&D environment, this \"build it all first\" approach has opportunity cost — what research could that engineering time have supported?\n- \"Mandatory APIs that every high-risk model must call\" — in an R&D environment, models evolve rapidly. Rigid mandatory APIs may create friction for experimental work or slow down iteration. However, the proposal says \"modular\" which somewhat mitigates this.\n- The scope is \"all AI pipelines\" and \"every high-risk model.\" In a research lab, you might have dozens of projects in various stages. Forcing all to conform before deployment could be seen as either efficient (one framework) or constraining (one-size-fits-all may not suit every research pipeline).\n- The proposal is somewhat generic — it doesn't specify what the logging actually captures beyond \"system inputs and outputs\" and \"operational state.\" Article 12(3) for biometric systems requires very specific fields. A truly universal framework needs to handle those domain-specific requirements too, or it will need per-domain extensions anyway, eroding the \"universal\" benefit.\n- However, from a pure resource-allocation standpoint, even if you need domain-specific extensions on top of a common core, having that common core (timestamping, input/output capture, event recording) is still more efficient than each team building their own.\n\n**Net assessment:**\nThe proposal aligns well with resource-allocation efficiency. Building one modular framework and requiring all teams to use it is the most cost-effective approach versus fragmented per-project compliance. The upfront investment is real but amortized across many projects. It leverages existing engineering capacity in a focused way rather than spreading thin. The \"modular\" aspect helps accommodate diversity in research. The mandatory API requirement creates clear accountability without over-constraining (it's about what you log, not how you do your science).\n\nThe main risk is that \"universal\" might become an over-engineering target — trying to make it work for every possible case before shipping a v1. But the proposal as stated is reasonable: modular library, mandatory APIs for logging operational state, standardization. This is achievable and high-value.\n\nI'd lean toward approve with a moderately high promise score. It's not a 95 because of the upfront cost risk and the \"universal\" scope creep potential, but it's clearly one of the most resource-efficient approaches to compliance. I'll say around 78-82. Let me settle on 80.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The lab director's concern is specifically about:\n1. Resource-allocation efficiency — this proposal is HIGHLY efficient (build once, use everywhere)\n2. Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints — the framework has an upfront cost but pays back across all projects; it doesn't consume research time directly (engineering builds it, researchers just call APIs)\n3. Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure — it integrates INTO existing pipelines rather than requiring new infrastructure\n\nThis is a strong fit. I'll approve with a score around 82.\n",
