        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2503\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Article 12's operative obligation is that the system must *technically allow for the automatic recording of events* and that logging captures specific, enumerated event types (Art. 12(2)(a)–(c), plus Annex III 1(a) minimum fields where applicable). This proposal builds an anomaly-detection and alerting layer on top of existing logs rather than ensuring the foundational logging architecture actually records the compliance-relevant events in the required granularity, format, and lifetime retention. From my vantage point, committing scarce implementation resources to a secondary analysis tool before verifying (and remediating, if needed) that the underlying log schema satisfies the letter of Article 12 would misallocate the very resources needed for institutional survival and audit readiness.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 35\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2503 from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal is: \"High-Risk Event Detection and Alerting Framework\" - a real-time monitoring tool that scans system logs for patterns indicative of risks in Article 12(2), focusing on detecting substantial modifications or performance failures using anomaly detection to flag drift and operational errors automatically. It sits atop existing logging infrastructure.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance perspective:\n\n**Article 12 Analysis:**\n- Article 12(1) requires high-risk AI systems to \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" This is about LOGGING capability - the system must be able to record events.\n- Article 12(2) requires logging capabilities to enable recording of events relevant to: (a) identifying situations that may present a risk or substantial modification, (b) facilitating post-market monitoring (Article 72), and (c) monitoring operation (Article 26(5)).\n- Article 12(3) specifies minimum logging for Annex III point 1(a) systems.\n\n**Key distinction:** Article 12 is about RECORD-KEEPING - the system must be able to LOG events automatically. It is NOT a mandate to build an active anomaly detection or alerting framework. The regulation says the system shall \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs).\" This means:\n- The logging capability must exist in the technical architecture\n- Logs must capture specific event types relevant to traceability, post-market monitoring, and operational monitoring\n- For Annex III 1(a) systems, there are specific minimum fields\n\n**Assessment of P2503:**\n\nThe proposal proposes building a \"lightweight analysis layer using anomaly detection\" that sits atop existing logging infrastructure. This goes BEYOND what Article 12 requires. Article 12 requires:\n1. Automatic recording capability (passive logging)\n2. Specific event types must be captured\n3. For certain systems, specific minimum data fields\n\nThe proposal reframes this as \"active tool for improving prototype quality\" and \"anomaly detection to flag drift.\" While this might have operational value, from a compliance standpoint:\n\n**Arguments for approval (compliance perspective):**\n- It does address Article 12(2)(a) - identifying situations that may present risk or substantial modification. Having automated detection could help ensure you're actually catching these events in logs.\n- Article 12(2)(b) mentions facilitating post-market monitoring (Article 72). An alerting system could support this.\n- It builds on existing logging infrastructure, which is good - it doesn't reinvent the wheel.\n- From a regulatory readiness standpoint, having an active detection layer demonstrates to authorities that you're not just passively storing logs but actively ensuring compliance-relevant events are captured and flagged.\n\n**Arguments for rejection (compliance perspective):**\n- Article 12 is fundamentally about RECORDING CAPABILITY, not about ANALYSIS or ALERTING. The regulation says the system must \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events.\" It does NOT say you need an anomaly detection layer on top.\n- The core compliance obligation is: (1) build logging capability into the system architecture, (2) ensure it captures the right event types, (3) keep records available throughout lifetime. This proposal doesn't directly address these core obligations - it builds something ON TOP of them assuming they already exist.\n- \"Sits atop our existing logging infrastructure\" - but what if the existing infrastructure doesn't capture the specific events required by Article 12(2)(a), (b), (c)? The anomaly detection layer is useless if the underlying logs don't contain the right data. This proposal seems to assume compliance with the logging requirement and adds value on top, rather than ensuring the core compliance obligation is met.\n- From a resource allocation standpoint, building an anomaly detection system before ensuring your logging architecture actually meets Article 12's specific requirements (especially the Annex III 1(a) minimum fields if applicable) would be misallocated resources.\n- The \"prototype quality improvement\" framing suggests this is being positioned as an R&D tool rather than a compliance deliverable, which dilutes its regulatory focus.\n- Anomaly detection for \"drift and operational errors\" is broader than what Article 12 requires. Article 12(2)(a) specifically mentions \"situations that may result in the high-risk AI system presenting a risk within the meaning of Article 79(1) or in a substantial modification.\" The scope needs to be precise for compliance.\n- There's no mention of data retention, accessibility for authorities, format requirements, or the \"lifetime\" aspect required by Article 12.\n\n**My decision:** From my compliance vantage point, this proposal is well-intentioned and has some tangential value, but it does NOT directly address the core obligations of Article 12. It's a value-add layer that assumes the foundational compliance work (building proper logging capability with the right event types) is already done. As a Compliance Officer whose job is institutional survival and regulatory compliance, I need to ensure the FLOOR is met first before investing in enhancements. This proposal, while not harmful, diverts resources from what might be the more urgent task of ensuring the logging architecture itself complies.\n\nHowever, I should also consider: does having this system help demonstrate compliance? In a regulatory audit, showing you have active monitoring on top of your logs could be seen favorably. But it's not required, and investing scarce resources in it before confirming the base requirement is met would be a misallocation.\n\nI'll reject this. It's an enhancement that presupposes the core obligation is already satisfied, and from my vantage point, I need to ensure the core obligation (proper logging capability capturing the right events) is the priority. The promise score should reflect that while it has some tangential compliance value, it's not what I would prioritize with scarce resources for meeting Article 12 obligations.\n\nPromise: maybe around 30-40. It's not irrelevant to compliance at all - post-market monitoring support and risk identification are relevant. But it's the wrong shape of investment from my perspective. The core obligation is recording capability, not analysis on top. I'll say 35.\n",
