        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2677\",\n      \"rationale\": \"A single sidecar-level middleware at our existing container orchestration layer is the most resource-efficient way to satisfy Article 12's universal logging mandate: one bounded implementation cost replaces per-prototype custom logging, it reuses infrastructure we already operate, and by decoupling compliance capture from core model code it preserves the engineer-hours that fund actual research iteration. The main residual risk is that a 'regardless of architecture' generic layer may still need per-system configuration to meet the granular field-level requirements in Article 12(3) for Annex III categories, but as an extensible foundation rather than a final specification, the architectural bet is sound and the funding profile is predictable.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 84\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Lab Director concerned with resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal is \"Unified Compliance Logging Middleware\" — a sidecar proxy in the container orchestration layer that captures event timestamps and system state across all high-risk AI prototypes, creating a consistent log format without modifying core research code.\n\nLet me think about this from my specific vantage points:\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency:**\n- A single unified middleware serving all high-risk AI prototypes is inherently efficient — one implementation serves many systems rather than building custom logging per prototype. This is the kind of \"build once, deploy everywhere\" approach that maximizes resource leverage.\n- The sidecar proxy pattern means it doesn't require modifying core research code, which reduces integration friction and the number of engineer-hours needed per project.\n- However, there's a question: does this actually cover what Article 12 requires? Article 12(2) requires logging events relevant to (a) identifying risk situations/substantial modifications, (b) facilitating post-market monitoring, and (c) monitoring operations under Article 26(5). A generic \"event timestamps and system state\" capture might be necessary but not sufficient. For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), specific data points are required (period of use, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification). A generic middleware might not capture those domain-specific fields without per-system configuration.\n- Still, as a foundational layer that handles the common elements (timestamps, system state, inputs/outputs) while allowing per-system extensions, it's a reasonable architectural choice.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- The decoupling argument is strong: by making compliance an infrastructure concern rather than a per-research-project concern, you free up scientist/engineer time for actual research. This directly addresses the tension between regulatory burden and scientific productivity.\n- Funding a one-time middleware investment is more predictable and bounded than funding ongoing custom logging development across every new prototype. From a budget planning perspective, this is attractive.\n- The risk: if the middleware is too rigid or too generic, it could become a bottleneck or require costly modifications later. But as a \"sidecar\" that intercepts without modifying core code, it's designed to be swappable/upgradable independently.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Implementing this at the container orchestration layer means leveraging infrastructure we likely already have (Kubernetes or similar). This is efficient — we're not building new compute clusters or new deployment pipelines, just adding a sidecar container to existing orchestration.\n- It uses existing patterns (sidecar proxy) that are well-understood in our engineering stack, reducing learning curve and implementation risk.\n- The \"without modifying core research code\" aspect means we don't have to refactor existing prototypes to comply — we're using what's already there more effectively.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- Does it actually meet the specific requirements of Article 12(3) for Annex III 1(a) systems? Those require very specific data fields. A generic middleware might need significant per-system configuration, which could erode the \"one implementation\" efficiency argument.\n- \"Regardless of the specific model architecture\" is a strong claim. Some architectures may not easily expose inputs/outputs at a proxy level (e.g., streaming inference, multi-stage pipelines). But this is an engineering detail that can be addressed in implementation.\n- The proposal frames it as preserving \"prototyping velocity,\" which from my vantage point is exactly the right framing — compliance shouldn't slow down research iteration.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a well-conceived infrastructure-level solution that aligns strongly with resource efficiency (one build, many uses), leverages existing orchestration infrastructure, and decouples regulatory overhead from scientific work. The main risk is whether it can handle the domain-specific logging requirements of Annex III categories without becoming so configurable per-system that it loses its \"unified\" character. But as a foundational layer with extension points, this is the right architectural bet.\n\nFrom my vantage point, I'd approve this. It's efficient, leverages existing infrastructure, and frees research resources. My promise score would be high — maybe 82-85. It's very promising but not perfect because the generic nature might need per-system extensions that add complexity, and \"event timestamps and system state\" alone may not satisfy all of Article 12's specific requirements without additional configuration work. But the architecture is sound and the resource-allocation logic is strong.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 83.\n",
