ption to ongoing scientific work.\n- The \"gold standard of scientific reproducibility\" framing goes beyond strict compliance but is a natural byproduct — and it's essentially free once the system is built, so it doesn't add resource cost.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This is where I'm most positive. Hooking into existing training loops means we leverage what we already have rather than building parallel systems. It's an additive layer, not a replacement.\n- The risk: if our training loops are poorly standardized across projects, the \"unified API\" might become a complex integration project that takes much longer than expected and ties up engineering resources.\n- Also: \"all high-risk projects\" — we need to scope this carefully. Starting with the most active/high-risk projects makes sense rather than trying to be comprehensive from day one.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal is somewhat generic in its description. It doesn't specify what happens when a project uses an unusual framework or custom training code that doesn't fit the \"standard\" hooks.\n- No mention of storage/retention strategy, which Article 12 implies (lifetime logging). Storing logs for the full lifetime of a system could become expensive at scale.\n- No mention of who maintains this infrastructure long-term — it's an ongoing cost, not just a one-time build.\n- The scope \"across all high-risk projects\" without phasing could be a resource trap.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThe direction is right and aligns well with my priorities: minimal researcher overhead (resource efficiency), hooks into existing infrastructure rather than replacing it (infrastructure reuse), and addresses a hard compliance requirement efficiently. The main risk is scope creep (\"all high-risk projects\") and the upfront engineering cost if our stack is heterogeneous. But fundamentally, this is the kind of proposal that makes sense — you need to do the logging anyway, and automating it through existing infrastructure is the most efficient path.\n\nI'd approve this, but with moderate-to-high promise. It's aligned with my priorities but the execution risk (heterogeneous infra, scope) keeps it from being a 90+. I'd say around 72-78. Let me settle on 74 — strong alignment with resource efficiency and infrastructure reuse, but the \"across all high-risk projects\" scope and lack of phasing strategy give me pause on execution cost.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The core insight is correct: automate logging into existing loops = minimal researcher disruption + compliance satisfied. This is exactly the kind of \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\" I want to see. The fact that it's a one-time build with ongoing marginal cost near zero for each additional logged event makes it resource-efficient at scale. My concerns about scope are valid but manageable — they're implementation details, not fundamental flaws in the approach.\n\nI'll go with approve and 76.\n",
