n verification). A \"research initiative\" to develop ML tools to analyze logs is not the same as implementing the mandatory logging infrastructure itself.\n- There's a risk here that this proposal could be misread as substituting advanced analytics for basic compliance. The minimum requirements in Article 12(3) are prescriptive - they don't say \"develop an AI system to interpret your logs.\" They say record specific data points. A research project might delay or distract from meeting these baseline obligations.\n- However, the proposal says it's a layer ON TOP of logging (\"interpret system logs\"), not a replacement for logging itself. So it doesn't seem to displace basic compliance.\n- The \"market-leading safety feature\" language is appealing from reputation standpoint but feels like marketing speak layered over what should be a straightforward compliance implementation. As Compliance Officer, I'm less interested in the commercial framing and more in whether this strengthens our actual compliance posture.\n- The ML-based approach introduces its own complexity: if the diagnostic layer itself has errors or misses anomalies, could that create additional liability? Would it need to be validated? This adds a layer of regulatory surface area (potentially subject to other AI Act requirements if deployed as part of a high-risk system).\n\n**Net assessment:**\nThe proposal is directionally aligned with Article 12's purpose - it strengthens traceability, supports risk identification (Art. 79(1)), and aids post-market monitoring (Art. 72). It goes beyond the minimum but in the right direction. However:\n- It's a \"research initiative\" which means uncertain timeline and deliverables. For compliance, I need certainty that mandatory logging is in place by the relevant deadline.\n- The ML component introduces new risk surface that would itself need to be managed under the Act.\n- The commercial framing (\"market-leading safety feature\") suggests this might be prioritized for market positioning rather than compliance sufficiency.\n\nBut on balance: it does NOT conflict with Article 12. It enhances our ability to meet Article 12(2)(a) and (b). It's additive, not substitutive. The risk is opportunity cost - are we spending resources here instead of nailing the basics? But since I'm judging this proposal on its own merits (not comparing), it does represent a genuine compliance enhancement that also supports market reputation.\n\nI think this clears my bar but isn't a slam-dunk 100 because:\n- The \"research initiative\" framing means uncertain delivery timeline vs. hard regulatory deadlines\n- ML-based interpretation adds complexity and its own compliance surface\n- It's more of an enhancement than addressing the core mandatory requirement\n- But it IS aligned with the direction and purpose of Article 12\n\nI'll approve it but give a moderate-to-good promise score. Maybe 65-70. It's genuinely useful for our compliance posture, supports the traceability goals of Recital 71, and strengthens our ability to demonstrate risk identification under Art 12(2)(a). But the research framing and ML complexity introduce uncertainty that keeps it from being a clean \"must-do.\"\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 68.\n",
