ean.\n- \"Institutional survival\" angle: Article 12 violations in the EU AI Act carry significant penalties (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices, up to €15M or 3% for other violations including high-risk system requirements). A systemic failure in logging could cascade across multiple deployed systems.\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n- The proposal is scoped to \"prototype designated as high-risk\" — this is correct and appropriate. Not all R&D output will be high-risk, so the gate only triggers where needed.\n- It's a verification tool, not a logging implementation itself. It checks whether logging works but doesn't build the logging. This is fine — it's a complement to engineering work, not a substitute. However, if the logging isn't built, this suite will just flag failures without solving them. But that's still valuable as early warning.\n- The \"checklist of Article 12 requirements\" needs to be carefully maintained as the regulation evolves (implementing acts, harmonised standards, guidance from AI Office). This is a maintenance burden but manageable.\n- For systems where logging is deeply embedded in architecture (e.g., the reference database check in 12(3)(b), or identification of natural persons in verification per Article 14(5) referenced in 12(3)(d)), automated probes may have limits — some logging requires understanding business context. But even partial automation is better than nothing.\n- The CI/CD gate concept is well-aligned with modern DevOps and doesn't require exotic infrastructure.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a high-value, focused compliance control that directly maps to Article 12 obligations. It reduces the probability of non-compliance reaching market, creates evidence for auditors, and protects institutional reputation. The implementation cost is moderate (integration testing + probe development) relative to the risk it mitigates. I would strongly approve this.\n\nPromise score: From a compliance perspective, this is ve