any labs produce scientifically excellent work that then stalls because compliance wasn't considered until the end.\n\nConcerns from my vantage point:\n- This is described as a \"new institutional workflow\" — that implies process overhead. However, it's framed as integrating into *existing* peer reviews (mandatory checkpoints during internal peer reviews), which means it leverages existing infrastructure rather than building new processes from scratch.\n- It only applies to projects \"identified as high-risk\" — so it doesn't impose blanket overhead on all research. This is resource-efficient scoping.\n- The proposal is methodological/workflow in nature, not a massive capital expenditure. Implementing traceability checkpoints in peer reviews requires process design and some training, but not major infrastructure buildout.\n- \"Mandatory\" could be seen as adding friction to scientific work, but the argument that it prevents costly technical debt is compelling from a resource-efficiency standpoint. The cost of retrofitting logging into mature AI systems can be orders of magnitude higher than designing it in from the start.\n\nOverall assessment: This proposal directly addresses my domain concerns. It's about efficient use of existing infrastructure (peer reviews), preventing waste (technical debt, rework), and ensuring that limited funding goes toward projects that can actually reach market rather than stalling at compliance. The scope is appropriately limited to high-risk projects only. It's a process/workflow change, not a capital-intensive buildout. The resource commitment is moderate (process design, training) but the ROI in avoided rework costs is significant.\n\nI would lean toward approving this. It's a proportionate response that leverages existing structures and prevents downstream waste. From a pure resource-efficiency standpoint, designing compliance into the architecture early is strictly cheaper than bolting it on later. The \"mandatory\" aspect within 