onsistent event schemas, which makes debugging, auditing, and post-market monitoring easier. Better structured data than ad-hoc per-project logging.\n- However, there's a risk: if the middleware is too rigid or has performance overhead, it could slow down prototyping iterations. But \"plug-and-play\" suggests minimal integration friction.\n- The proposal acknowledges the key tension: compliance shouldn't \"slow down individual research teams.\" This shows awareness of the practical constraint.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- \"Core infrastructure team will develop\" — this is a centralized build. There's a risk of becoming a bottleneck if the infra team can't keep up with diverse project needs. But for a lab-scale operation, one infra team maintaining a logging library is reasonable.\n- The scope is appropriately bounded: it's a middleware/library, not a full platform. It doesn't overreach into data storage, analytics dashboards, or regulatory reporting — just the recording layer. This makes it technically tractable.\n- \"Plug-and-play\" is somewhat aspirational but achievable if the API surface is well-designed (a few decorators/context managers for Python pipelines).\n\n**Verdict:**\nThis is a solid, technically viable proposal that directly serves my domain of concern. It's not a novel research idea — it's infrastructure engineering applied to a compliance requirement, which is exactly what should be done. The technical path is clear, the scope is bounded, and it directly accelerates prototyping by removing per-project logging boilerplate. I would approve this with high confidence.\n\nPromise score: This is very promising from my vantage point. It's technically straightforward (not cutting-edge R&D, just solid engineering), well-scoped, and directly addresses a practical pain point that affects prototyping speed. I'd put it around 82-88. It's not a 95+ because there are always integration challenges across heterogeneous stacks, and \"plug-and-play\" can