ese specifics or over-log unnecessarily.\n- \"Technical viability for regulatory audits\" - this is a bit vague. Compliance isn't just about having logs; it's about the content being appropriate, complete, and retrievable in a form that demonstrates compliance to authorities (Article 12(2) requires traceability \"appropriate to the intended purpose\").\n- The proposal doesn't address data retention periods, access controls for different stakeholders, or how logs integrate with the broader technical documentation requirements of Article 11/Recital 71.\n- It's an implementation detail that is necessary but not sufficient. It addresses the \"technical allow\" part of Art 12(1) but doesn't address what events are \"relevant\" per Art 12(2)(a)-(c).\n\n**From my compliance perspective:**\nThis is a foundational and necessary component. Without automated logging, we simply cannot comply with Article 12(1). It's not optional - it's a \"shall.\" Building this in-house as a standardized layer makes sense for institutional consistency and audit readiness. The risk of NOT doing this is direct regulatory non-compliance, potential fines (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover under Article 99), and inability to demonstrate compliance to notified bodies or market surveillance authorities.\n\nHowever, the proposal as stated is somewhat thin - it captures the mechanism but not the full content requirements. But that's okay for a \"pursue\" decision; we can flesh out specifications during implementation. The core idea is sound and directly maps to a mandatory regulatory requirement.\n\nI'd approve this. It addresses a hard \"shall\" requirement, standardizes approach (good for audit), reduces human error risk, and is necessary for institutional survival under the Act. But it's not a 100 because the proposal as written doesn't fully address the specific content requirements of Art 12(3) or the traceability purpose of Art 12(2). I'd give it maybe an 82-85.\n\nLet me settle on my assessmen