nt:**\n- Monthly mandatory workshops are a recurring resource drain. Every hour in a workshop is an hour not spent on research, coding, or deployment work. At scale across the lab, this adds up significantly. The opportunity cost is real.\n- \"Tied to project milestone approvals\" — this could create bottlenecks. If researchers must complete compliance training before milestones are approved, it could slow down scientific progress and delay deliverables that funding agreements require. This risks creating tension between scientific ambition and the pace of funded work.\n- The framing of \"institutional survival depends on...\" is somewhat alarmist but also reflects reality — without compliance, the lab cannot operate in the EU market. So this isn't purely a cost; it's an existential necessity.\n- However, from a resource-allocation perspective: is a *certification program* the most efficient way to ensure traceability? It's a soft intervention (training) rather than embedding traceability into tooling and workflows. The actual engineering work of implementing automatic logging (Article 12(1)) still needs to happen. Training alone doesn't build the systems; it just makes people aware they need them.\n- The program doesn't directly address the technical infrastructure needed for logging capabilities. It's a human capital investment, not an infrastructure investment. From my perspective on \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure,\" this is somewhat misaligned — I'd want to see the engineering resources directed toward building the logging systems, with training as a supporting element rather than the centerpiece.\n- That said, if researchers don't understand WHY they need logs or what documentation standards apply, the engineering investments in logging tools will be underutilized or poorly designed. So there's a reasonable case that workforce competency is a prerequisite for effective use of infrastructure.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a reasonable, rela