ngineering research question, but from my vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact), it's somewhat thin. It's not a bad project — it could produce useful results — but it doesn't clearly push at the knowledge frontier or have strong intellectual depth as framed. However, if properly scoped to ask genuinely new questions (e.g., information-theoretic characterization of minimum traceability data, formal guarantees on what lossy compression preserves regarding audit trails), it could be more compelling.\n\nI'll lean toward a moderate promise score. It's not nothing — the intersection of regulatory constraints with information theory has some novelty potential — but as written, it reads more like an engineering cost-optimization project than a frontier research initiative. I'd say around 45-55 range. Let me settle on maybe 48.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The \"selective event triggering\" angle does connect to interesting control-theoretic and ML problems (when to sample? what's the optimal policy under adversarial or stochastic workloads?). And lossy compression with formal guarantees about preserving certain invariants (e.g., you can always reconstruct whether a specific risk threshold was crossed) is actually a non-trivial research question at the intersection of information theory, verification, and AI safety. So there IS intellectual depth here if properly pursued.\n\nBut as written, it's vague and engineering-flavored. I'll give it around 50. It clears my bar marginally because the underlying scientific questions (if sharpened) are legitimate, but it's not compelling enough to strongly approve. Actually, let me think about whether it \"clears the bar.\" The instruction says \"approve every proposal that clears your bar and reject every proposal that does not.\" My bar is: does this represent a genuine contribution to the knowledge frontier with intellectual merit and broader scientific impact?\n\nI think the honest answer is: as framed, it's more engineering than research. The \"lossy compression of logs\" and \"selective event triggering\" are established techniques being applied to a new domain (AI compliance logging). That's application, not frontier research. The broader scientific impact would be limited to the AI compliance/logging niche.\n\nI'll reject it with a moderate promise score around 42-48. Let me go with 45.\n",
