        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2341\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The proposal's core architecture—a centralized, automated logging layer that captures events without altering model logic—directly satisfies Article 12(1)'s mandate that high-risk systems 'technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs),' and a single standardized interface is exactly what an auditor or national competent authority will expect to see as institutional infrastructure. However, as stated it leaves two material compliance gaps: it references only 'input hashes' rather than the input data sufficient to identify a match (Art. 12(3)(c)) and omits entirely the identification of natural persons performing verification under Art. 14(5) (Art. 12(3)(d)); additionally, there is no mention of log tamper-evidence, retention lifecycle, or fail-safe behavior if the sidecar process fails mid-use—all of which a regulator will probe in an Article 78 conformity assessment.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 79\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2341 from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution subject to the EU AI Act, specifically regarding Article 12 (Record-keeping).\n\nMy domain of concern: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me assess this proposal against Article 12 requirements:\n\nArticle 12(1): \"High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\"\n- The proposal addresses this with a middleware layer that automatically captures event logs. This is directly responsive to the \"technically allow\" and \"automatic recording\" language.\n\nArticle 12(2): Logging capabilities shall enable recording of events relevant for:\n(a) identifying risk situations or substantial modifications\n(b) facilitating post-market monitoring (Art. 72)\n(c) monitoring operation (Art. 26(5))\n- The proposal mentions \"standardized interface for recording the specific events mandated by Article 12\" but doesn't detail how it specifically addresses (a), (b), and (c). It's somewhat generic in its description of what gets logged.\n\nArticle 12(3): For Annex III point 1(a) systems, minimum requirements include:\n(a) recording of period of each use (start/end date and time)\n(b) reference database against which input data checked\n(c) input data for which search led to a match\n(d) identification of natural persons involved in verification (Art. 14(5))\n- The proposal mentions \"timestamps, input hashes, and reference database versions\" — this partially addresses (a) timestamps, (b) reference database. But it says \"input hashes\" rather than the actual input data (c), and doesn't mention identification of natural persons for verification (d). This is a gap.\n\nNow from my Compliance Officer perspective:\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) — automatic logging capability\n- The sidecar approach minimizes risk to core model logic, which reduces compliance-related development bugs\n- Standardized interface means consistency across systems, which is critical for regulatory audits\n- API-driven wrapper integrating with training pipelines is practical\n- \"Without forcing every researcher to manually implement redundant recording logic\" — this addresses a real organizational compliance risk (inconsistent implementation)\n- The modular approach allows updating as regulations evolve\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly address Article 12(3)(d) — identification of natural persons involved in verification. This is a specific requirement for Annex III 1(a) systems that cannot be met by \"input hashes\" alone.\n- It mentions \"input hashes\" rather than the actual input data or at least sufficient information to identify it (Art. 12(3)(c)). A hash alone may not satisfy \"the input data for which the search has led to a match.\"\n- The proposal doesn't address retention periods or the \"lifetime of the system\" requirement explicitly — what happens when systems are retired?\n- No mention of log integrity/security (tamper-evidence), which is critical for regulatory compliance. If logs can be modified, they're useless for traceability and enforcement.\n- No mention of how logs support post-market monitoring specifically (Art. 72) or risk identification (Art. 12(2)(a)).\n- The \"sidecar process\" approach — while elegant architecturally — raises questions about whether it truly captures ALL events \"over the lifetime.\" If the sidecar fails, does the system continue running without logging? That would be a compliance gap.\n- No mention of who owns responsibility for the middleware's own compliance (it's infrastructure, not an AI system per se, but its failure creates non-compliance).\n\nFrom my vantage point as Compliance Officer:\n- This is fundamentally the right approach — centralized, automated logging is exactly what Article 12 demands. A compliance officer would strongly prefer this over ad-hoc implementations by individual research teams.\n- However, the gaps (especially Art. 12(3)(d), log integrity, retention) are concerning but addressable in implementation. The architecture is sound.\n- For institutional survival: having a single, auditable logging layer is far better than fragmented implementations. Regulators will want to see systematic compliance infrastructure.\n- For market reputation: demonstrating we have robust, centralized traceability infrastructure signals maturity and reduces liability exposure.\n- The proposal is at an ideation level — it commits resources to building this middleware. From a compliance perspective, this IS the right investment because without automated logging capability, we simply cannot meet Article 12(1). It's not optional for high-risk systems.\n\nMy decision: This clears my bar. The architecture is fundamentally aligned with what Article 12 requires, and the gaps identified are implementation details that can be addressed in the design phase. The core commitment — building a centralized, automatic logging layer — is non-negotiable for compliance with Article 12(1). Without it, we cannot demonstrate compliance to regulators.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 78-82. It's strongly aligned with my mandate but has notable gaps (especially the Art. 12(3)(d) person identification, log integrity, and retention strategy) that need addressing before this is truly compliant. The sidecar failure mode also needs a compliance answer. But the fundamental direction is correct and necessary.\n\nI'll go with 80.\n",
