hnical documentation with operational logging.\n- Article 12(2) requires specific types of logs: situations presenting risk, post-market monitoring data, and operation monitoring. The proposal doesn't explicitly address these three categories.\n- Article 12(3) for biometric identification systems (Annex III point 1(a)) has very specific minimum logging requirements. The proposal doesn't mention this at all.\n- \"Guarantees the lab is audit-ready\" - no system can truly \"guarantee\" anything; this overstates what an automation pipeline can deliver. There will still be gaps, edge cases, and interpretation questions from regulators.\n- \"Eliminates human error\" - also overstated. Automation introduces its own class of errors (configuration issues, mapping errors between version control events and documentation fields, etc.)\n- The scope seems very broad (\"transforms raw R&D data into comprehensible information\") - this is a massive engineering undertaking that could take years to build properly, and the proposal doesn't address phasing or minimum viable compliance.\n- However, as a compliance matter, having an automated pipeline IS the right direction. Manual documentation that falls out of sync with actual system changes is a major regulatory risk.\n\nFrom my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):\n- This is clearly aligned with what I need to demonstrate compliance\n- The automation angle reduces ongoing compliance costs and human error risk\n- However, the proposal as stated has gaps regarding Article 12's specific logging requirements vs. broader technical documentation\n- The overconfident language (\"guarantees,\" \"eliminates\") suggests it hasn't been stress-tested against actual regulatory scrutiny\n- Still, the core direction is correct and important\n\nAs a Compliance Officer, I would want to approve this because:\n1. It directly supports Article 12 compliance\n2. Audit-readiness is existential for institutional