cture:**\n- Integrating into \"existing prototype containers\" means leveraging infrastructure the lab already has rather than building parallel systems. This is efficient.\n- A shared internal library is a natural fit for how labs typically share code/tools (internal GitHub repos, container registries, etc.)\n- The standardized log format across projects also aids in later auditability and reduces fragmentation — you don't have to parse 15 different logging formats during an audit.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Every model execution\" is broad. In R&D, there are many exploratory runs that will never become high-risk systems. Over-capturing could generate massive log storage costs. But the proposal says \"high-risk prototypes,\" which narrows scope somewhat. Still, in practice, distinguishing at prototype stage whether something will be \"high-risk\" can be uncertain.\n- The middleware needs to be maintained as models change (new architectures, new modalities). This is a non-trivial ongoing cost. But it's still far cheaper than per-project logging development.\n- Integration into containers assumes a containerized workflow. If some research isn't containerized yet, there's migration overhead. But most modern ML R&D labs are largely containerized.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a strong, pragmatic proposal from my vantage point. It:\n1. Solves a real compliance requirement (Article 12(1) mandates automatic recording) with minimal disruption to research workflows\n2. Is built once and reused many times (high resource efficiency)\n3. Protects researcher time for actual science (protects ambition under funding constraints)\n4. Leverages existing container infrastructure rather than building new parallel systems\n5. Has bounded, predictable costs\n\nThe main risk is scope creep in \"every model execution\" vs. only those that will actually be high-risk, but the proposal does say \"high-risk prototypes\" which is appropriate scoping. The shared library approach is 