grate with existing model pipelines, authentication systems, and possibly multiple AI system instances. This adds integration complexity.\n- The \"ground truth data to refine models' alignment\" part is a research/ML concern that's somewhat orthogonal to the logging requirement. It expands scope significantly — you'd need data pipelines for structuring feedback, versioning against model snapshots, etc.\n- The proposal conflates two things: (1) meeting Article 12(3)(d) compliance (logging who verified what and when), which is a straightforward engineering task; and (2) building a research-grade feedback loop for model alignment refinement, which is a much larger ML infrastructure project.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- Scope creep: The proposal frames itself around Article 14(5)/Article 12(3)(d) compliance but then pivots to \"provides high-quality ground truth data to refine our models' alignment\" — that's a much bigger engineering project than what the regulation requires.\n- The \"dedicated dashboard\" approach vs. instrumenting existing workflows: Building a separate interface adds friction to human expert workflows, which could reduce adoption and data quality. A lightweight integration into existing tools might be faster to prototype and more effective.\n- Technical risk is low for the basic logging requirement but high for the alignment-refinement pipeline if that's actually what's being committed to.\n- The proposal doesn't specify how \"qualitative reasoning\" will be captured in a technically reproducible way — free text? Structured taxonomies? Audio recordings? This ambiguity makes it hard to scope and prototype.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe basic compliance logging (who verified, when, what was corrected) is technically trivial and easy to prototype. The proposal as written bundles this with a much more ambitious model-alignment data pipeline that's a different order of magnitude in engineering effort. From a pure prototyping speed perspective, 