nical challenges that would introduce bugs or edge cases beyond what any competent dev team handles daily.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Standardized\" implies it needs to work across potentially multiple verification workflows, which could add complexity if there's no existing common data model for system outputs. But this is a manageable design challenge, not a viability risk.\n- The proposal mentions linking \"specific system outputs\" to verifiers - this requires that the system outputs are already structured and queryable, which is a reasonable prerequisite but adds integration work. Still straightforward.\n- One minor concern: \"dedicated user interface\" implies building new UI rather than integrating into existing verification workflows. This could be faster (greenfield) or slower (need to migrate users from whatever they currently use). But from a prototyping standpoint, a focused new dashboard is actually cleaner to build quickly.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is technically very viable. It's a well-scoped, bounded piece of work using mature technology patterns. The prototype can be built quickly and iterated on with low risk. The connection to the specific regulatory requirement (Article 12(3)(d)) is direct and clean - no over-engineering or scope creep apparent in the proposal text.\n\nFrom my vantage point, this clears the bar easily. It's technically sound, well-scoped, quick to prototype, and uses proven patterns. I'd approve it with a high promise score because there's very little technical risk and the path from prototype to production is short and predictable.\n\nI'll give it around 85-90. The only reason not higher is that \"standardized\" across potentially multiple systems could introduce some integration complexity, and building a \"dedicated\" UI means it exists as a separate artifact that needs its own maintenance. But these are minor concerns for what is fundamentally a low-risk, high-viability technical proposal.\n",
