abase state. The content-addressable storage with cryptographic hashing provides immutability, which is crucial for compliance evidence integrity (you can't tamper with logs).\n\nFrom a compliance perspective:\n- This is a strong, well-targeted technical proposal that directly addresses specific regulatory requirements in Article 12(3)(b) and partially (c).\n- The immutable nature (cryptographic hashing, content-addressable storage) ensures log integrity, which is critical for demonstrating compliance to regulators. If a regulator asks \"what was the state of your reference database when this prediction was made?\", this system provides an auditable answer.\n- It supports traceability (Recital 71 emphasizes this).\n- The \"lifetime\" requirement in Article 12(1) is supported by immutability - logs can't be altered or deleted, ensuring they persist throughout the system's lifetime.\n- From a market reputation standpoint, having a robust, auditable data lineage system demonstrates serious commitment to compliance and could be a competitive differentiator.\n\nConcerns:\n- It specifically addresses Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification). If the lab develops other high-risk AI systems beyond this category, this proposal alone won't cover all of Article 12(1) and 12(2). However, the proposal is scoped to what it does address.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention logging start/end times (12(3)(a)) or identification of natural persons for verification (12(3)(d)). But those are separate sub-requirements that could be handled by other systems/proposals.\n- \"Content-addressable storage\" is a well-understood technology (like what's used in git, IPFS, etc.) so implementation risk is moderate.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a solid, narrowly-scoped proposal that directly maps to specific regulatory text. From a compliance officer's perspective, this is exactly the kind of targeted, evidence-generating infrastructure you want. It provides auditable proof o