ion, verifier identity).\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The promise to \"minimize friction for developers\" is attractive. If it works, researchers don't need to write logging boilerplate.\n- However, building a truly universal framework that handles all the Article 12 requirements across diverse architectures is itself a massive engineering project. This could become a bottleneck rather than an accelerator.\n- The risk: we spend 6-12 months building this \"unified\" framework, and it either doesn't cover edge cases or becomes so complex that integrating with new prototype types takes as long as writing custom logging would have.\n- For prototyping speed specifically, a lightweight per-project logging wrapper might get us to compliance faster than waiting for a grand unified framework.\n\n**What's good:**\n- The direction is right - standardizing logging is better than each project reinventing the wheel.\n- Immutable storage addresses traceability requirements well.\n- Reducing developer burden is aligned with prototyping velocity.\n- Sidecar pattern is clean separation of concerns.\n\n**What's concerning from my vantage point:**\n- \"Regardless of specific model architecture\" is a very strong technical claim that may not hold up in practice.\n- A single library covering ALL high-risk prototypes is a large scope that risks becoming a project of its own, competing for the same engineering resources needed to build actual AI systems.\n- The Annex III 1(a) specific requirements (reference database, input data matching, verifier identity) are domain-specific and likely require hooks beyond simple API interception.\n- \"Without requiring researchers to write custom logging code\" - in practice, you'll always need project-specific configuration for what constitutes a meaningful event. The framework can standardize the plumbing but not eliminate all integration work.\n\n**Decision:** This is directionally sound but has significant technical scope risk. From my vantage point on prototyping speed and technical viability:\n- The core concept (standardized logging middleware) is viable and would help.\n- But the \"unified across all architectures\" claim stretches credibility for a first implementation.\n- It could accelerate compliance IF scoped realistically, but as stated it's an ambitious platform project that might slow us down.\n\nI think this clears my bar with reservations. The core pattern (sidecar + immutable log) is technically sound and would genuinely reduce per-project friction. The \"unified across all architectures\" scope is the risk, but even a first version covering 70% of cases (the common inference API pattern) would be valuable for prototyping speed. I'll approve it but with a moderate promise score reflecting the scoping risk.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. My bar is: does this clear my threshold for committing scarce implementation resources? \n\nThe proposal as stated is a \"unified\" framework - that's a big engineering commitment. If we commit to building this, it's 6+ months of work before it delivers value. During that time, individual projects can't rely on it and still need their own logging (or don't comply). The question is whether the unified approach genuinely saves more engineering effort than per-project lightweight solutions would.\n\nFor prototyping speed specifically: if I'm a researcher spinning up a new high-risk prototype next month, I need working logs NOW, not in 6 months when the \"unified framework\" ships. Unless there's a phased delivery model (which isn't stated).\n\nBut counter-argument: building 10 different logging solutions is MORE total engineering effort than one shared framework. The upfront investment pays off at scale. And the sidecar pattern means once it exists, onboarding new projects is near-zero friction.\n\nI'll approve it - the technical approach is sound, the direction is right for our portfolio of high-risk systems, and it would genuinely reduce per-project en