        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1751\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Article 12(3)(c) mandates recording of 'the input data for which the search has led to a match' as a specific, verifiable traceability record; Recital 71 requires this information be 'comprehensible' so that notified bodies and market-surveillance authorities can actually verify compliance. Substituting cryptographic hashes or differentially-privacy-perturbed proxies for the mandated data risks producing logs that fail the very purpose of the article (traceability, post-market monitoring under Art. 72, substantial-modification detection under Art. 79(1)), and an R&D project structured around that substitution could commit scarce engineering resources to a technical path whose conclusion—'we store a hash instead of the data'—would not satisfy a regulator's inspection request. The genuine tension with GDPR data-minimisation is real, but it must be resolved first by legal counsel interpreting the interplay between Regulation 2016/679 and Article 12(3), after which engineering implements the (narrower) technically required record with appropriate access controls and retention; an open-ended research project inverts that sequence and delays concrete compliance.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 35\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the perspective of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P1751 is about \"Privacy-Enhanced Audit Log Research\" - developing secure, privacy-preserving methods for recording input data required by Article 12(3), using cryptographic hashing and differential privacy to store reference database matches without exposing sensitive raw data.\n\nLet me assess this from a compliance perspective:\n\n**Article 12 requirements:**\n- Article 12(1): Automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system - mandatory technical capability.\n- Article 12(3)(c): \"the input data for which the search has led to a match\" - this is specifically required to be recorded.\n- Article 12(3)(b): \"the reference database against which input data has been checked by the system\"\n\nThe proposal wants to use cryptographic hashing and differential privacy to store \"reference database matches without exposing sensitive raw data.\"\n\n**Compliance concerns:**\n\n1. **Article 12(3)(c) requires recording of \"the input data for which the search has led to a match.\"** This is a specific, concrete requirement. The text says \"input data\" - not a hash of it, not a privacy-transformed version. If we store only a cryptographic hash or differentially-privacy-perturbed version, can regulators verify compliance? Can auditors reconstruct what was actually input?\n\n2. **Article 12(3)(b) requires recording \"the reference database against which input data has been checked.\"** Storing just the name/identifier of the database seems straightforward, but if we're applying differential privacy to this, it could undermine traceability.\n\n3. **Recital 71 emphasizes:** \"Having comprehensible information on how high-risk AI systems have been developed and how they perform throughout their lifetime is essential to enable traceability of those systems, verify compliance with the requirements under this Regulation.\" The word \"comprehensible\" is key - a cryptographic hash or differentially privacy-perturbed record may not be \"comprehensible\" in the way the regulation envisions.\n\n4. **Regulatory verification:** When a notified body or market surveillance authority requests logs to verify compliance (Article 79, substantial modifications; Article 72 post-market monitoring), they need to actually READ and understand the logs. If we've hashed everything, can an auditor verify what happened? The purpose of logging under Article 12 is traceability - if the data is obfuscated beyond recognition by design, does it still serve the traceability function?\n\n5. **However**, there IS a legitimate compliance angle: GDPR and data protection obligations exist alongside AI Act obligations. If we're recording biometric or personal data as part of verification logs (Article 12(3)(d) mentions \"identification of natural persons\"), we DO need to balance data minimization with traceability. So the concern isn't entirely misplaced.\n\n6. **But here's the critical issue:** The proposal frames this as an R&D research project. It says \"Researchers will explore...\" This is exploratory work, not a concrete implementation plan. As compliance officer, my job is to ensure we MEET regulatory requirements. An R&D project that might produce tools in the future doesn't guarantee compliance NOW. And worse - if the research concludes that you CAN'T store raw input data due to privacy constraints, that's actually a problem because Article 12(3)(c) says you MUST record it.\n\n7. **The tension is real but the proposal mischaracterizes it.** The AI Act doesn't say \"record whatever data protection allows.\" It mandates specific records. If there's a conflict between GDPR and AI Act logging requirements, that's a legal question to be resolved through legal counsel (data minimization exceptions, legitimate interest assessments, etc.), not through an R&D project on differential privacy.\n\n8. **Risk of non-compliance:** If we implement hashing/differential privacy on our logs based on this research, and a regulator comes asking \"show me the input data for which the search led to a match\" (Article 12(3)(c)), and we say \"here's a SHA-256 hash\" or \"here's a differentially-privacy-perturbed approximation,\" that is very likely NOT compliant. The regulation requires the actual data be recorded, not a privacy-transformed proxy.\n\n9. **Market reputation angle:** The proposal claims it protects market reputation by combining compliance with \"state-of-the-art privacy engineering.\" But if this approach actually creates non-compliance (because logs aren't truly traceable), it would HURT our reputation when a regulator flags us or a data subject complaint reveals inadequate logging.\n\n10. **Institutional survival:** If we build systems based on assumptions that hashing satisfies Article 12(3)(c), and those assumptions are wrong, we face enforcement action, potential market withdrawal orders, and significant fines (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for serious violations).\n\n**My assessment as Compliance Officer:**\n- The underlying concern (balancing data protection with logging) is legitimate.\n- BUT the approach (R&D project exploring hashing/differential privacy) risks creating a false sense that compliance can be achieved through engineering rather than legal interpretation.\n- Article 12(3)(c) specifically requires \"the input data\" - not a proxy, hash, or perturbed version. The regulation's purpose (Recital 71: traceability, verifying compliance) would be undermined if the logs are unreadable to auditors by design.\n- The proper path is: legal counsel determines what level of detail Article 12(3)(c) truly requires in light of GDPR constraints; then engineering implements that specific requirement with appropriate access controls and retention policies. Not an open-ended R&D project.\n- This proposal, if approved, would consume scarce implementation resources on research that may lead to a solution that is actually NON-compliant (because the regulator wants readable traceability data), while delaying actual compliance work.\n\nHowever, I should note there IS a narrow legitimate angle: for Article 12(3)(d) \"identification of natural persons,\" you might legitimately need pseudonymization techniques. And access control on logs is standard. But the proposal specifically targets \"input data\" and \"reference database matches\" - which are the core traceability requirements.\n\nI'll reject this but give it a moderate promise score because the underlying tension is real, just the approach and scope are problematic from a compliance standpoint. Maybe 35-40? The concern is valid (data protection matters), but the solution proposed risks undermining rather than achieving compliance. It's not terrible - it shows awareness of dual obligations. But as a compliance officer, I need to ensure our logs are actually compliant first, then optimize for privacy within those bounds. An R&D exploration that might conclude \"we can't store what we're required to store\" is dangerous for institutional survival.\n\nI'll go with reject and promise 35.\n",
