therwise go to research. The question is whether the aggregate savings justify the upfront investment.\n\n3. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: The proposal specifically mentions \"hooks into existing training and inference frameworks,\" which suggests it's designed to integrate with what we already have rather than requiring a complete overhaul. This is good — it leverages existing infrastructure rather than replacing it. A unified layer that sits on top of existing systems is more efficient than forcing each team to build their own solution or requiring migration to entirely new platforms.\n\n**Assessment:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Eliminates redundant work across teams (classic efficiency gain)\n- Leverages existing frameworks rather than replacing them\n- Provides consistency, which reduces error rates and audit complexity\n- Frees research teams from compliance implementation burden\n- Scales well — as we add new high-risk pipelines, the marginal cost of logging decreases\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n- Upfront engineering investment is substantial (designing API, database schema, integration with multiple frameworks)\n- Risk of over-engineering if our portfolio of high-risk systems is small or heterogeneous\n- The middleware team becomes a bottleneck — any new system must go through their interface\n- Maintenance burden on the central team as frameworks evolve\n- If we only have 2-3 high-risk pipelines, the ROI might not be compelling vs. individual implementations\n\nFrom my perspective as Lab Director focused on resource efficiency and effective use of infrastructure: This is a strong proposal. The core insight — that compliance logging is a cross-cutting concern better served by shared infrastructure than by N redundant implementations — is sound from an allocation standpoint. The fact that it hooks into existing frameworks rather than requiring migration aligns with \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\" The productivity argument (freeing researchers from repetitive compliance work) directly supports my mandate to balance scientific ambition against the drag of regulatory overhead.\n\nThe main risk is scale: if we only have a handful of high-risk pipelines, the centralization overhead might not justify itself. But even with modest numbers (say 3-5 teams), avoiding 3-5 separate implementations and maintenance streams is efficient. And as the EU AI Act's scope broadens or our pipeline portfolio grows, this becomes more valuable.\n\nI would approve this. It's a textbook case of \"invest once in shared infrastructure to avoid N× redundant spending.\" My promise score should reflect high confidence but not absolute certainty (the scale question and maintenance burden give me some pause). I'd say around 82-85.\n",
