ty of prototyping:**\n- From my vantage point, the key question is: does this accelerate or slow down prototyping?\n- Pros: If teams don't have to build logging from scratch each time, that saves weeks per project. Centralized log management reduces debugging overhead. Standardization means compliance reviewers can rely on a known pattern.\n- Cons: Building and maintaining a universal middleware framework is itself a substantial engineering commitment. Before it's useful, it needs to be built, tested, documented, and integrated. That's a multi-quarter effort at minimum. For early-stage prototypes that are being iterated rapidly, adding a mandatory logging layer \"at the start of every project lifecycle\" adds friction. Prototyping speed is about moving fast — if the middleware isn't mature enough for edge cases, engineers will work around it or be blocked waiting for support.\n- The proposal says \"Engineers will implement this module at the start of every project lifecycle.\" This is a process mandate that could slow prototyping if the framework has gaps or bugs for new architectures not yet supported.\n\n**Key concern from my perspective:** The proposal conflates two things: (1) basic telemetry/timestamps logging (which is straightforward and where a shared library helps), and (2) the domain-specific compliance logging required by Article 12(3) (reference databases, match data, verifier IDs). A \"universal\" framework that handles only the easy part isn't sufficient for compliance. One that tries to handle all parts becomes a massive project with long lead time. The sweet spot — a thin event-emission layer plus per-domain log adapters — is more nuanced than what's described here.\n\n**Verdict:** The core idea (shared logging library) has merit and would reduce redundant work. But as stated, it's under-specified on the domain-specific requirements that actually drive compliance, and over-promises on \"universal\" applicability. From a prototyping speed standpoint, 