        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2430\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal directly operationalises the core obligation in Article 12(1) (automatic, lifetime-spanning event recording) and provides a systemic control that eliminates the single greatest compliance risk I see across our portfolio: inconsistent or ad-hoc logging across teams. A unified, default-on, modular framework is exactly the architectural posture that would satisfy a notified body's expectation of traceability under Article 12(2)(a)-(c), and it positions us well for the Annex III paragraph-3 minimums once those specifics are layered into the modular API. My residual concern is that the proposal does not yet address log integrity (tamper-evidence), retention periods, or regulator-access workflows, but as a foundational infrastructure commitment it is squarely aligned with the regulation's letter and spirit.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 83\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2430 from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal: \"Unified Traceability Framework\" — a shared software library providing automatic and standardized event logging for all high-risk AI deployments via a modular API that captures timestamps and system events by default across different R&D projects.\n\nLet me assess this against Article 12 requirements:\n\n**Article 12(1):** \"High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\"\n- The proposal directly addresses this with \"automatic and standardized event logging\" and \"captures timestamps and system events by default.\" This is a strong alignment.\n\n**Article 12(2):** Logging capabilities must enable recording of events relevant for:\n(a) identifying situations that may result in risk or substantial modification\n(b) facilitating post-market monitoring\n(c) monitoring operation per Article 26(5)\n- The proposal mentions \"system events\" broadly but doesn't specifically address these three categories. However, a unified framework could be designed to capture these.\n\n**Article 12(3):** For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics), minimum requirements include:\n(a) start/end date and time of each use\n(b) reference database used for checking\n(c) input data that led to a match\n(d) identification of natural persons involved in verification\n- The proposal mentions \"timestamps\" which partially covers (a), but doesn't specifically address the biometrics-specific requirements.\n\n**Recital 71:** Mentions logs, technical documentation kept up to date throughout lifetime, general characteristics, algorithms, data, training/testing/validation processes, risk-management system documentation.\n\n**From my Compliance Officer vantage point:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly addresses the core obligation of Article 12(1) — automatic recording of events over lifetime\n- \"By default\" is a strong compliance posture (opt-in would be risky)\n- Standardization across teams reduces the risk of one team missing requirements\n- Modular API approach allows for scalability and adaptability to different system types\n- Reduces reliance on individual researchers to remember logging — this is a systemic control, which is what compliance wants\n- \"Over the lifetime\" concept is embedded in the design (timestamps by default)\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level. It says \"modular API that captures timestamps and system events by default\" but doesn't specify how it addresses Article 12(2)(a)-(c) specifically, nor the Annex III point 1(a) minimum requirements in paragraph 3.\n- A single unified library for ALL high-risk AI deployments may be overly rigid — different risk categories have different logging needs (Annex I vs Annex III systems). The modular aspect helps but isn't elaborated.\n- \"Minimizing engineering burden on individual researchers\" is a good operational goal but from compliance perspective, the concern is whether the framework actually captures all required data points for each category of high-risk system.\n- No mention of data retention periods, log integrity (tamper-proofing), access controls, or how logs will be made available to notified bodies or regulators upon request.\n- No mention of how this integrates with post-market monitoring (Article 72) or the risk management documentation requirements from Recital 71.\n\nHowever, as a proposal to commit resources to pursue — it's clearly aligned with the core regulatory obligation. A unified logging framework is essentially what Article 12 demands in practice: automatic, lifetime-spanning, traceable event recording. The fact that it's \"by default\" and \"standardized\" addresses the biggest compliance risk (inconsistent implementation across teams).\n\nFrom a market reputation standpoint, having a unified compliance infrastructure demonstrates organizational maturity to regulators, customers, and partners. It shows systemic commitment rather than ad-hoc responses.\n\nFor institutional survival: non-compliance with Article 12 could result in penalties under the EU AI Act (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices, up to €15M or 3% for other violations). Having a unified framework reduces the probability of gaps.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a foundational compliance infrastructure piece that directly maps to Article 12(1) and supports 12(2). The fact that it's not fully specified at the Annex III level is normal for an initial proposal — the implementation can address those specifics. The direction is correct, the approach (unified, automatic, default-on, modular) is sound from a compliance architecture perspective.\n\nPromise score: This is a strong alignment with core regulatory requirements. I'd say around 82-85. It's not a perfect proposal (lacks specificity on Annex III requirements, data retention, log integrity), but it addresses the fundamental obligation in a way that, if properly implemented, would be a major compliance asset. The \"modular\" aspect gives flexibility to address different system categories. I'll go with 83.\n\nDecision: Approve.\n",
