nical artifact. It's a sign-off gate, not an engineering deliverable.\n- The proposal says \"project leads must verify that logging capabilities align with the system's intended purpose\" - but it doesn't specify what the actual logging implementation looks like. It's checking for existence rather than quality of the technical solution.\n- From a prototyping speed perspective, adding mandatory sign-off gates at each stage could slow down iteration cycles. During early prototyping, you might not yet know the full \"intended purpose\" or which events are \"relevant,\" so requiring verification against Article 12 criteria before you've even settled on your architecture could be premature and counterproductive.\n- However, it's true that catching compliance gaps early IS good engineering practice. The question is whether a formal \"audit protocol with signed documentation\" is the right mechanism versus simply incorporating logging requirements into the technical design document from day one.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a reasonable process idea but it's not technically specific. From my vantage point, what matters is: does this actually help me build better, faster prototypes? A sign-off gate adds bureaucratic friction to prototyping. The underlying principle (design for logging early) is sound engineering practice that I would naturally follow anyway as part of technical design reviews. But formalizing it as a separate \"audit protocol\" with documentation sign-offs at each stage feels like governance overhead layered on top of what should be an integrated part of the technical architecture process.\n\nThat said, in an R&D institution with multiple teams, having some checkpoint mechanism does prevent teams from going down paths where logging becomes very hard to retrofit. The \"costly retrospective engineering fixes\" argument is legitimate - if you build a system without any event capture infrastructure and then discover at deployment that Article 12 requires auto