ment, reducing rework cycles. If you discover at audit time that your system doesn't log the reference database ID, you have to go back, add it, re-test — expensive. Catching this in CI means a developer sees it on their next PR merge.\n- Positive: Standardizes what \"compliant logging\" looks like across teams, so engineers don't each interpret Art 12 differently. This reduces prototyping friction because you know the bar upfront.\n- Negative: Building the suite itself is an implementation cost. You need to build probes for different system types, maintain them as requirements evolve (the Act has implementing regulations coming), and keep them in sync with actual system behavior.\n- Negative: The \"simulated interactions\" part could become brittle. If your system architecture changes (different event buses, async vs sync logging), the test suite needs updating. This adds maintenance overhead that could slow prototyping if not managed well.\n- Positive: It's a one-time investment with recurring payoff. Every subsequent prototype benefits from having this gate already in place.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nThis is a solid, technically viable proposal. The engineering pattern (integration tests as compliance gates in CI/CD) is proven and low-risk. It directly addresses a real pain point: logging requirements are easy to miss during rapid prototyping because developers focus on model performance and features. Making it a gate means compliance becomes part of the development loop rather than an afterthought.\n\nThe main technical risk is maintaining the test suite as both the system architectures evolve AND as the regulatory interpretation/implementation evolves (companion guidelines, standards). But that's manageable.\n\nFrom my vantage point: this improves prototyping quality (fewer late-stage rework cycles) and speed (developers get immediate feedback rather than waiting for audit findings). It's technically straightforward to build incrementally — start with a basic