- Reduces manual burden on researchers, which means less likelihood of missed logs\n- A modular API could be adapted to different system types\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n- The proposal is somewhat generic. It says \"track system performance and errors\" but doesn't explicitly map to the specific logging requirements in Article 12(2)(a)-(c) or the minimum requirements in Article 12(3).\n- For biometric systems (Annex III 1(a)), it needs to capture very specific data points (reference database, input data leading to match, verification persons). The proposal doesn't explicitly address these.\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" - the proposal mentions \"over the duration of the lifetime\" implicitly through \"immutable, secure database\" but doesn't specify retention periods or lifecycle management.\n- Recital 71 also requires technical documentation to be kept up to date throughout the lifetime - the logging framework should feed into this.\n- The proposal is a \"software library integrated into all R&D pipelines\" - this is an engineering implementation detail. As compliance officer, I need to ensure it meets the regulatory requirements, not just that it's technically elegant.\n\nHowever, the core concept is sound and directly addresses the mandatory requirement of Article 12(1). A unified framework is actually a sensible institutional approach because:\n1. It ensures no project can be missed\n2. It provides consistent audit trails across the institution\n3. It reduces compliance risk from project-by-project variability\n4. The \"modular API\" design could accommodate different system types\n\nThe main gap I see is that the proposal as stated doesn't explicitly ensure it covers all of Article 12(3)'s minimum requirements for biometric systems, and doesn't clearly map to the three traceability purposes in Article 12(2). But these could be specification requirements added during implementation.\n\nAs a compliance officer, I would say this is fundamentally aligned with