portunity cost. Engineering talent in an R&D lab is often better spent on model development, experimentation, or new capabilities.\n2. Scope: \"integrated into all high-risk projects\" and \"across our portfolio\" — this could be a very large undertaking if the lab has many diverse projects. The resource commitment could be substantial upfront before any compliance benefit is realized.\n3. However, the alternative (each project building its own logging) would likely cost MORE in aggregate resources. A shared framework is the more efficient path IF it can actually accommodate diverse use cases.\n4. \"Modular API\" suggests flexibility, which is good for resource efficiency — you don't need to rebuild for each new project type.\n5. The proposal addresses a hard regulatory requirement (Article 12), so the work must be done regardless. The question is whether doing it once centrally vs. per-project is more efficient. It clearly is, from a resource perspective.\n6. Risk: if the framework is poorly designed or too rigid, it could become a bottleneck that slows down R&D projects waiting for logging integration. But \"modular\" and \"standardized library\" suggest reasonable flexibility.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- This is compliance work, not scientific ambition per se. However, without it, the lab cannot operate high-risk AI systems legally in the EU, which would block all downstream scientific and commercial work. So it's an enabler.\n- The upfront cost is real but bounded (build once). The ongoing cost is lower than per-project solutions.\n- It doesn't directly advance scientific goals, but it protects the lab's ability to continue its work legally.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- A library/API that integrates into existing codebases is a reasonable approach. It doesn't require new hardware or new environments necessarily.\n- The question is whether \"all high-risk projects\" means retrofitting many systems, which could be disruptive to ongoing research timelines.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a sound proposal from a resource-efficiency standpoint. Building one shared framework is clearly more efficient than N separate implementations. It addresses a mandatory requirement (not optional), so the work must happen. The modular design reduces future marginal costs for new projects. The main risk is engineering resource competition with scientific R&D, but that's mitigated by the \"build once, use everywhere\" nature and the fact that compliance is non-negotiable.\n\nI would lean toward approving this. It's a necessary infrastructure investment that maximizes resource efficiency (one build vs. many) and protects existing R&D operations from regulatory risk. The promise score should reflect that it's a solid, well-scoped proposal but not groundbreaking — it's \"must-do\" compliance work done efficiently. I'd put it around 72-78. It's clearly the right approach (shared framework over per-project), addresses a hard requirement, and is resource-efficient in design. The slight deductions are for the engineering opportunity cost concern and the risk of scope creep with \"all projects.\"\n\nLet me settle on 74 — strong approval from my vantage point because it's the most resource-efficient way to handle a mandatory obligation, but I'm not at 90+ because it does pull engineering away from R&D and the \"all high-risk projects\" scope could balloon.\n",
