le 12\" part is where it gets tricky. The mapping logic (which log field corresponds to which regulatory requirement) requires careful design and domain knowledge. This isn't trivially automated.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems specifically (biometric identification), the logging requirements are very specific: start/end times, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification. A generic ELK dashboard would need significant customization per system type.\n- The \"separates technical act of recording from administrative burden of reporting\" framing is a reasonable architectural principle — it suggests clean separation of concerns between the logging layer (embedded in the AI system) and the reporting/visualization layer (this tool).\n\n**Concerns:**\n- This is a visualization/reporting tool, not the core implementation of Article 12(1)-(3). It presupposes the existence of well-structured logs. If we approve this without also ensuring the logging infrastructure is in place, it's solving the wrong problem or at best a partial solution.\n- The \"directly map to specific requirements\" claim is optimistic. Regulatory requirement mapping requires semantic understanding of what each log entry means in context — not just string matching on field names.\n- From a prototyping speed perspective: ELK is fast to deploy, but the actual value-add (the regulatory mapping layer) is where the engineering effort concentrates, and it's not as \"plug-and-play\" as the proposal implies.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe technical approach (ELK for log visualization) is sound and well-understood. It's a reasonable component of a broader compliance strategy. However:\n1. It's a downstream tool that depends on upstream logging infrastructure being properly designed first.\n2. The \"direct mapping to Article 12 requirements\" is underspecified — this is where the real engineering challenge lies, not in standing up ELK.\n3. From a prototyping speed pe