ucture can be reused across projects, maximizing ROI on a single investment.\n\nArguments AGAINST / Concerns:\n- Building a centralized middleware layer and API has upfront development cost. If we only have one or two high-risk systems, is the overhead justified? However, even for a single system, Article 12 requires this, so it's not optional.\n- \"R&D teams must integrate\" — mandatory integration could slow down research cycles if the API isn't well-designed or if integration is cumbersome. But this is a design concern, not a reason to reject the proposal itself.\n- The scope is clear and bounded: logging infrastructure for Article 12 compliance. It's not a sprawling platform that would consume disproportionate resources.\n\n**Assessment:**\nFrom my perspective as Lab Director concerned with resource efficiency and effective use of infrastructure: This is a high-value, bounded investment. It's legally required (Article 12(1)), it prevents per-team duplication, it scales across projects, and it protects against the much more expensive scenario of retrofitting compliance during an audit. The upfront cost is justified by both regulatory necessity and long-term resource savings. It's essentially building a necessary utility that all high-risk projects will need, rather than letting each team reinvent the wheel.\n\nThe main risk is execution — if the API design is poor or integration overhead is too high, it could slow research. But that's an implementation detail, not a reason to reject the concept. The proposal as stated is well-scoped and addresses a genuine resource-allocation concern (consistency, reuse, avoidance of duplication).\n\nI would approve this with a high promise score. It's one of those proposals where compliance spending also generates efficiency gains — rare alignment between regulatory obligation and operational pragmatism. From my vantage point, this is close to \"must do\" territory because it's both legally required AND resource-efficient compar