ary cost of doing business under the EU AI Act. Without it, the lab can't deploy high-risk AI systems in regulated markets.\n- The question is whether building a \"universal middleware layer\" from scratch is the most resource-efficient approach vs. leveraging existing logging frameworks (ELK stack, OpenTelemetry, etc.) or commercial compliance tools.\n- An immutable database has storage and infrastructure costs that scale with usage volume over the system's lifetime. For research pipelines where you might iterate rapidly, this could be expensive.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The lab likely already has some logging, monitoring, and observability infrastructure (e.g., Prometheus, Grafana, existing telemetry). Building a parallel \"universal middleware layer\" risks duplicating or conflicting with what exists.\n- A better approach might be to extend existing observability stacks with compliance-specific fields rather than building from scratch.\n- The proposal says \"integrated into all high-risk AI pipelines\" which implies retrofitting, which is expensive and disruptive to ongoing research.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThe core idea (automated, standardized logging for compliance) is sound and necessary. However:\n1. Building a custom \"universal middleware layer from scratch\" when existing observability infrastructure likely exists is not resource-efficient.\n2. The performance claim (\"without degrading overall inference performance or latency\") is optimistic and unverified — if wrong, it creates technical debt across all pipelines.\n3. Scope creep risk: \"all high-risk AI pipelines\" is very broad for a lab that may have heterogeneous systems.\n4. It's a compliance necessity but the implementation approach (custom build vs. extending existing infrastructure) matters greatly from a resource perspective.\n\nMy decision: This is a necessary capability, but as proposed — building a new universal middleware layer from scratch with an immutable data