ents\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" is critical — does this framework ensure logs are retained for the full lifetime? The proposal doesn't mention retention periods or archival.\n- Doesn't explicitly address Recital 71's broader documentation requirements (though those may be covered by other proposals/processes)\n- The scope is \"all R&D pipelines\" but Article 12 applies specifically to high-risk systems — over-engineering could waste resources, but under-scoping would miss the point. Actually, having it in ALL pipelines is a safe default that ensures no high-risk system accidentally lacks logging.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a solid, well-aligned proposal with strong compliance value. The automation-by-default approach directly addresses the core risk of non-compliance (reliance on human diligence). Standardization ensures uniform coverage. It maps well to Art. 12(1) and partially to 12(2). The main gap is that it doesn't explicitly enumerate all the specific event categories required, but as a framework/library, it's designed to be extensible.\n\nFrom institutional survival: non-compliance with Article 12 could lead to enforcement actions, market access restrictions, or reputational damage. This proposal directly mitigates that risk by making compliance the default state rather than an opt-in task for researchers.\n\nFrom regulatory compliance: Strong alignment with Art. 12(1), reasonable foundation for 12(2). Would need to ensure the specific event categories are configured per system type, but the architecture supports this.\n\nFrom market reputation: Demonstrating a systematic, automated approach to record-keeping signals maturity and commitment to EU regulators and business partners.\n\nI'd approve this. It's a foundational compliance infrastructure piece that directly addresses the most critical operational requirement of Article 12. The promise score should be high but not perfect — it's not a complete solution (doesn't cover all specific requir