n processes directly to the EU AI Act requirements\" — this is essentially creating a traceability matrix between our internal artifacts and regulatory requirements. This is genuinely useful and could prevent costly rework during audits. The upfront cost of building the mapping is real but bounded; the ongoing maintenance cost depends on how well-automated it is.\n\n2. **Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints**: A pure compliance system that doesn't advance science has a harder claim on limited resources. However, this isn't purely a tax — good documentation practices (version control, clear provenance tracking for training data) actually improve reproducibility and collaboration within the lab. The \"automatic trigger update alerts\" feature could also help scientists know when their work needs updating, which is scientifically useful beyond compliance. But if the primary driver is regulatory checkbox-filling with no scientific co-benefit, it competes directly with research programs for funding.\n\nThe proposal frames it as protecting \"market reputation\" by demonstrating transparency to \"external auditors and stakeholders.\" From a resource perspective, this is a defensive investment — it avoids potential fines, market access loss, or reputational damage. The downside of NOT doing this (for high-risk AI systems) could be very expensive (fines up to 7% of global turnover under the Act). So there's a strong cost-avoidance argument.\n\n3. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: This is where I need to be most critical. The proposal says \"deploy version-controlled documentation tools.\" If we already have:\n- Git repositories for model code\n- Data lineage tracking in our ML pipeline (e.g., MLflow, DVC)\n- Experiment tracking (Weights & Biases, Neptune, or internal tools)\n- Document management systems\n\n...then the incremental cost of ADDING regulatory mapping on top is modest. The proposal seems to assume we need to \"deploy\" new 