eal efficiency problem (compliance documentation drift), leverages automation to reduce recurring manual effort, and the risk of non-compliance is severe enough that this isn't optional for high-risk systems. But it's not a 90+ because the proposal as stated could lead to over-engineering or duplication of existing infrastructure, and the \"market reputation\" framing slightly mischaracterizes what's actually needed (legal compliance, not marketing). The promise from my resource-allocation perspective: if done right by extending existing tools, maybe 62-68. If it means buying a new platform, maybe 45-50. I'll settle around 63.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Article 12 is a MANDATE for high-risk AI systems. We don't have the option to not comply. The question is whether this specific proposal is the efficient way to comply or if it's over-engineering the response. Given that we already presumably document our R&D processes (we're an R&D institution), the incremental step of mapping those existing records to Act requirements and adding automated alerts on model changes is relatively low-cost. I'll go with 65.\n",
