ntation (technical documentation) but Article 12 specifically requires \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" - this is slightly different from documenting system characteristics. However, the proposal mentions integrating logs, so it's partially addressing the logging requirement too.\n3. The scope seems reasonable - it's not proposing to build an entire compliance platform from scratch, just a pipeline connecting existing systems to a documentation output. This is proportionate.\n\nResource allocation assessment:\n- Uses existing infrastructure (MLflow, Git) ✓\n- Reduces recurring human cost of compliance paperwork ✓\n- Proportionate scope - not over-engineered ✓\n- Directly supports regulatory requirement for up-to-date technical documentation ✓\n- The investment is in connecting existing dots rather than building new systems ✓\n\nFrom my vantage point, this is a strong proposal. It's efficient use of what we already have, it reduces recurring administrative overhead that competes with scientific work, and it directly addresses a core regulatory obligation. The \"living document\" approach means the marginal cost of compliance documentation decreases over time rather than scaling linearly with number of systems.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score: It's very well-aligned with my mandate. It uses existing infrastructure efficiently, reduces ongoing resource drain on researchers, and addresses a concrete regulatory requirement without overreaching. I'd give it a high score - maybe 82-85. It's not perfect (the template engine mapping to EU requirements has some complexity that could underdeliver), but the core concept is sound and efficient.\n\nLet me settle on 83.\n",
