ned).\n- This is a significant intellectual concern: if you apply differential privacy to your traceability logs, you've introduced uncertainty into exactly the records that are supposed to provide deterministic audit trails. The EU AI Act requires that logs enable identifying situations presenting risk — if your logs are differentially private sketches, you may have lost the very information needed for compliance verification.\n- \"Compressed event streams\" is vague and doesn't clearly articulate a novel scientific contribution. Lossless compression of structured events is well-understood; lossy compression would undermine traceability requirements.\n\nFrom broader scientific impact:\n- If this work produces usable results, they'd be incremental improvements in log storage efficiency for AI systems — useful but not transformative.\n- The fundamental tension described (real-time performance vs. record-keeping) is more of an engineering tradeoff than a deep scientific problem. Modern hardware and storage make most logging overhead negligible relative to the AI inference itself.\n- The proposal doesn't clearly identify what would be novel or publishable at a high level. It reads more like an applied engineering project dressed up with \"differentially private\" language.\n\nMy assessment: This proposal has some surface-level appeal but contains a fundamental conceptual confusion (applying DP to compliance logs undermines their purpose), the intellectual contribution is modest and incremental, and it doesn't clearly push the knowledge frontier in any meaningful way. The \"fundamental tension\" framing oversells what is essentially an engineering optimization problem. From my vantage point as a researcher focused on scientific merit and the knowledge frontier, this doesn't clear the bar for approval. It's not bad work per se, but it's not research that would meaningfully advance our understanding or produce contributions of lasting intellectual value.\n\nI'll reject it