om simple thresholding to sophisticated temporal graph analysis. Without more specificity, it's hard to gauge whether this would produce truly novel intellectual contributions or just incremental engineering.\n- There's a question of data access: will the lab actually have access to sufficient high-risk AI deployment logs? If this is an R&D institution developing its own systems, they'd have their own logs, but the scale might be limited compared to what large deployers would generate.\n- \"Forensic analysis\" could also mean this is somewhat reactive (post-hoc) rather than truly predictive, which limits its scientific novelty in the context of model monitoring literature that already exists (e.g., drift detection papers).\n- However, the specific angle of using *regulatory-mandated* structured logs as a standardized data substrate for cross-system comparison is genuinely underexplored.\n\nFrom my vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact):\n- The intellectual merit is moderate-to-good. It sits at an interesting intersection of regulatory infrastructure and AI safety science. The key question is whether the methods developed would be novel or merely applied existing drift-detection techniques to a new data source.\n- Broader scientific impact: potentially significant if it creates standardized methodologies for analyzing production AI behavior at scale, which is currently a gap in the literature.\n- Knowledge frontier: yes, it pushes into territory where we don't yet have good methods because this kind of structured temporal logging data didn't previously exist at scale.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar as a research proposal with moderate-to-high promise. It's not a groundbreaking idea per se (drift detection is well-studied), but the specific framing around regulatory logs and cross-system comparability gives it a novel angle. The intellectual merit is real but somewhat dependent on execution depth. I'd approve it but wouldn't g