        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to screen proposal P2443 from the vantage point of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution whose domain of concern is **technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping**.\n\nLet me analyze the proposal:\n\n**P2443: \"Traceability Validation Sandbox\"**\n- A dedicated infrastructure environment for simulating and stress-testing traceability under various failure modes.\n- Allocates a segment of existing compute cluster to run shadow versions of AI systems.\n- Purpose: verify that logs capture risk-triggering events.\n- Framed as proactive mitigation of financial/legal risks from post-market monitoring failures.\n\nNow let me assess this from my vantage point: **technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping**.\n\n**Technical Viability Assessment:**\n- Running \"shadow versions\" of AI systems to stress-test logging is technically feasible but raises several concerns:\n  - What does it mean to run a \"shadow version\"? For ML systems, you need the same model weights, data pipelines, and inference environments. This isn't trivially just \"allocate compute.\" You need to replicate the full deployment stack or at least the logging-relevant portions.\n  - \"Simulating various failure modes\" is vague. What failure modes? Network partitions? Data corruption? Model drift? Hardware failures? The proposal doesn't specify, which makes it hard to assess technical feasibility of the specific approach.\n  - However, from a prototyping standpoint, this is essentially an integration testing / chaos engineering approach applied to logging infrastructure. That's a well-understood pattern (Chaos Monkey, Gremlin, etc.). The core idea — test your logging pipeline under failure conditions before production — is technically sound.\n  - The key question: does this actually validate that logs will capture risk-triggering events? If you're running shadow versions, the inputs might not be representative of real-world