e logging triggers\" part is the most interesting: what's the optimal policy for deciding when to log? That's a non-trivial decision theory / information theory problem in the context of AI systems.\n\n**Broader scientific impact:** Provenance tracking has applications well beyond AI regulation — data lineage in scientific computing, audit trails in finance, reproducibility in ML research, etc. If the methods developed here are generalizable, they could have broader impact. However, the proposal as stated is quite narrow (\"one small team,\" \"benchmark against standard tools\") and doesn't signal a path to significant broader impact.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- The framing \"turns a regulatory burden into a scientific contribution\" is somewhat disingenuous. It's primarily an engineering optimization problem motivated by compliance, with some research elements attached. The intellectual merit is moderate at best.\n- \"One small team\" doing \"experiments\" and \"benchmarking\" sounds more like applied R&D than frontier research.\n- However, the selective logging trigger design IS a genuinely interesting research question. If the team approaches it from an information-theoretic or optimal-stopping perspective, there could be real scientific contribution.\n- The proposal is somewhat vague on what makes this scientifically novel versus just \"make logging faster/cheaper.\"\n\n**Overall assessment:** This has moderate intellectual merit as framed. The core research question (optimal selective logging for AI systems) is legitimate but the proposal doesn't clearly articulate why this pushes a knowledge frontier beyond incremental improvement. It's more applied engineering with a research veneer. From my vantage point, I'd find it somewhat promising but not compelling enough to commit scarce implementation resources when there might be higher-impact research directions. The \"selective logging triggers\" component could be elevated to genuine research if pro