gulatory compliance (ensures Article 12 is met consistently), and market reputation (demonstrates mature governance).\n\nThe proposal is well-aligned with what a compliance officer would want to see. It's a foundational infrastructure investment that enables ongoing compliance rather than a one-off fix. The resource efficiency argument also matters — if you're an R&D institution, you can't afford every team to build their own logging from scratch.\n\n**Decision:** Approve. This is squarely within my domain and addresses core regulatory obligations in the most efficient way possible.\n\n**Promise score:** I'd rate this very high. It's a direct implementation of Article 12 requirements at the institutional level, reduces compliance risk across all projects, and makes audit readiness significantly easier. The main caveat is that it needs to be properly scoped to handle the specific minimums in Article 12(3) for Annex III systems, but as a framework concept, it's excellent. I'd say around 90-92. It's not a 100 because the proposal is somewhat high-level and doesn't address edge cases (e.g., what happens if the logging system itself needs to be logged? Data sovereignty for log storage? Retention periods?). But those are implementation details, not conceptual flaws.\n\nI'll go with 92.\n",
