ing for the next quarterly audit could delay necessary engineering work. From a development standpoint, I'd want this to be more event-driven or at least have a fast-path for reclassification.\n- The \"mandatory compliance map\" is useful but adds a gate before prototyping can start. For experimental work, this could slow down iteration cycles.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a reasonable governance framework that has indirect benefits for development teams (clarity on what to build), but it's fundamentally an administrative process rather than a technical solution. From my vantage point:\n- It doesn't directly address the engineering of logging systems (the \"how\" of Article 12 compliance).\n- It does reduce uncertainty for development teams, which helps planning and reduces rework.\n- The quarterly cadence is potentially too slow for agile development cycles.\n- It's a necessary enabler but not sufficient — I still need the actual technical solutions for implementing logs.\n\nI think this clears my bar at a moderate level. It's useful as a classification mechanism that prevents wasted engineering effort, but it's not itself a technically viable solution to Article 12 compliance — it's a process wrapper around the real engineering work. The quarterly board adds overhead that could slow prototyping rather than speed it up for experimental projects.\n\nI'll approve it at a moderate promise score because it does reduce technical uncertainty and prevent misallocation of engineering resources, which are in my domain. But I won't give it a high score because:\n1. It's administrative, not technical — doesn't solve the \"how do we build the logging\" problem.\n2. Quarterly cadence is too slow for development iteration cycles.\n3. The mandatory compliance map gate could delay prototyping start.\n\nI'd say around 55-60. It helps but isn't a core technical solution, and the process overhead could actually impede speed of prototyping for experimental work.\n\nLet me settl