icant domain expertise to design meaningful test cases. The proposal is somewhat generic; it doesn't specify how comprehensive the testing would be relative to the full scope of Article 12(3) (which has very specific minimum logging requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems).\n- There's a risk of creating a false sense of compliance: passing an automated test suite doesn't guarantee real-world logging completeness. However, this is inherent to any testing approach and the proposal positions it as a \"validation framework\" rather than a certification.\n- Resource commitment: building and maintaining such a suite requires ongoing investment. But from a compliance perspective, this is exactly where resources should go - it's a core compliance infrastructure investment.\n- The scope seems reasonable - it's specifically about Article 12 logging capabilities, not trying to be a full AI Act compliance suite.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It directly addresses a specific regulatory requirement (Article 12), reduces enforcement risk, supports market reputation by demonstrating systematic compliance controls, and prevents the catastrophic scenario of deploying a non-compliant system that then gets flagged in a post-market review or incident investigation. The \"pre-deployment\" gate is exactly what a compliance function needs - it creates a checkpoint where we can say \"no, this system does not ship until its logging meets Article 12.\"\n\nThis clears my bar decisively. I'd give it a high promise score because:\n- It's directly relevant to my core domain (regulatory compliance)\n- It's actionable and scoped appropriately\n- It creates institutional protection against the most common regulatory failure mode (deploying without adequate logging)\n- It supports all three of my concerns: survival (avoids fines/enforcement), compliance (verifies Article 12 conformance), and reputation (demonstrates systematic quality controls to market