on or our R&D mission.\n- This is the kind of proposal where the \"science\" is really standards-setting/governance politics, not advancing AI capabilities. It's ambitious in a non-scientific way — it's about institutional positioning.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Our existing infrastructure (compute clusters, data pipelines, model development environments) is oriented toward building and testing AI systems. Developing a public schema is a documentation/governance activity that doesn't leverage our technical infrastructure well. It requires policy expertise, legal knowledge of the Act's specifics, and negotiation skills — not GPU hours or ML engineering talent.\n- However, one could argue that if we're already implementing Article 12 logging for our own systems, there's some natural overlap in understanding what data fields matter. But the act of standardizing and publishing that as an open format is a different activity entirely.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis proposal, while well-intentioned and potentially valuable to the broader ecosystem, misallocates my lab's scarce resources from a compliance-and-efficiency standpoint. The primary stated justification is thought-leadership positioning, which is a marketing/PR goal, not an operational one. The collaboration with other institutions introduces coordination overhead without guaranteeing timely output. Our obligation under Article 12 is to implement logging for OUR systems — we can do that using existing or emerging standards without needing to CREATE the standard ourselves.\n\nThe \"collaborate with other R&D institutions\" framing sounds like it spreads the cost, but in practice, inter-institutional standards work requires dedicated project management, legal review of IP/licensing, multiple revision cycles, and ongoing maintenance — all of which consume staff hours that could go toward our own compliance implementation or scientific projects.\n\nI'll reject this from my vantage point. It'