        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1931\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Article 12 mandates that high-risk AI systems technically enable automatic event recording and capture specific data fields (timestamps, reference databases, input data, verifier identities) to support traceability and post-market monitoring; the obligation is an engineering implementation requirement, not a research question about semantically interpreting those logs. This proposal commits scarce resources to a causal-inference publication project that satisfies no clause of Article 12, does not build the logging infrastructure regulators will audit for, and frames a compliance duty as a knowledge-contribution exercise—none of which advances institutional survival or reduces regulatory exposure in any concrete way.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 9\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P1931 from the perspective of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, specifically regarding Article 12 (Record-keeping).\n\nMy domain of concern: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Research into Semantic Traceability and Interpretability\" - a scientific project to evolve raw system logs into interpretable narratives of AI decision-making processes using causal inference and latent space analysis, with publication in peer-reviewed journals.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance perspective:\n\n**Relevance to Article 12:**\n- Article 12(1) requires high-risk AI systems to \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" This is a technical engineering requirement, not a research question.\n- Article 12(2) specifies what logging capabilities must enable: identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operations. These are functional requirements about what the logs capture.\n- Article 12(3) gives minimum logging requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification): recording periods of use, reference databases, input data for matches, and identification of natural persons in verification.\n\nThe proposal is fundamentally a research project on making logs \"interpretable\" through causal inference and latent space analysis. This goes well beyond what Article 12 requires. The regulation demands:\n1. Automatic recording capability (engineering)\n2. Specific event types to be logged (functional specification)\n3. Minimum data fields for certain systems (data requirements)\n\nThe regulation does NOT require logs to be \"semantically interpretable\" or to produce \"narratives of AI decision-making processes.\" It requires that logs exist and capture specific information relevant to traceability, risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operational monitoring. A compliance officer's job is to ensure the system can log the required events - not to develop novel causal inference algorithms for interpreting those logs.\n\n**Compliance assessment:**\n- This proposal does NOT address any specific gap or requirement in Article 12. The article requires engineering implementation of logging capabilities, not research into making them more interpretable.\n- \"Publishing in peer-reviewed journals\" is a knowledge contribution activity, not a compliance activity. It doesn't directly advance the institution's regulatory position.\n- However, one could argue that better understanding of logs supports post-market monitoring (Article 72) and operational monitoring (Article 26(5)). But those articles have their own specific requirements, and the logging requirement in Article 12 is about CAPACITY to record, not about interpreting what was recorded.\n- The risk here: this commits scarce resources to a research project that doesn't directly satisfy any compliance obligation. The compliance need is to BUILD logging infrastructure that captures the right events - an engineering task. This proposal is a science project.\n\n**Market reputation angle:**\n- Publishing on AI transparency could enhance market reputation somewhat. But it's a stretch from \"compliance\" to \"publishing causal inference papers about log interpretation.\"\n- Regulators are looking for compliance, not research publications. A regulator auditing the institution would want to see that logs exist, capture required data, and enable traceability - not that the team published a paper on semantic interpretability of those logs.\n\n**Institutional survival angle:**\n- Committing resources to this means NOT committing them to actually building the logging infrastructure required by Article 12(1), (2), and (3). This is a resource allocation concern from my vantage point.\n- The proposal frames regulation as something to \"transform\" through research, which is a dangerous framing for compliance. Compliance requires implementation, not research into how to make things more elegant or interpretable beyond the requirement.\n\n**My decision:**\nThis proposal does not meet my bar for approval. It's a well-conceived research project that may have value in an academic context, but from a strict compliance perspective:\n- It doesn't implement any requirement of Article 12\n- It doesn't address a specific compliance gap\n- It commits resources away from the actual engineering work needed (building logging systems)\n- The \"publication\" aspect is irrelevant to regulatory compliance\n- The regulation asks for automatic recording, not semantic interpretation\n\nI would reject this. However, it's not a terrible idea - it could have marginal relevance to post-market monitoring capabilities and general transparency posture. I'd give it a low promise score, maybe 10-15, acknowledging the tangential connection but making clear it doesn't serve my compliance function.\n\nLet me settle on a score: The proposal has some very distant relationship to compliance (better understanding of system behavior could support monitoring), but fundamentally it's a research publication project masquerading as a regulatory response. From my vantage point, I'd give it maybe an 8-12. It's not zero because the concepts touch on traceability and transparency themes that overlap with Article 12's purpose (Recital 71 mentions \"comprehensible information\"), but the execution (causal inference research + journal publication) is not what a compliance officer would back. I'll go with 10.\n",
