opment metadata. However, Recital 71 does tie technical documentation to the record-keeping requirement broadly.\n- The proposal says \"scrape metadata from experiment tracking tools\" - this is a reasonable starting point but may miss operational logs (the actual Article 12(1) requirement about logging during system operation).\n- There's a risk that auto-generated documentation, while efficient, might lack the contextual judgment needed to properly document \"risks,\" \"limitations,\" and \"risk-management systems\" as required. These aren't purely mechanical derivations from code.\n- The proposal doesn't address retention requirements or accessibility for authorities (Article 12 doesn't explicitly state retention periods in this excerpt, but broader regulation likely does).\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a compliance perspective, this is a strong direction that addresses real gaps. The key value is: (1) ensuring documentation stays current with the system (addressing the \"up to date\" requirement), (2) reducing human error in transcribing technical details, and (3) creating an auditable trail from code to documentation. However, it's somewhat narrow - it addresses the documentation generation side but not the operational logging side of Article 12(1)-(3). Still, as a component of a broader compliance strategy, it's valuable.\n\nThe risk is that if we approve this, researchers might assume \"documentation is handled\" while missing that Article 12 also requires operational logging capabilities built into the system itself (the \"technically allow for automatic recording of events\" part). But that's not a reason to reject this proposal - it just means it's one piece.\n\nI think I'd approve this with moderate-to-high confidence. It directly supports compliance with the documentation aspects of Article 12 and Recital 71, reduces institutional risk from documentation gaps (which could lead to enforcement action), and improves market reputation by showing systematic com