t in Recital 71 means this isn't a one-time cost — it's a continuous maintenance obligation. An automated pipeline addresses that recurring cost more sustainably than periodic manual updates.\n- However, I need to be careful: does automating documentation creation risk producing low-quality or shallow documents? If the template engine just fills in metadata but misses critical context (e.g., why certain validation choices were made, nuanced limitations), it could create a false sense of compliance while actually requiring human review anyway.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- MLflow (or similar experiment tracking) is likely already in place or partially in place at an R&D lab. The proposal explicitly says \"integrate experiment tracking tools like MLflow\" — the word \"like\" suggests it's not yet confirmed to be there. This is a potential gap: if the infrastructure isn't mature, you'd need to build both the tracking AND the documentation layer.\n- The template engine approach is relatively standard and low-risk technically. It's not a novel research investment; it's an engineering task with well-defined scope.\n- One concern: the proposal focuses on training/validation metadata → documentation. But Recital 71 also requires documentation of \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used as well as documentation on the relevant risk-management system.\" The pipeline may not cover all required elements without significant expansion.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a solid operational proposal with clear ROI if the underlying infrastructure (experiment tracking) already exists or is close to existing. It addresses a genuine recurring cost that scales with the number of high-risk AI systems the lab develops. The main risks are: (1) initial build cost vs. savings timeline, (2) whether it actually covers all Recital 71 requirements without becoming a patchwork, (3) dependency on experiment tracking maturity.\n\nFrom my vantage po