ticated logging demonstrates due diligence. But a research project that hasn't produced an implementable solution doesn't demonstrate compliance — it delays it.\n\n8. The proposal says \"This advances the scientific understanding of AI reliability beyond simple timestamps.\" This is positioning the work as academic contribution rather than regulatory response. From my vantage point, I need to know: will this produce a compliant logging implementation by the deadline? The answer implied by \"research project to identify\" is no — it's exploratory.\n\n**My decision:** Reject. The framing as open-ended research into what constitutes a \"relevant event\" mischaracterizes the compliance obligation. Article 12 sets clear requirements; we need engineering implementation, not scientific inquiry into whether we should log events. The resources would be better directed toward building logging infrastructure that satisfies the stated requirements. The \"beyond simple timestamps\" framing also suggests potential underestimation of what the regulation already mandates at minimum.\n\n**Promise score:** From a pure compliance standpoint, this is low. There's perhaps a small sliver of legitimate value in understanding which signals correlate with risk for designing logging architectures for non-Annex III systems, but the research framing and open-ended nature make it poorly suited to meeting a hard regulatory deadline. I'd give it maybe an 8-12. It's not zero because the underlying question (what events are relevant) does need some engineering judgment for systems beyond Annex III 1(a), but as framed, it's the wrong type of investment from my vantage point.\n\nI'll go with 10.\n",
