ar) is commodity — no R&D risk.\n- A prototype could be built quickly: set up Elasticsearch, ingest logs via Logstash/Filebeat, build Kibana dashboards. Days to weeks for a functional MVP.\n- However, the \"compliance report generation\" part has non-trivial complexity if it needs to map to specific regulatory artifacts (e.g., proving Article 12(3)(a)-(d) requirements are met). The mapping from raw events to compliance assertions is where the real engineering effort lies.\n- The claim that it \"won't slow down core development cycle\" is reasonable — it's a parallel infrastructure project, not blocking on feature work. Though it does require coordination with the logging design phase.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal is somewhat generic. \"ELK stack or similar\" is hand-wavy for an actual implementation plan.\n- It doesn't address the schema design question: what structured fields need to be captured at ingestion time to make later querying meaningful? This is a critical prototyping decision.\n- It's essentially a reporting/presentation layer, not the core logging capability itself. It's useful but it's dependent on other work being done first (the actual automatic recording).\n- For speed of prototyping: the query interface and dashboard are quick to build, but getting the data model right for compliance-specific queries is where iteration will happen.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a technically viable, low-risk infrastructure project with well-understood solutions available. It's not an R&D bet — it's an engineering execution task. The prototype can be built quickly with commodity tools. However, its value is contingent on the upstream logging design being done properly. As a standalone proposal, it addresses the \"comprehensible information\" and \"traceability\" aspects of Recital 71 well. It's a sensible engineering response to the regulatory requirement.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar for approval. It's technically sound, uses proven