        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2324\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The proposal maps directly onto the Article 12(1) mandate for automatic, lifetime-spanning event logging and uses an immutable ledger, which is exactly what a market-surveillance authority or enforcement body would expect to see during an audit. The modular architecture allows us to layer in the system-specific minimums of Art. 12(3) (e.g., reference-database references, matched-input-data capture for Annex III 1(a) biometric systems) without re-architecting, and institutionalising this across all models gives us a defensible, uniform compliance posture rather than project-by-project patchwork. Two implementation risks to flag during scoping: 'asynchronous' telemetry introduces a non-zero window of data loss that could undermine the completeness guarantee regulators expect, and 'input hashes' alone may not satisfy Art. 12(3)(c)'s requirement to record the actual input data for which a search led to a match—so the build must capture or reliably reference full inputs, not merely their digests.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 80\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2324 from my vantage point as the Compliance Officer of an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal describes: \"Standardized Traceability Framework for High-Risk AI. A modular software architecture that automatically captures event sequences and data lineage across all lab models. We will develop an asynchronous telemetry system that records timestamps, input hashes, and operator IDs into a secure, immutable ledger. This ensures baseline compliance for every prototype while minimizing the manual documentation burden on researchers.\"\n\nLet me assess this against Article 12:\n\n**Article 12(1):** \"High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\"\n- The proposal directly addresses this with an \"asynchronous telemetry system\" that automatically captures event sequences. This is a strong match.\n\n**Article 12(2):** Logging capabilities shall enable recording of events relevant for:\n(a) identifying situations that may result in risk or substantial modification;\n(b) facilitating post-market monitoring (Art. 72);\n(c) monitoring operation (Art. 26(5)).\n- The proposal mentions \"event sequences and data lineage\" which could support these, but it's somewhat generic. It records timestamps, input hashes, operator IDs — this covers some aspects but doesn't explicitly address the risk identification or post-market monitoring dimensions in a detailed way.\n\n**Article 12(3):** For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics), minimum requirements include:\n(a) recording of period of each use (start/end date and time);\n(b) reference database against which input data was checked;\n(c) input data for which search led to a match;\n(d) identification of natural persons involved in verification (Art. 14(5)).\n- The proposal captures timestamps (partial match to (a)), operator IDs (partial match to (d)), but does NOT mention reference databases or matched input data specifically. However, the \"modular\" and \"data lineage\" language suggests extensibility.\n\n**Recital 71:** Emphasizes comprehensible information on development and performance throughout lifetime, technical documentation kept up to date, algorithms, data, training, testing processes, risk-management system documentation.\n- The proposal focuses on runtime event logging but doesn't explicitly address the broader technical documentation requirements (algorithms, data provenance, training/testing processes). However, \"data lineage\" could be interpreted as covering some of this.\n\n**My compliance assessment:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly addresses Art. 12(1) mandate for automatic event recording over lifetime\n- \"Immutable ledger\" supports audit trail integrity — critical for regulatory inspections\n- \"Modular software architecture\" allows adaptation to different system types (Annex III categories have different minimum requirements in Art. 12(3))\n- \"Timestamps, input hashes, operator IDs\" map well to several specific logging requirements\n- Reducing manual documentation burden is good from a compliance sustainability perspective — if researchers are overburdened, they'll cut corners on records\n- \"Across all lab models\" suggests institutional-wide coverage, which is important for systematic compliance rather than ad-hoc per-project approaches\n\nWeaknesses/Gaps:\n- \"Asynchronous telemetry\" could raise questions about completeness — what if the system crashes before the asynchronous write completes? For regulatory purposes, you need guaranteed recording.\n- The proposal says \"baseline compliance for every prototype\" but Article 12 applies to high-risk AI systems specifically, not prototypes per se. However, building it into all models from the start is a good compliance-by-design approach.\n- Doesn't explicitly address the full scope of Art. 12(3) minimums (reference databases, matched input data)\n- \"Input hashes\" vs. actual \"input data\" — the regulation in Art. 12(3)(c) requires recording the actual input data for matches, not just a hash. A hash is verifiable but doesn't provide the same evidentiary value unless you can reconstruct or reference the original.\n- Doesn't explicitly mention retention periods or how logs tie into post-market monitoring workflows (Art. 72)\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point:\n- This is a solid foundational infrastructure proposal that directly addresses the core mandate of Article 12(1)\n- The \"immutable ledger\" aspect is particularly valuable for regulatory audits and enforcement scenarios — if a market surveillance authority requests records, you need tamper-evident logs\n- The modular design allows us to add system-specific logging (e.g., for biometric systems under Annex III 1(a)) without rebuilding\n- It's an institutional-level commitment rather than project-by-project patching, which reduces compliance risk\n\nHowever, there are gaps:\n- Asynchronous recording introduces a small window of vulnerability for data loss\n- Input hashes alone may not satisfy the \"input data\" requirement in Art. 12(3)(c) — need to verify whether full data capture is included or just hashing\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly address log retention, access controls for authorities, or integration with post-market monitoring processes\n\nOverall: This clears my bar as a compliance officer. It addresses the core regulatory requirement (automatic logging over lifetime), uses an immutable store (critical for audit), and takes a systematic institutional approach. The gaps are implementation details that can be addressed in scoping without changing the fundamental architecture. I would approve this but note that during implementation we'd need to ensure: synchronous or near-synchronous writes for critical events, full data capture (not just hashes) where Art. 12(3) requires it, and explicit mapping of log fields to each sub-paragraph of Article 12.\n\nPromise score: This is a strong proposal from a compliance perspective. It hits the core requirement directly. I'd say around 78-82. It's not perfect (the asynchronous concern, the hash vs. full data question), but it's clearly the right direction and addresses the main regulatory mandate. I'll go with 80.\n",
