to €35M or 7% of global turnover). This protocol is a direct mitigation.\n- The version control integration is smart — it creates an immutable audit trail linking each code state to its corresponding compliance documentation.\n- For market reputation: being able to demonstrate a robust, gate-controlled compliance process differentiates us in the EU market and builds trust with clients and regulators.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- The proposal says \"before any model enters the testing phase\" — Article 12 is about lifetime logging, not just pre-testing documentation. The protocol needs to extend through post-market monitoring (Article 72) and ongoing operation. As written, it seems focused on the development gate rather than the full lifecycle. However, it does say \"integrated directly into the existing R&D project lifecycle\" which could encompass more phases.\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level — it's a protocol/framework rather than specifying exactly what logs are captured, how Article 12(3) minimum requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification) would be met, etc. But as a screening exercise, the framework itself is sound.\n- \"Compliance officers will sign off on each gate\" — this could create bottleneck concerns, but from a compliance standpoint, having explicit human accountability at gates is exactly what regulators expect under the risk management system requirements (Article 9).\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention automatic recording of events (logs) as required by Article 12(1). It focuses more on documentation. However, linking code commits to documentation updates implies that logging capabilities would be documented and verified at each gate, which is a reasonable interpretation.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a strong, well-aligned proposal from a compliance perspective. It creates structural accountability, ensures traceability (a core Article 12 requirement), integrates compliance into the development process rather than treating it as an afterthought, and directly serves institutional survival by preventing non-compliant releases. The gated review with compliance sign-off is exactly what we'd want to show regulators. It addresses the \"up to date\" documentation requirement from Recital 71 through version control linkage.\n\nThe main gap is that it's more focused on the development/documentation lifecycle than explicitly addressing the automatic event logging (the technical capability of Article 12(1)). But as a protocol, it would naturally require verification that logging capabilities exist and function at each gate before progression. I think this clears my bar.\n\nDecision: Approve. It directly serves regulatory compliance, institutional survival, and market reputation.\n\nPromise score: This is very well-aligned with my mandate. The integration into version control is particularly strong for audit trail purposes. The gated review creates clear accountability. I'd say around 82-85. It's not a 95+ because it doesn't explicitly address the technical automatic logging requirement (Article 12(1)) in its description — it's more about documentation milestones than ensuring the system technically implements automatic event recording. But as a compliance governance framework, it's strong. I'll go with 83.\n",
