neering resources from scientific work. If properly scoped as integration of existing tools rather than a greenfield build, this would be efficient. But as written, it's somewhat vague on whether we're leveraging existing infrastructure or starting from scratch.\n\nI think I'd lean toward approve, because the fundamental need is real and regulationally mandated, but with moderate concern about scope creep in implementation. The promise score should reflect that the concept is right but the execution framing could waste resources if not carefully scoped to use existing infrastructure.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, with a promise around 68-72. The concept directly addresses Article 12 requirements and would be genuinely useful for audit readiness. But the \"integrated platform\" language suggests potential over-investment when existing tooling (Git, MLflow/W&B, CI/CD pipelines) could be wired together more efficiently. From a pure resource-efficiency standpoint, I'd want to see this scoped as integration work on existing assets rather than new platform development.\n\nI'll go with approve at 70.\n",
