he automatic recording of events\"\n- A formalized administrative process adds governance structure - who is authorized to verify, what constitutes verification, etc. This supports audit trails and regulatory inspections\n- Integration into existing dashboards (model evaluation) means it's embedded in workflow rather than being a separate step that might be skipped - this is good for actual compliance practice\n- The proposal specifically targets Annex III point 1(a) systems, which is the biometric identification/categorization category. This shows appropriate scoping.\n\nPotential concerns:\n- It only addresses Art 12(3)(d), not the full scope of Art 12. But that's fine - it's a focused proposal for one specific requirement.\n- \"Digital signature system\" - need to ensure this meets any eIDAS requirements or other regulatory standards for electronic signatures if applicable, but as a logging mechanism it should be sufficient.\n- The phrase \"without disrupting the scientific workflow\" is aspirational but from a compliance standpoint, ensuring the process is actually followed (not just technically available) is what matters. Integration into dashboards helps with this.\n\nFrom my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):\n- This is a targeted, well-scoped proposal that addresses a specific legal obligation\n- The automation reduces compliance risk (human error in manual logging)\n- It's practical and implementable\n- It demonstrates to regulators and market participants that the institution takes traceability seriously\n- The formalization of an administrative process adds governance rigor\n\nThis is a solid, well-targeted compliance proposal. I would approve it. The promise score should reflect how directly it maps to a legal requirement and how effectively it implements it. I'd say around 82-85. It's clearly good but not transformative - it addresses one specific sub-requirement (though an important one). It's well-designe