cal viability standpoint, it's still well within normal engineering scope.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is actually very prototype-friendly. The core pattern (wrap model calls → emit structured log events → write to append-only store) can be prototyped in days to a couple of weeks. The API wrapper layer is the main new code; the backend logging infrastructure likely already exists or can be assembled from standard components.\n- The \"standardized\" aspect means one implementation serves multiple models, which accelerates coverage across deployments.\n- However, the word \"centralized\" and \"all high-risk AI deployments\" suggests a platform-level effort rather than a single prototype. The initial prototype (one model wrapped, logging to a local store) is fast; full cross-deployment rollout takes longer but is incremental.\n- Quality: immutable append-only logs are simple to get right. The schema design for what events to capture is the main intellectual work, and Article 12 gives a clear spec for that.\n\n**Potential concerns from my vantage:**\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level/visionary (\"This is a centralized technical system designed to...\"). It reads more like an architecture statement than a concrete implementation plan. But the core technical approach (API wrappers → telemetry → immutable store) is sound and implementable.\n- \"Without manual intervention\" — this is correct and necessary per Article 12(1). The technical mechanism for this (middleware/hooks on inference calls) is standard.\n- One gap: the proposal mentions \"session durations and input matches\" but doesn't explicitly address all of 12(3)(a)-(d), particularly the identification of natural persons in verification (12(3)(d)). That's more of a workflow/identity integration concern than pure logging tech, but it could add complexity.\n- The proposal is broad (\"all high-risk AI deployments\") which means the implementation scope is large. But the prototype can start narrow