c project\" / \"research into...\" It's exploratory. The question is whether it's worth committing implementation resources to.\n\n   From compliance standpoint:\n   - I need to ensure any logging system we deploy meets Article 12 exactly.\n   - Research into efficient logging architectures IS useful for ensuring we CAN meet the requirement without performance degradation that makes the system impractical.\n   - BUT \"sampling\" of mandatory per-event logs is a compliance no-go. The proposal doesn't clearly separate what's acceptable (compression, efficient storage, smart indexing) from what's not (dropping/sampling mandatory event records).\n\n   I think this gets a \"reject\" or very low promise because:\n   - The framing around \"sampling\" introduces compliance risk that isn't adequately bounded in the proposal text.\n   - As stated, it could lead to implementation of non-compliant logging (sampled logs = missing mandatory events = violation).\n   - The legitimate efficiency research is better handled as an engineering task within existing system development, not as a separate \"scientific project\" with publication goals.\n   - From my compliance perspective, the risk of this research leading to a compliance gap outweighs the marginal benefit of slightly more efficient logging infrastructure.\n\n   Actually, let me reconsider one more time. The proposal is about RESEARCH. It's testing techniques. It says \"maintain regulatory utility.\" In a research context, you test things and then decide what to implement based on whether it meets requirements. That's actually reasonable. But...\n\n   My concern: if the lab commits resources to this as a standalone project with publication incentives, there's a risk that findings get pushed toward implementation before compliance has fully validated them. The \"publication opportunity\" framing introduces an incentive structure (publish results) that may conflict with careful compliance validation.\n\n   I'll go with: reject