        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2272\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal directly serves my core mandate: by making compliance engineering a visible, pre-funded line item in every grant application rather than an unfunded expectation absorbed by researchers, it protects scientific experimentation budgets from being silently eroded by regulatory work. It is also remarkably low-cost to implement—it requires only a change to the internal grant template and a finance-committee standing instruction, leveraging existing governance infrastructure without new tooling or headcount. The main weakness is that a single fixed percentage applied to 'every project' is blunt: projects touching Annex III point 1(a) biometric systems carry materially heavier Article 12(3) logging obligations than others, and any non-high-risk exploratory work would be over-allocated; however, as a first-order allocation framework it can be tiered in iteration two without restructuring the mechanism.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 75\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to evaluate proposal P2272 from the vantage point of a Lab Director at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal carefully:\n\n**Proposal P2272: Compliance-Aware Resource Budgeting**\n- A new funding allocation model that earmarks a fixed percentage of every project's budget for regulatory engineering tasks.\n- Finance committee integrates mandatory compliance overhead into all internal grant applications.\n- Purpose: fund technical implementation of Article 12 requirements.\n- Rationale stated: prevents compliance costs from cannibalizing funds intended for core scientific experimentation and high-level ambition.\n\n**My assessment from the Lab Director vantage point (resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, effective use of existing R&D infrastructure):**\n\nPros:\n- This directly addresses a real resource-allocation problem. Without explicit budgeting for compliance work, it gets squeezed out of project budgets or done as an afterthought by overworked staff. Making it visible and funded is good practice.\n- It protects scientific ambition by explicitly separating \"compliance engineering\" from \"core research,\" so researchers aren't forced to choose between running experiments and writing log schemas.\n- A fixed percentage model is administratively simple — the finance committee can implement it without case-by-case negotiation, reducing overhead in the allocation process itself.\n- It's a governance/financial mechanism rather than a technical one, meaning it doesn't require new R&D infrastructure or new tooling to implement. It leverages existing grant application processes and finance committees. Low incremental cost to set up.\n\nCons / Concerns:\n- A \"fixed percentage\" is blunt. Some high-risk AI systems (e.g., biometric identification under Annex III point 1(a)) have much heavier Article 12(3) logging requirements than others. A one-size-falls-all percentage may overfund low-complexity projects and underfund complex ones. However, this is a first-order approximation that can be refined later.\n- \"Regulatory engineering tasks\" for Article 12 specifically — how much of the budget does this actually consume? For most labs, implementing automatic logging (Article 12(1)), event recording capabilities (12(2)), and possibly Annex III-specific fields (12(3)) is a bounded software engineering task. A fixed percentage might be overkill if it's say 10-15%, or too low if it's 2%. The proposal doesn't specify the percentage, which is a gap but not a fatal one — the finance committee would calibrate.\n- Risk of creating a \"compliance silo\" where engineering work for Article 12 becomes disconnected from the actual system architecture. If logging is treated as a separate funded line item rather than integrated into the development lifecycle, you might get bolted-on solutions that are harder to maintain. However, this is more of an implementation detail than a flaw in the budgeting mechanism itself.\n- \"Every project's budget\" — does the lab have non-high-risk AI projects? If so, applying a mandatory compliance overhead to ALL projects is wasteful for those not subject to Article 12. The proposal says \"every project\" which could be inefficient if some projects are purely exploratory or work on non-high-risk systems. This is a resource-allocation concern from my vantage point — you're tying up budget in compliance lines that won't be used.\n\nWait, re-reading: \"integrate a mandatory compliance overhead into all internal grant applications to fund the technical implementation of Article 12 requirements.\" If the lab only develops high-risk AI systems (which seems likely given they're subject to the EU AI Act), then \"all\" might be appropriate. But if some projects are early-stage or non-high-risk, this is a misallocation.\n\nActually, the proposal says it's for an R&D institution that \"develops AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\" This could mean all their systems are high-risk, or just that they're in scope. The phrasing \"every project\" is a bit sweeping but the intent is clear: make compliance visible and funded.\n\n**Overall judgment:**\nFrom my vantage point on resource allocation and protecting scientific ambition, this is a sound governance mechanism. It's low-cost to implement (changes grant templates and finance committee procedures), it addresses a real problem (compliance work getting starved of resources), and it protects the lab's core research mission by making compliance costs explicit rather than hidden. The main weakness is the \"fixed percentage\" and \"every project\" blanket approach, which could be refined with tiered percentages or scoping to high-risk projects only. But as a first-order allocation framework, it's practical and efficient.\n\nI'd approve this. It's a sensible, low-cost governance change that directly serves my mandate of resource-allocation efficiency and protecting scientific ambition from being eroded by unfunded compliance obligations. The promise score: I think this is solid but not transformative — it's good administrative hygiene. Maybe 72-78. Let me settle on 74. It's a clear improvement, implementable with existing infrastructure (finance committee, grant application process), low risk, but the \"fixed percentage for every project\" approach has some inefficiency that keeps it from being a 90+.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The key value proposition from my vantage point:\n1. Resource-allocation efficiency: YES — makes implicit costs explicit, prevents budget drift. Good.\n2. Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints: YES — directly protects research funds by ring-fencing compliance as separate