d engineering problem with clear inputs/outputs.\n\n**Speed of prototyping:**\n- The core concept (version-controlled docs repo) can be prototyped quickly — even a simple Git+YAML/JSON pipeline or an existing tool like Docusaurus with CI integration gets you 70% there in days.\n- The harder part is the \"direct linking\" to model versioning and training logs, and auto-populating regulatory templates. A prototype of this integration (e.g., a webhook from MLflow that updates a structured doc) could be built in a sprint or two.\n- However, \"real-time\" and \"immediate accurate evidence during external audits\" set a high bar for the full solution. The prototype can demonstrate feasibility quickly though.\n\n**Quality of prototyping:**\n- This is a well-bounded problem with clear success criteria (can you auto-generate an Annex IV-compliant technical file from system metadata?). Good for iterative development.\n- Risk: scope creep. \"Integrated platform\" could balloon. But as a prototype, focusing on one AI system end-to-end is very doable.\n\n**Alignment with Article 12 specifically:**\n- Article 12(1) requires automatic recording of events (logs). This proposal touches on that via \"training logs\" but the core focus is more on documentation (Article 4(7)/Annex IV) than on runtime event logging. Still, Recital 71 explicitly connects record-keeping with technical documentation availability, so this is in scope for Article 12's spirit.\n- The proposal addresses the \"availability of technical documentation\" requirement well — making it dynamic and linked to actual system state rather than manually maintained PDFs.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n- \"Integrated platform\" is vague on scope. Could mean building a whole new system or could mean integrating 3-4 existing tools. The prototype value depends heavily on scoping discipline.\n- The primary driver stated is \"protect our market reputation\" during audits — this is valid but it's more of a compliance/legal moti