        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2597\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core mechanism—wrapping training loops with a metadata-capture API and piping structured records into an append-only store—is technically well-trodden ground (MLflow, DVC, W&B all demonstrate feasibility) and could be prototyped in weeks rather than months. From my desk this removes the manual 'did we log which dataset snapshot and seed' overhead that currently slows iteration on high-risk prototypes, and it maps cleanly onto Recital 71's requirement to document 'algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes.' My main caution is the phrase 'systemic framework … across different research teams,' which risks inflating a focused instrumentation layer into a lab-wide infrastructure program; but as scoped for our own high-risk runs it is buildable and directly useful.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 74\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Autonomous Research Provenance Engine\" - a systemic framework that automatically captures hyperparameters, dataset versions, and execution traces for every high-risk experimental run, integrated via a specialized API into core training loops, piping metadata into a versioned immutable ledger.\n\nLet me assess this from my perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- The concept of capturing hyperparameters, dataset versions, and execution traces is well-established in ML engineering (MLflow, W&B, DVC, etc.). This is technically feasible.\n- \"Specialized API into our core training loops\" - this is straightforward engineering. Wrapping training scripts with metadata capture hooks is a known pattern.\n- \"Versioned and immutable ledger\" - technically achievable (git-based versioning, append-only databases, blockchain if truly needed but probably overkill). This is solvable.\n- The scope says \"systemic framework\" which could balloon, but the core mechanism is clear.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This directly addresses a pain point I deal with: when we prototype new models, tracking what exactly was used (which data version, which hyperparameters, which code commit) is often manual and error-prone.\n- An automated API integration into training loops would reduce overhead for prototyping teams - they don't have to manually log things.\n- However, \"systemic framework\" language suggests a broad infrastructure project that could slow down prototyping if it requires every team to adopt it before running experiments.\n- The phrase \"for every high-risk experimental run\" is scoped well enough - not all runs need this overhead.\n\n**Relevance to Article 12:**\n- Article 12(1) requires automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system. This proposal captures execution traces and metadata for training runs, which contributes to the \"development\" traceability mentioned in Recital 71 (\"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used\").\n- Article 12(2) requires logging capabilities for identifying risks, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operations. The provenance data helps with all three - if something goes wrong, you can trace back to the exact configuration.\n- For Annex III systems (Article 12(3)), this is less directly applicable since those are more about runtime logs of specific use cases (biometrics, etc.), but for high-risk systems under Article 6(2) that we develop internally, the training provenance is highly relevant.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- \"Systemic framework\" could become a massive engineering project. The risk is it becomes a 12-month infrastructure build before anyone gets value.\n- However, the core mechanism (API wrapper + immutable ledger) is very prototypeable in weeks, not months. A minimal viable version could be built quickly.\n- The \"drastically improving scientific reproducibility\" part is a bonus benefit but also scope creep risk - if we try to solve inter-team reproducibility as part of this, it becomes a different (larger) project.\n- Integration into \"core training loops\" requires coordination with the teams that own those loops. This is a practical engineering concern but manageable.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core technical mechanism is sound and well-understood. The API + ledger pattern is proven. It's technically viable to prototype quickly (a basic version in 2-4 weeks). It directly addresses the \"automatic recording\" requirement of Article 12(1) for the development/training phase. From a prototyping speed perspective, having this automated removes a manual bottleneck and makes our experiments more reproducible without adding much per-run overhead once integrated.\n\nThe main risk is scope inflation (\"systemic framework,\" \"across different research teams\"), but the core idea is solid and prototypeable. I'd approve this as technically viable with clear path to implementation, while noting that the systemic/enterprise framing needs to be scoped down for initial delivery.\n\nPromise score: This is a solid, technically feasible proposal that addresses a real requirement and a real engineering pain point. It's not revolutionary but it's practical and directly relevant. I'd put it around 72-78. The \"systemic framework\" language gives me slight pause about scope creep, and the fact that tools like MLflow/W&B already do much of this means we might be reinventing rather than adapting. But the specific integration for regulatory compliance (immutable ledger, Article 12 mapping) adds value beyond existing tools. I'll go with 74.\n",
