jects is a significant upfront engineering commitment. Different AI systems (vision, NLP, recommendation, etc.) have very different event structures. A truly unified API might be over-engineered for some use cases or under-specified for others.\n- CON: The proposal says \"our engineers will develop\" — this commits scarce implementation resources to building infrastructure BEFORE any specific product is ready. From a prototyping speed perspective, you want to get specific systems shipping first, then abstract common patterns. Building the framework upfront could delay individual projects.\n- However, the counter-argument: if multiple high-risk AI projects are in flight simultaneously (which seems likely given this is an R&D institution developing multiple AI systems), building shared infrastructure early makes sense to avoid duplicated effort and inconsistent implementations that would need rework later.\n\n**Article 12 Specifics:**\n- Article 12(1) requires automatic recording of events over the lifetime — a middleware library fits well here as it can be embedded in system code.\n- Article 12(2) requires logging for traceability, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring — a unified framework with configurable event schemas could address all three.\n- Article 12(3) has specific minimum requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics-related) — the proposal mentions \"input hashes\" which aligns with capturing input data references, though it doesn't explicitly mention the reference database or the verification personnel identification required by 12(3)(b)-(d).\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a pure technical viability standpoint, this is very feasible. It's standard middleware development. The architecture is sound.\n\nFrom speed/quality of prototyping: This is the key tension. A shared framework reduces long-term per-project overhead but requires significant upfront investment. For an R&D institution with multiple high-risk AI systems in development, 