        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the perspective of a Compliance Officer whose domain is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P1461 is about creating a \"Provenance and Traceability Scientific Initiative\" — essentially an R&D project to develop cryptographic hashing for event logs in AI systems, integrating prototypes into high-risk projects to test viability.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance vantage point:\n\n**What Article 12 actually requires:**\n- Automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system (Art. 12(1))\n- Logging capabilities for specific purposes: identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, monitoring operation (Art. 12(2))\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), minimum logging requirements are specified (Art. 12(3))\n\n**Assessment from compliance perspective:**\n\nArguments in favor:\n- The proposal does address the core subject of Article 12 — logging and traceability\n- Cryptographic hashing could strengthen tamper-proofing, which supports \"traceability\" mentioned in Recital 71\n- Integration into high-risk projects means it's not purely theoretical\n- \"Publishable scientific contribution that enhances the lab's prestige\" touches on market reputation\n\nArguments against:\n- This is framed as a *research project* to \"explore\" and \"test viability\" — it does NOT commit to implementing compliant logging. Article 12(1) says high-risk AI systems SHALL technically allow for automatic recording. This is mandatory, not optional research. A research project exploring whether cryptographic hashing might work doesn't satisfy the obligation to have logging in place NOW for deployed or soon-to-be-deployed high-risk systems.\n- The proposal \"shifts focus from passive logging to active, verifiable traceability\" — this framing dangerously implies that current/passive logging is insufficient or that the lab can defer actual compliance implementation while researchers explore novel methods. This is a compliance risk: if we're exploring whether logging will work in 2-3 years, what are we doing now for systems already subject to Article 12?\n- \"Allocate a small team of researchers\" — this is an R&D investment, not an engineering/compliance implementation commitment. Compliance needs concrete implementation timelines, responsible parties, and integration into the product development lifecycle. A research initiative with open-ended exploration doesn't meet that bar.\n- The proposal integrates prototypes \"into our most ambitious high-risk projects to test viability.\" Testing a prototype in a live high-risk project is itself a compliance risk — if the logging isn't fully operational and compliant during testing, we're already out of compliance for those systems.\n- The primary motivation stated is \"transforms a compliance burden into a publishable scientific contribution that enhances the lab's prestige\" — this is reputation-driven framing, not compliance-driven. From my vantage point, I need to see that the PRIMARY objective is meeting Article 12 obligations with clear timelines and deliverables, not publishing papers.\n- There's no mention of specific regulatory deadlines (the EU AI Act has phased implementation), no mention of which systems are affected, no mention of the minimum requirements in Art. 12(3) for Annex III(1)(a) systems specifically.\n- \"Explore cryptographic hashing\" is vague — it doesn't specify what the deliverable is, when compliance will be achieved, or how this maps to specific Article 12 requirements.\n\n**My decision:** This proposal, as framed, does not meet my compliance bar. It's a research exploration that uses compliance language but doesn't commit to actually implementing compliant logging by the required deadlines. It risks creating a gap where we're \"exploring\" solutions while our systems need to be compliant now or within defined