rvival through systemic compliance\" - aligns with my mandate\n\nConcerns/Weaknesses:\n- The proposal mentions \"timestamps, input data references, and user IDs\" but doesn't explicitly mention: the reference database against which input data has been checked (Art 12(3)(b)), or specifically matching events. These are critical for Annex III 1(a) systems.\n- It's somewhat generic - a \"common API\" is an engineering construct, but compliance requires specific fields and retention periods\n- Doesn't mention log retention period (\"over the lifetime of the system\" - what does that mean operationally?)\n- Doesn't address the broader Art 12(2) requirements about identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, or monitoring operation\n- \"User IDs\" is a simplification - Art 12(3)(d) specifically requires identification of natural persons involved in verification per Article 14(5), which may be more specific than just logging user IDs\n- No mention of log integrity/immutability (important for traceability and audit)\n- No mention of how logs feed into post-market monitoring (Art 72) or risk identification (Art 79(1))\n\nHowever, from a compliance perspective, this is a solid foundational step. It addresses the core technical requirement of Art 12(1). The fact that it's \"unified\" and integrated into CI/CD means we won't have a patchwork of logging solutions where some systems comply and others don't - that's a major compliance risk I'd worry about.\n\nThe proposal is not perfect in its specificity regarding Annex III 1(a) minimums, but as a framework/approach, it gets us most of the way there. The gaps (reference database logging, specific match events) can be addressed within the \"unified framework\" without changing the fundamental approach.\n\nAs a Compliance Officer focused on institutional survival and regulatory compliance:\n- This is clearly aligned with my mandate\n- It's proactive rather than reactive (\"prevents costly retroactive fixes\")\n-