icated staff (perhaps 2-4 engineers for build + ongoing maintenance). That's a real cost in an R&D lab where funding is constrained.\n- The proposal says it integrates with \"current compute clusters\" which is good, but the middleware itself needs hosting, maintenance, versioning updates as the AI Act evolves, etc. Ongoing operational cost is non-zero.\n- However, compared to each of N projects building their own logging (N × engineering effort), a shared service scales much better. The marginal cost per additional project drops significantly.\n\n**Resource-allocation assessment:**\nThis is a classic \"build once, use many\" infrastructure investment. In an R&D lab with multiple high-risk AI projects (or even a few), the upfront cost is amortized across all of them. The alternative — each team rolling its own logging — wastes engineering cycles on compliance plumbing rather than research. This directly serves resource-allocation efficiency and effective use of existing infrastructure (it plugs into current clusters).\n\nThe risk is scope creep: \"standardized API\" could balloon if teams request custom features. But the proposal as stated is focused: timestamps, inputs, versioning — these are exactly what Article 12(3) requires for Annex III systems. It's not overreaching.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a pragmatic, finite investment that reduces per-project compliance costs and leverages existing infrastructure. The main risk is making it \"mandatory\" without ensuring the middleware team can keep up with demand, but that's an operational management issue, not a fundamental flaw in the proposal.\n\nPromise score: I'd give this around 78-82. It's clearly aligned with my concerns about efficiency and existing infrastructure. The slight deduction is for the \"mandatory\" framing creating a potential bottleneck, and the ongoing maintenance cost that isn't fully accounted for in the description. But it's a strong proposal from an efficiency standpoint.\n\nI'll go 