ents\n2. Addresses a recurring cost (documentation maintenance over the system's lifetime) with a one-time build\n3. Frees scientific capacity for actual research rather than compliance paperwork\n4. Scales well — as we develop more systems, marginal documentation cost decreases\n5. The core technical approach (pipeline + template generator mapping existing structured data to regulatory formats) is sound and not exotic\n\nThe main risk is the semantic gap between R&D records and regulatory requirements, but that's a bounded problem solvable with careful template design and human-in-the-loop review for judgment sections. It doesn't undermine the overall value proposition.\n\nI'd rate this quite highly from my vantage point. It's efficient, leverages existing assets, reduces recurring costs, and directly serves the compliance mandate without diverting significant R&D capacity. I'll approve with a high promise score — maybe 82-85. It's not a 100 because there is real engineering work to bridge the semantic gap, and the \"automatic\" generation will need human oversight for certain narrative sections that can't be purely extracted from logs. But it clears my bar decisively.\n",
