tal to proving compliance - if regulators come knocking (under Article 78, notified bodies can request documentation), having up-to-date records is essential for institutional survival\n- Addresses \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes\" explicitly mentioned in Recital 71\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- The proposal focuses on documentation rather than the specific logging requirements of Article 12(1) (automatic recording of events/logs). It's adjacent to but not fully implementing Article 12. However, it does support the broader record-keeping framework.\n- Quarterly reviews may be insufficient depending on the pace of model development - if you're iterating weekly, quarterly documentation reviews could leave gaps. But this is a minor concern and can be addressed in implementation.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention risk-management system documentation (also required by Recital 71), though it does mention validation processes.\n- It's somewhat generic - \"integrated toolchain linking Git commits directly to model weights and dataset versions\" is a good start but the specifics of what events are logged (per Article 12(2) categories) aren't addressed here.\n\n**From my specific vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\n- This is a foundational compliance infrastructure. Without up-to-date technical documentation, we cannot demonstrate compliance to notified bodies or national competent authorities. This is a direct regulatory requirement.\n- Market reputation: Being known as an institution that maintains rigorous documentation practices builds trust with clients and regulators.\n- Institutional survival: Failure to maintain records could lead to enforcement actions, fines (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover under Article 99), and loss of ability to operate in the EU market.\n- This is a clear \"must-have\" for compliance with the Act's documentation requirements.\n\nThe proposal is solid, directly relevant, and addresses a core regulatory obligation. It's not a moonshot or an exploratory idea - it's a concrete implementation approach to a clear legal requirement. I would approve this.\n\nPromise score: This is a high-value, clearly necessary compliance measure. It's not flashy but it's essential. I'd rate it around 82-85. It's very promising from my vantage point because without this, we simply cannot comply with the Act's documentation requirements. The only reason I'm not giving it higher is that it doesn't fully cover the Article 12(3) specific logging requirements for Annex III systems, and quarterly reviews might need to be more frequent depending on development velocity. But as a foundational protocol, it's strong.\n\nI'll go with 84.\n",
