        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2206\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core mechanism—hash-based input versioning, DB-version tagging at the inference boundary, and structured timestamped logging—is a well-trodden pattern (content-addressable snapshots, OpenLineage-style event capture) that I could spin up a working prototype for an Annex III 1(a) biometric pipeline within a few days. However, the proposal's framing ('maps the flow of data from ingestion to final output,' 'centralized ledger') over-scopes relative to what Article 12(3) actually mandates (a reference-DB identifier, matched input data, start/end times, verifier ID), and a true centralized immutable ledger in the hot inference path introduces latency and SPOF concerns that would complicate integration rather than accelerate it.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 74\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2206 is an \"Automated Data Lineage Tracking System\" that:\n- Logs database versions and specific input sets for each inference\n- Maps data flow from ingestion to output\n- Uses hash-based versioning in data pipelines\n- Creates immutable links between inputs and results in a centralized ledger\n- Replaces manual documentation with automated snapshots\n- Claims to satisfy Article 12 requirements for tracking reference databases\n\nLet me assess this from my technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- Hash-based versioning is a well-understood, proven technology (git-style content addressing). This is technically straightforward.\n- Data lineage tracking is an established pattern in data engineering (e.g., Apache Atlas, OpenLineage, Marquez). The concepts are mature.\n- However, \"immutable links between inputs and results\" at the inference level introduces significant complexity. For ML systems, especially those with ensemble models or multi-stage pipelines, capturing exact input sets per inference can be computationally expensive.\n- A \"centralized ledger\" is a single point of failure and potential bottleneck in high-throughput inference scenarios. The architecture choice matters enormously here - if we're doing real-time biometric verification (Annex III 1(a)), the logging overhead needs to be minimal.\n- Hash-based versioning works well for batch data but becomes tricky with streaming inputs or when input data is very large (e.g., video streams for facial recognition). You can't hash a full video frame set efficiently without significant I/O overhead.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The core concept (hash the input, record the DB version, log timestamp) is actually quite fast to prototype. A basic MVP could be built in days: intercept inputs at the inference boundary, compute SHA-256 of the payload, look up current DB schema/version hash, write a structured log entry.\n- However, \"maps the flow of data from ingestion to final output\" is much harder to prototype quickly if it means full lineage tracking through every transformation step. That's a multi-week project with significant integration work across pipelines.\n- The proposal conflates two things: (1) simple input/output logging per inference (easy), and (2) full data lineage/provenance tracking across the entire pipeline (hard, complex). The Article 12(3) requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems are actually quite specific and narrow - they want start/end times, reference database version, input data that matched, and verifier identification. That's much simpler than full \"data lineage.\"\n- The proposal over-engineers relative to the actual regulatory requirement for biometric verification systems. Article 12(3)(b) says \"the reference database against which input data has been checked\" - this is a single DB version identifier, not a full lineage graph.\n\n**From my vantage point:**\nThe core idea is technically sound and the simple version (hash inputs, tag with DB version, log timestamps) is very quick to prototype and directly addresses Article 12(3). However, the proposal as written describes something much broader (\"maps the flow of data from ingestion to final output,\" \"centralized ledger,\" \"replaces manual documentation\") than what's actually required. The full lineage tracking scope would be a significant engineering investment with diminishing returns for compliance purposes.\n\nThe question is: does it clear my bar? From a prototyping perspective, the minimal viable version (log input hash + DB version + timestamps per inference) is extremely quick to build and technically straightforward. It's a proven pattern. The \"centralized ledger\" language worries me architecturally but could be interpreted as simply a structured log store.\n\nI think this clears my bar because:\n1. The core technical approach (hash-based versioning, automated logging at inference boundary) is well-understood and quick to implement\n2. It directly addresses the most concrete requirements in Article 12(3) for biometric systems\n3. A prototype could be running in days\n4. The technology stack is standard (no exotic dependencies)\n\nBut I'm slightly concerned about scope creep in the description (\"maps the flow of data from ingestion to final output\" suggests full lineage, which is much harder). And \"centralized ledger\" introduces a potential bottleneck/architectural concern for real-time systems.\n\nI'll approve it because the core technical approach is viable and quick to prototype, but I'd want to scope it down in practice. The promise score should reflect that it's solid but not extraordinary - it's doing something well-understood correctly. Maybe 72-78 range. It's technically sound, proven approaches, fast to prototype the minimal version. But the full description as written has architectural concerns (centralized ledger for real-time inference) and scope ambiguity.\n\nI'll go with approve, promise 74.\n",
