ion is: do we need novel cryptographic logging before we can comply? No - the regulation says \"technically allow for automatic recording of events.\" Standard logging infrastructure satisfies this.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:** My lab likely already has engineering teams building compliance-focused logging into our AI systems. This proposal would pull researchers away from those practical implementation tasks toward a theoretical exploration. The infrastructure I have is tuned for applied R&D with deliverables that map to regulatory requirements and product development cycles.\n\nHowever, I should consider: is there a case where compressed/verifiable logging actually matters at scale? If we're running very large-scale systems where storage of every log entry becomes prohibitive, then probabilistic sketching could have practical value. But the proposal frames this as \"theoretical\" and about \"advancing the knowledge frontier,\" not about solving an immediate compliance or operational problem.\n\nMy assessment: This is a scientifically interesting idea but doesn't clear my bar for committing scarce implementation resources. It's a \"nice to explore someday\" project, not something that addresses our regulatory obligations or operational efficiency in any near-term way. The regulation requires logging capability - it doesn't require novel cryptographic or compression schemes. Standard engineering solutions meet the requirement. Committing my team to a theoretical exploration when we have compliance deadlines and practical implementation needs is poor resource allocation.\n\nI'll reject this, but give it a moderate promise score because the underlying ideas (compressed verifiable logs) could become relevant if our systems scale to points where storage becomes a genuine bottleneck. Maybe around 30-40? It's not zero - the concepts are sound and could matter in 3-5 years as system complexity grows. But from my resource-allocation vantage point, it