ce logging tends to become an afterthought that gets bolted on late, causing rework and slowing delivery. A shared library with clear contracts would speed up prototyping significantly because developers know upfront what events they need to emit.\n- \"Lightweight wrapper around existing telemetry\" - this is the right call for speed. Building new infrastructure from scratch would be slow. Reusing existing telemetry means the hard part (event collection, storage, retrieval) is already done. You're just adding a semantic mapping layer.\n- The risk: if the middleware becomes a bottleneck or requires changes to existing telemetry format, it could actually SLOW prototyping initially. But \"lightweight wrapper\" suggests they've thought about this - it's an additive layer, not a rearchitecture.\n\n**Quality considerations:**\n- A standardized API means consistent logging across systems, which improves debugging and audit quality.\n- The mapping to specific Article 12 requirements provides a clear acceptance criterion for the middleware itself - you can test whether logs meet the regulatory spec.\n- One concern: \"maintaining high prototyping speed\" while adding mandatory event emissions could create friction if the API is not truly zero-config or if it introduces latency into pipelines. But this is manageable with async logging patterns.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a technically viable, well-scoped engineering solution that directly addresses a real pain point (compliance as a tax on prototyping speed). The approach of wrapping existing telemetry rather than building new infrastructure is smart and fast to implement. The main risk is the \"universal\" scope - whether one API can cleanly serve all Annex III categories without becoming either too abstract or requiring many special cases. But even if it needs per-category adapters, the core pattern (capture → map → emit structured log) is sound.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar. It's a pragmatic 