gging requirements about events relevant to risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring. It seems more focused on the technical documentation aspect than the event-logging capability itself.\n- However, Recital 71 does say \"This requires keeping records AND the availability of technical documentation\" — so documentation is one part. Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems has very specific logging requirements (period of use, reference database, input data matches, identification of natural persons in verification). This proposal doesn't address those specific event-logging capabilities.\n- The proposal says it focuses on \"the comprehensible information requirement of Recital 71\" — this is partially aligned but narrow. Article 12 has both the automatic logging requirement (para 1) AND the documentation/record-keeping aspect. The proposal seems to address the latter more than the former.\n- From a compliance perspective, having automated documentation generation is valuable because it reduces human error and ensures consistency, which is critical for regulatory inspections. If auditors come, having a system that automatically generates up-to-date documentation is far better than relying on researchers to manually maintain it.\n\n**My professional assessment:**\nAs Compliance Officer, I care about: (1) can we demonstrate compliance if an authority inspects us? (2) are we maintaining records throughout the lifetime of the system? (3) is our documentation clear and comprehensive as required?\n\nThis proposal directly addresses the practical challenge of keeping technical documentation \"up to date throughout the lifetime of the AI system\" — a requirement that, in practice, many organizations struggle with because it relies on human diligence. Automating this reduces compliance risk significantly. The integration with version control adds traceability.\n\nHowever, I note it's somewhat narrow — it addresses the documentation/record-keeping side but not the specific event-logging capabilities required by Article 12(2) and (3). Still, it's a complementary piece that fills an important gap in our compliance infrastructure. It doesn't replace the need for proper logging architecture, but it makes the \"availability of technical documentation\" requirement much more robust.\n\nFrom my vantage point: This is genuinely useful for institutional survival because if we get hit with a market surveillance action or notified body audit, being able to produce current, comprehensive, auto-generated technical documentation is far superior to scrambling through spreadsheets and Slack messages. It protects market reputation by showing regulators we have systematic compliance processes in place.\n\nI'd approve this. It's a practical, well-scoped compliance enabler. The promise score: it's solid but not the whole picture (it doesn't cover Article 12(3)'s specific logging for biometric ID systems, or the risk-event logging of 12(2)). But as a standalone piece addressing the documentation/record-keeping requirement, it's strong. I'd say around 72-78. Let me settle on 75 — it addresses an important compliance gap well, but from my perspective it's one component of a broader Article 12 compliance strategy rather than a complete solution to the article's requirements.\n",
