ing audits\" - this is slightly misleading. Cryptographic hashing doesn't make tampering \"immediately\" detectable; it makes it *detectable* if you have the original hash to compare against. You need a trusted anchor point (original hash stored elsewhere, or a public ledger). But this is an implementation detail, not a fundamental flaw in the concept.\n\n2. \"Write-once-read-many\" - this is practical and well-understood technology. No major concerns here.\n\n3. The proposal focuses on storage integrity rather than the *content* of what's logged (which Articles 12(2) and 12(3) specify). It complements but doesn't replace the need for proper logging content. However, it's a necessary component.\n\n4. Resource commitment: This is infrastructure work that has clear regulatory drivers. The cost is justified by the fact that without it, we can't demonstrate compliance with Article 12 in any meaningful way during audits or incident investigations. It's not speculative - it directly maps to regulatory requirements.\n\n5. \"Over the lifetime of the system\" - this means potentially years of storage. WORM storage handles this well and is actually more efficient than maintaining mutable databases for historical data.\n\n**Compliance assessment:**\n- This is a direct enabler for Article 12 compliance. Without integrity guarantees, our logs are vulnerable to challenge by regulators.\n- It supports our ability to demonstrate compliance during audits (Article 43, notified body involvement).\n- It protects institutional survival: if an AI system causes harm and we're investigated, being able to prove log integrity is essential.\n- Market reputation: clients and partners in regulated sectors will expect this level of assurance.\n- It's a well-understood technology pattern (WORM + hashing), so implementation risk is low.\n\n**Decision:** This clearly clears my bar. It's not speculative R&D - it's infrastructure that directly enables compliance with a specific regulatory requirement