it all project architectures well; over-unification can create bottlenecks or force awkward compromises that slow research teams.\n- The proposal frames it as \"compliance plumbing\" which is accurate but also signals this has zero scientific upside — purely defensive spending.\n- Risk of building something before fully understanding the diverse logging needs across different high-risk projects (Article 12(3) has specific minimums for biometric systems, but other categories may need different event types).\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is genuinely one of the most sensible things to do. If we have multiple teams building their own loggers, that's wasted engineering effort and inconsistent data capture (which creates compliance risk). Centralizing it IS efficient use of resources. Integrating with existing infrastructure (compute clusters, data lakes) means we're not spending on new hardware or cloud services — we're leveraging what we already pay for.\n\nThe concern about scientific ambition vs. funding is real: this is pure overhead with no research output. But the alternative — every team building its own logger — is worse from a resource perspective. The \"small task force\" framing keeps the commitment bounded.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's efficient, uses existing infrastructure, and prevents duplicated work. The promise score should be moderately high but not 100 because: (a) it's pure compliance overhead with no scientific return, (b) scope could creep, (c) \"small task force\" is underspecified in terms of duration and opportunity cost to those engineers' other projects. But the core logic is sound from my vantage point.\n\nI'd put promise around 78-82. Let me settle on 80. The decision is approve — this is efficient resource allocation and leverages existing infrastructure, which are exactly my concerns.\n",
