ototyping speed perspective, an LLM-based document synthesis tool could produce a prototype relatively quickly (weeks to a couple months) that demonstrates the concept. But going from prototype to production-quality compliance documentation generator is a much longer path.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A proof-of-concept prototype: very achievable in weeks. Take a few R&D artifacts, feed them through an LLM pipeline with appropriate prompts and structured output, generate a draft document. This is doable.\n- But the quality bar for compliance documentation is extremely high. Errors could have legal consequences. The \"human review\" step is critical but doesn't eliminate the need for the tool to be highly accurate.\n- The prototyping speed advantage here is real: you can iterate on prompt engineering, test different artifact types, and demonstrate value quickly.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n1. The proposal conflates \"research opportunity\" with a practical compliance need. From a development standpoint, the actual requirement (Article 12) is about *logging capabilities* in the system — automatic recording of events. This proposal addresses Recital 71's documentation aspect, which is related but different. The logging infrastructure (the technical requirement) and the documentation synthesis are separate engineering challenges.\n2. \"AI-driven tools that automatically synthesize\" — the word \"automatically\" sets a very high bar. In practice, this will be semi-automated with significant human curation. But for prototyping purposes, you can build toward that.\n3. The proposal is framed as turning \"a regulatory burden into a research opportunity.\" From my perspective, I need to ask: does this actually solve the technical problem we face? We need logging infrastructure (Article 12(1)) and the documentation must be \"kept up to date\" throughout the system's lifetime. A one-shot document generator doesn't address the ongoing maintenance requirement well unless it's integrated into a continuous pipeline.\n4. Technical risk: LLMs can hallucinate. For compliance documents, hallucinated claims about what a system does or how it was trained could be catastrophic. The \"human review\" mitigates this but adds a step that reduces the automation benefit.\n5. The \"small team\" allocation is reasonable for a prototype. I could envision 2-3 engineers building a working pipeline in 4-6 weeks that takes a training log + model card + code repo and produces a structured draft document mapped to Recital 71 categories.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a development/prototyping standpoint, this is technically feasible as a prototype within reasonable timelines. The core NLP/information-extraction challenge is well-studied. However:\n- It addresses the documentation aspect (Recital 71) rather than the actual logging infrastructure requirement (Article 12(1)-(3)). These are related but distinct technical challenges.\n- The path from prototype to production is longer and riskier than the proposal suggests, due to accuracy requirements for compliance documents.\n- It's a reasonable prototyping target — you can build something demonstrable quickly.\n- But it doesn't directly solve the Article 12 technical requirement (automatic event logging in the system itself).\n\nI think this is moderately promising from my vantage. It's technically feasible to prototype, the team size is reasonable, and it addresses a real pain point. However, it somewhat misses the mark on what Article 12 actually requires technically (the logging infrastructure), focusing instead on the documentation synthesis which is more of a one-time/periodic task rather than an always-on technical capability. The \"research opportunity\" framing also makes me slightly cautious — as a development engineer, I want to know: does this produce something we can ship into our systems, or is it a research project that produces papers?\n\nI'll approve it at a moderate promise score. It's