source efficiency.\n6. Standardized API means teams don't each reinvent the wheel — economies of scale across projects.\n\nConcerns/Risks:\n1. Building a \"shared software library\" still requires engineering resources up front. Who maintains it? This is an ongoing cost. But compared to N teams each building their own compliance logging, it's clearly more efficient.\n2. \"Prerequisite for compute allocation\" — this could create a bottleneck if the framework isn't robust or if integration takes too long. Could slow down research throughput temporarily. But this is a one-time transition cost.\n3. Scope: \"all high-risk AI projects\" — need to make sure this doesn't over-engineer logging for simpler systems. The Article 12 requirement has tiers (general logging vs. Annex III point 1(a) specific requirements). A one-size-fits-all API might be overkill for some projects or insufficient for others. But a well-designed framework with configurable depth addresses this.\n4. \"During training and inference\" — broad scope. Training logs can be enormous. Storage costs could add up. But again, if we're wrapping existing trackers, much of the infrastructure (storage) may already exist.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong proposal from my perspective. It leverages existing infrastructure (experiment trackers), consolidates effort into one shared component rather than N siloed efforts, embeds compliance into an existing governance gate (compute allocation), and serves dual purposes (compliance + reproducibility). The resource efficiency argument is compelling. The main cost is upfront engineering for the library and a transition period, but these are bounded and one-time relative to ongoing per-team costs avoided.\n\nI'd rate this quite highly. It's well-aligned with my domain concerns. The \"wraps existing experiment trackers\" detail shows it was designed with infrastructure reuse in mind, not greenfield build. The compute allocation gate is smart governance design. The dua